n convex --environment node\",\"timeout\":120000,\"description\":\"Run convex tests\"}},{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_01E5pQEr6ruMZ3pck4YPyVUo\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"./node_modules/.bin/vitest run convex --environment node\",\"timeout\":120000,\"description\":\"Run convex tests via direct binary\"}}],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"ddfd3615-214a-424a-b808-7cb1df5c8e9c\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4724:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":0,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"thinking_delta\",\"thinking\":\" The archiveRoutePatterns code has a void smell, and the derived view page limit could\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"cee5df8b-27bb-4f77-b29c-92f9321788ce\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4846:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01A2ei7FcVysVTvrZvp5J84Q\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I have enough context to make my canonical review. Let me synthesize:\\n\\n1. **Gate Results**: Both `green-verify` and `verify-all` passed (confirmed by reviewers 1 and 2 who actually ran the gates, and supported by the story-by-story team-impl-log which shows all 8 stories passing both gates).\\n\\n2. **The Convex authorization finding** (reviewer-2's critical block):\\n   - The browser has no direct Convex access (grep confirmed no ConvexProvider/ConvexReactClient in client code)\\n   - Convex is only accessed server-side by Fastify via `ConvexPlatformStore`\\n   - The mutations validate ownership at the server level, which is consistent with the rest of the codebase, so this isn't an exploitable security gap—it's just an asymmetric pattern in how API keys are gated that I'm downgrading to a non-blocking observation.\\n\\n3. **The production producer gap** (reviewer-1's EPIC7-B001 major block):\\n   - `script-execution.service.ts` does emit empty archive entries, but the epic requirement isn't actually about a model-driven runtime—it's about something else that I need to clarify.\\n\\n4. **The stale archive signature** (reviewer-1's EPIC7-B002 major block):\\n   - The signature only fingerprints the core identifiers and timestamp, leaving provenance fields out, but `patchArchiveEntry` invalidates all derived caches immediately, so the race condition window is actually quite tight.\\n   - Archive data itself stays consistent, and the read-time enrichment handles provenance separately, which means reviewers 3 and 4 don't see this as a blocker.\\n   - Downgrade: **non-blocking minor** (the signature gap should be hardened eventually, but it's not critical now).\\n\\nBoth gates passed verification. Neither reviewer's blocking concern holds up under scrutiny—one was based on misreading the scope (B001 is already accepted as out-of-scope), and the other assumed a threat model that doesn't fit how Convex actually runs in this deployment (server-side only, no browser clients).\\n\\nNow I'm writing out the final review JSON with the exact reviewer labels and outcomes.\\n\\nI need to compile the findings carefully:\\n\\n**Blocking findings: []** (nothing survives independent verification as a hard blocker)\\n\\n**Non-blocking findings:**\\n\\n1. Archive vocabulary gap (5 of 7 kinds missing from the default execution path)—observation with human ruling, converging evidence from reviewers 1, 3, and 4, already accepted as platform-standup state per the team log.\\n\\n2. Archive-signature gap where computeArchiveSignature skips provenance and status fields—minor issue with a quick fix, flagged by reviewer 1 only, real but creates only transient inconsistency.\\n\\n3. Asymmetric Convex apiKey gating on archiveEntries...\\n\\nNow looking at the remaining findings: the archiveTurns lack the *ForService+apiKey pattern that derivedArchiveViews uses, though this is a minor human-ruling issue since the browser has no direct Convex access and the server-side-only pattern aligns with repo conventions (reviewers 2, 3, 4 converging). The derived view offset pagination has an O(p²) complexity shape flagged by reviewers 1, 2, and 4. There's also duplication in listAllArchiveEntries and a spec mismatch where appendFinalizedEntry requires sourceObjectId when it should be optional. Finally, NullPlatformStore boots outside production without live Convex access—an observation that needs human ruling.\\n\\nThe E2E tests use InMemoryPlatformStore which bypasses real Convex round-trips, and there's a redundant hasStoredTurns check. The archiveRoutePatterns code has a void smell, and the derived view page limit could truncate for processes with 25+ turns. The default execution payload is a static template, and the test coverage for archive-router.test.ts is still being evaluated.\\n\\nNow I'm consolidating the findings across reviewers—the archive vocabulary gap aligns with the downgraded EPIC7-B001 plus feedback from reviewers 3 and 4, the asymmetric Convex apiKey gating matches reviewer 2's critical finding and subsequent reviewer notes, the signature gap corresponds to EPIC7-B002, and offset pagination ties to reviewer 2's earlier observation. I'm mapping these to canonical IDs while preserving the original reviewer attributions.\\n\\nThere are two unresolved policy questions that need human judgment: whether NullPlatformStore in non-production environments is acceptable, and whether the apiKey gate gap should extend to archiveEntries and archiveTurns given the existing repo conventions.\\n\\nGenerating JSON output... Claude reviewers (epic-reviewer-3, epic-reviewer-4) passed. Independent verification resolves all blocking findings downward. EPIC7-B001 (no first-party producers for 5 of 7 archive kinds): confirmed in code, but the epic scope doesn't require a model-driven runtime—only the infrastructure to receive and serve archive entries, which the team-impl-log records as accepted platform-standup state. EPIC7-B002 (stale cache during deferred reconciliation): confirmed that computeArchiveSignature omits provenance/status fields, but patchArchiveEntry already invalidates derived caches atomically, so the race window is narrow and effects are transient rather than corrupting data. For the public Convex archive bypass: archiveEntries and archiveTurns are registered without apiKey gates, but browser clients can't access Convex directly since there's no ConvexProvider in client code—Convex is only called server-side by ConvexPlatformStore, and the mutations validate ownership. This matches the pattern across other repo tables and represents an asymmetric hardening approach rather than an end-user-accessible breach. Both verification gates passed in independent runs with all eight stories completed.\\n\\nNow I'm reviewing the cross-story composition: all eight stories flow correctly from archive entries through finalization, read/reopen, turn derivation, structural views, provenance enrichment, and degraded-state hardening with no schema or ID drift. Cache invalidation is consistent throughout—appendArchiveEntry and patchArchiveEntry clear both archiveTurns and derivedArchiveViews, while upsertArchiveTurns clears derived caches.\\n\\nThe deferred patching approach correctly invalidates cached turns and views, though there's a narrow transient window where stale derived views could survive if provenance or status fields are patched between a rebuild and invalidation—this causes temporary inconsistency rather than permanent corruption. Turn IDs and derived view IDs are deterministically stable, keeping provenance traceable across cache rebuilds as the design requires.\\n\\nFor archive entry kinds, the first-party runtime only archives user messages and process events by default, while the other five PRD-mandated kinds are accepted by the system and tested but return empty in the integrated execution payload—this aligns with the team's current implementation state and doesn't violate the Epic 7 scope since that covers infrastructure rather than runtime content. The module structure is in place with all the archive services, routes, and contracts properly wired together.\\n\\nFastify handles finalization and access enforcement while Convex manages atomic sequencing and durable storage, with route handlers enforcing access checks before any archive reads. There's an inconsistency in API key gating where the derived archive views use hardened internal wrappers but the core archive entry and turn modules expose raw exports, matching other tables in the repo but diverging from the Story 5 hardening pattern—though the practical risk is minimal since browser clients can't access Convex directly. The archive signature computation only fingerprints the entry ID, sequence, and recorded timestamp.\\n\\nPatches to related artifact versions, source provenance, tool calls, entry status, or degradation reasons bypass the drift detector since they're invisible to the signature, leaving a narrow race condition where cache repopulation after invalidation could use stale archive state. NullPlatformStore is properly rejected in production but allowed in non-production environments where it returns synthetic responses, which is intentional and documented. The coverage assessment shows AC-1.x requirements are met through Convex's validation of all entry kinds, atomic sequence assignment, and finalization enforcement. ationKey idempotency per process.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-2.x (live/archive separation, finalization boundary): Covered. ArchiveFinalizationService is called only from completion points: accepted user responses and completed runtime objects. Raw deltas and interrupted partials never call append.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-3.x (archive read, reopen, access boundaries, degraded entry display): Covered. processAccessService.assertProcessAccess is called before every archive read. Empty and degraded states are returned through the archivePageSchema parse contract.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-4.x (turn derivation, archive-entry immutability, degraded turn handling): Covered. deriveTurnsFromArchiveEntries is deterministic over sorted entries. Degradation propagates from entries to turns without mutating archive rows.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-5.x (structural derived views without model-generated summaries): Covered. buildDerivedArchiveViews generates turn_range and chunk_candidate views with deterministic structural labels only.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-6.x (artifact and source provenance enrichment, per-entry degradation): Covered. enrichArchiveEntries batches artifact version and source provenance lookups; each entry degrades independently when its related record is unavailable.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-7.x (reload/environment-loss survival, bounded reads, derived-view failure isolation): Covered. All reads use durable Convex storage. Pagination limits are enforced. Archive reads succeed when derived-view refresh throws.\\\",\\n    \\\"Service tests for non-user archive kinds inject RuntimeArchiveEntry fixtures directly; no checked-in production execution path emits model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, or tool_result. Tests prove the infrastructure; the production producer gap is accepted scope-defer per team-impl-log.\\\",\\n    \\\"Browser e2e test validates archive behavior end-to-end.\\n\\nThe browser e2e test uses an in-memory store with pre-populated fixtures to validate routing, shell, and UI rendering against a live server, though it doesn't exercise a full round-trip with the production Convex store. The production path uses ConvexPlatformStore to call real Convex functions for archive operations and reads, with DefaultArchiveReadService, DefaultTurnDerivationService, and DefaultDerivedArchiveViewService instantiated unconditionally in the app unless overridden by test options. Test doubles like NullPlatformStore and InMemoryPlatformStore are used only in tests.\\n\\nThe default execution payload produces no canonical archive rows for message, reasoning, script, tool call, or tool result events—this is an accepted limitation documented in team logs and source comments. Archive finalization is wired up in ProcessEnvironmentService and ProcessResponseService constructors, so the finalization path runs against the real ConvexPlatformStore in production. Looking at the affected files and the second issue, I'm seeing that the archive signature computation is missing critical fields like provenance and status, which means cached signatures can become stale if those fields get patched later without invalidating the cache. I'm noticing that several Convex functions for archive operations are missing the API key validation that the derived views endpoint enforces, leaving those mutation and query endpoints exposed without proper authentication checks. Looking at the pagination implementation in the derived archive views, there's a quadratic scaling issue where each page load reads all previous rows server-side before slicing to the current page. The cursor validation also lacks range checks, though in practice the bounded number of derived views per process keeps this from being a real problem at current scale.\\n\\nMoving on to the next issue: the finalization service enforces a non-null sourceObjectId across all entry types, but the schema actually marks this field as optional depending on the entry kind. This means certain runtime entries like reasoning or script emissions could fail validation even though they shouldn't require a source object ID.\\n\\nThere's also a configuration issue where NullPlatformStore gets instantiated as a fallback when Convex isn't properly configured, which only hard-fails in production environments. This masks the missing archive backing in local and staging setups by returning synthetic responses instead of surfacing the actual configuration problem.\\n\\nI'm also noticing that the pagination logic for listing archive entries is duplicated across two different service files—the same loop appears in both the turn derivation and derived archive view services, which is a maintenance concern.\\n\\nAdditionally, there's a redundant check in the getTurns method where hasStoredTurns is being called after the stored page turns length has already been confirmed as zero. Looking at the next issue, there's a potential pagination problem where the derived archive view limit of 50 items might not be enough for processes with many turns—each turn generates multiple views, so anything over 25 turns could exceed the cap, and while pagination exists in the code, the client might not be exposing the next-page cursor to users properly.\\n\\nThen there's another observation about the execution payload generation: it's using a static template approach with hard-coded content per process type rather than being model-driven, which means while real artifacts and checkpoints are created, the synthesis content isn't actually generated by a model.\\n\\nThe e2e test for the archive flow is validating the routing and UI layer against an in-memory store with pre-populated fixtures, so it confirms the client shell works but doesn't actually verify a real Convex round-trip.  The durable storage behavior is covered separately in the Convex test suite and service tests, which is acceptable for closing out this epic. There are two unresolved items that need human judgment: whether the apiKey gate pattern from the Story 5 hardening should be applied consistently to the archiveEntries and archiveTurns functions, and whether NullPlatformStore should be available outside production.\",\"signature\":\"\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6530,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":54441,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":6530},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"uuid\":\"860c2333-6e78-4498-ad99-16590edf430e\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4964:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\"\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"appendFinalizedEntry enforces non-null sourceObjectId for all entry kinds;\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"d079fb5e-ee93-4ee3-9800-aa45df2a1f03\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4981:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\"\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N008\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"hasStoredTurns check in getTurns is redundant\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"0c4bde13-5f46-41e0-a4fe-0a6f7ae641b9\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4985:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\"\\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N009\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"void archiveRoutePatterns statement in archive.ts is a code smell\\\",\\n      \\\"\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"39630014-7b58-4900-9663-6ac74662c61e\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:4986:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:59 contains `void archiveRoutePatterns;` which evaluates and discards an object. Appears to suppress\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"a9e4e457-9438-419f-ae88-216b5700f6cb\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:5012:{\"type\":\"stream_event\",\"event\":{\"type\":\"content_block_delta\",\"index\":1,\"delta\":{\"type\":\"text_delta\",\"text\":\"ening story, CANON-N002 (archive signature gap) and CANON-N005\"}},\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"uuid\":\"883fbf88-aabb-4658-bb58-2d63b9402a5f\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:5015:{\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-sonnet-4-6\",\"id\":\"msg_01A2ei7FcVysVTvrZvp5J84Q\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I now have all the evidence needed. Let me produce the canonical review.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"pass\\\",\\n  \\\"reviewerLabels\\\": [\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-3\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-4\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"reconciliationSummary\\\": \\\"Two codex reviewers (epic-reviewer-1, epic-reviewer-2) blocked; two Claude reviewers (epic-reviewer-3, epic-reviewer-4) passed. Independent verification of the three contested findings resolves all downward. (1) EPIC7-B001 — no first-party producers for five of seven archive kinds: confirmed in code (script-execution.service.ts emits archiveEntries: []). However, Epic 7 scope covers the archive infrastructure, not a model-driven runtime. The team-impl-log records this as accepted platform-standup state and the source comment states the payload 'must not synthesize canonical archive entries'. Tests exercise all seven entry kinds via fixtures against the Convex primitive. This is a scope-conformant observation, not a blocking violation. Downgraded to CANON-N001. (2) EPIC7-B002 — stale cache during deferred reconciliation: confirmed that computeArchiveSignature fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt, omitting provenance and status fields. However, patchArchiveEntry already calls invalidateArchiveDerivedCaches atomically (convex/archiveEntries.ts:226), deleting all cached turns and views before returning. The residual risk is a narrow race where a concurrent rebuild reads pre-patch state and repopulates the cache after the invalidation; effect is transient inconsistency, not data corruption or data loss. Downgraded to CANON-N002. (3) epic7-public-convex-archive-bypass — public Convex archive functions lack apiKey gating: confirmed that archiveEntries and archiveTurns export raw mutation/query without the *ForService+apiKey pattern that derivedArchiveViews uses. However, a codebase grep found no ConvexProvider, ConvexReactClient, or VITE_CONVEX URL in the client directory; Convex is accessed exclusively server-side through ConvexPlatformStore. The public mutations validate processId/projectId ownership. The pattern is consistent with other repo tables (processes.ts, processOutputs.ts, sourceProvenance.ts). This is an asymmetric hardening gap from Story 5, not an end-user-accessible security bypass. Downgraded to CANON-N003. Both verification gates (green-verify and verify-all) passed in two independent codex runs, and all eight story gates passed in the team-impl-log. The epic infrastructure is correctly implemented with the platform-standup-state limitations explicitly documented and accepted.\\\",\\n  \\\"crossStoryFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"All eight stories compose correctly into the intended archive → turns → structural views flow: canonical entry persistence (Story 1) → finalization boundary (Story 2) → read/reopen surface (Story 3) → turn derivation (Story 4) → structural views (Story 5) → provenance enrichment (Story 6) → degraded-state hardening (Story 7). No cross-story schema or ID drift found.\\\",\\n    \\\"Cache invalidation is consistent end-to-end: appendArchiveEntry and patchArchiveEntry in convex/archiveEntries.ts delete all cached archiveTurns and derivedArchiveViews atomically; upsertArchiveTurns in convex/archiveTurns.ts deletes all cached derivedArchiveViews. This preserves the tech-design contract that turns and views are rebuildable projections, never canonical truth.\\\",\\n    \\\"Deferred archive patching (applyExecutionResultSideEffects writes a degraded row first, reconcileDeferredArchiveEntries patches later) correctly invalidates cached turns and views on patch. The computeArchiveSignature fingerprint in derived-archive-view.service.ts omits provenance and status fields, leaving a narrow race where a concurrent rebuild can repopulate the cache from pre-patch archive state after the patch invalidation. Effect is transient inconsistency, not permanent data corruption.\\\",\\n    \\\"Turn IDs are stable deterministic strings (processId:turn:turnIndex); derived view IDs are deterministic (processId:derived-view:viewKind:startIndex-endIndex). Provenance remains traceable across cache rebuilds as the tech design requires.\\\",\\n    \\\"The first-party runtime archives only user_message (via process-response.service.ts) and process_event (bridged via appendFromProcessHistoryItem). The remaining five PRD-mandated entry kinds (model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, tool_result) are accepted by the Convex primitive and exercised by tests, but the default integrated execution payload returns archiveEntries: []. This is accepted platform-standup state per the team-impl-log and does not violate Epic 7 scope, which covers archive infrastructure rather than model-driven runtime content.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"architectureFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"Module layout matches the tech design: convex/archiveEntries.ts, convex/archiveTurns.ts, convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts, convex/schema.ts, archive services, archive routes, contracts, and client sections are all present and wired as specified.\\\",\\n    \\\"Fastify owns finalization policy and access enforcement; Convex owns atomic sequence assignment, idempotency guard, and durable storage — consistent with the architecture split. Route handlers call processAccessService.assertProcessAccess before any archive read service runs.\\\",\\n    \\\"Asymmetric apiKey gating: convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts uses internalMutation/internalQuery plus *ForService apiKey-checked public wrappers (Story 5 hardening). convex/archiveEntries.ts and convex/archiveTurns.ts expose raw mutation/query. Browser clients have no direct Convex access (no ConvexProvider or VITE_CONVEX URL in client code), so the practical security risk is low. The pattern inconsistency relative to Story 5 is a cohesion gap worth resolving in a follow-up.\\\",\\n    \\\"computeArchiveSignature in derived-archive-view.service.ts fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt. Patches to relatedArtifactVersionId, relatedSourceProvenanceId, relatedToolCallId, entryStatus, or degradationReason via patchArchiveEntry are invisible to the conflict detector. Since patchArchiveEntry also invalidates the cache atomically, the residual risk is a narrow concurrent-rebuild race rather than a systematic data integrity gap.\\\",\\n    \\\"NullPlatformStore is correctly rejected in production (createApp enforces this at startup). It boots without error in non-production, returning synthetic archive responses — a deliberate fallback documented in source comments.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\": [\\n    \\\"AC-1.x (canonical entry persistence, taxonomy, ordering, idempotency, related-id links): Covered. convex/archiveEntries.ts appendArchiveEntry validates all seven entry kinds via a Set, assigns sequence atomically, and enforces finalizationKey idempotency per process.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-2.x (live/archive separation, finalization boundary): Covered. ArchiveFinalizationService is called only from completion points — accepted user responses (process-response.service.ts) and completed runtime objects (process-environment.service.ts). Raw deltas and interrupted partials never call append.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-3.x (archive read, reopen, access boundaries, degraded entry display): Covered. processAccessService.assertProcessAccess is called before every archive read. Empty and degraded states flow through the archivePageSchema parse contract.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-4.x (turn derivation, archive-entry immutability, degraded turn handling): Covered. deriveTurnsFromArchiveEntries is deterministic over sorted entries. Degradation propagates from entries to turns without mutating archive rows.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-5.x (structural derived views without model-generated summaries): Covered. buildDerivedArchiveViews generates turn_range and chunk_candidate views with deterministic structural labels only; no model-generated content path exists.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-6.x (artifact and source provenance enrichment, per-entry degradation): Covered. enrichArchiveEntries batches artifact version and source provenance lookups; each entry degrades independently when its related record is unavailable.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-7.x (reload/environment-loss survival, bounded reads, derived-view failure isolation): Covered. All reads use durable Convex storage. Pagination limits are enforced. Archive reads succeed when derived-view refresh throws — routes handle them independently.\\\",\\n    \\\"Service tests for non-user archive kinds inject RuntimeArchiveEntry fixtures directly; no checked-in production execution path emits model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, or tool_result. Tests prove the infrastructure works; the production producer gap is accepted scope-defer per team-impl-log.\\\",\\n    \\\"Browser e2e test (archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts) uses InMemoryPlatformStore with pre-populated fixtures and validates routing, shell, and UI rendering against a live Fastify server. It does not exercise a ConvexPlatformStore durable-storage round-trip. Acceptable for Epic 7 closeout; convex/archiveEntries.test.ts and service suites cover durable behavior.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"productionPathFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"ConvexPlatformStore is the production store and calls real Convex functions: appendArchiveEntry, upsertArchiveTurns, replaceDerivedArchiveViewsForService, and corresponding reads. No shims or fake adapters on the production path.\\\",\\n    \\\"DefaultArchiveReadService, DefaultTurnDerivationService, and DefaultDerivedArchiveViewService are instantiated unconditionally in app.ts when not overridden via test options.\\\",\\n    \\\"NullPlatformStore and InMemoryPlatformStore are test doubles only. NullPlatformStore is explicitly rejected in production (createApp throws when NODE_ENV=production or LIMINAL_VITE_RUNTIME_MODE=production). InMemoryPlatformStore is only reachable via test options.\\\",\\n    \\\"The default integrated execution payload (buildIntegratedWorkspaceExecutionSource) sets archiveEntries: [] and emits a single process_event history item per run. Canonical archive rows for model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, and tool_result are not produced by any checked-in production code path. Explicitly accepted as platform-standup state in the team-impl-log and in source-level comments.\\\",\\n    \\\"archiveFinalizationService is constructed with a default new ArchiveFinalizationService(platformStore) inside ProcessEnvironmentService and ProcessResponseService constructors. In production, platformStore resolves to ConvexPlatformStore, so the archive finalization path is real and durable.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"blockingFindings\\\": [],\\n  \\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Production runtime emits only two of the seven canonical archive entry kinds\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts buildIntegratedWorkspaceExecutionSource returns archiveEntries: [] in its result payload. process-environment.service.ts bridges process_event from history items; process-response.service.ts archives user_message on accepted responses. No checked-in production code path emits model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, or tool_result. The Convex primitive and service layer accept all seven kinds and tests exercise them via fixtures. The team-impl-log records this as accepted platform-standup state; the source comment explicitly notes the payload 'must not synthesize canonical archive entries'. Epic 7 scope covers archive infrastructure, not model-driven runtime content.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/process-environment.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/process-response.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-1.2\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"computeArchiveSignature omits provenance and status fields; concurrent rebuild can repopulate cache from pre-patch archive state\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts:262-270 fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt. Patches to relatedArtifactVersionId, relatedSourceProvenanceId, relatedToolCallId, entryStatus, or degradationReason via convex/archiveEntries.ts patchArchiveEntry are invisible to the drift detector. patchArchiveEntry calls invalidateArchiveDerivedCaches atomically (convex/archiveEntries.ts:226) deleting cached turns and views. The residual risk is a narrow race: a turn rebuild that started before the patch lands and completes after the cache was cleared will upsert turns derived from pre-patch archive state, repopulating the cache with stale projections. A subsequent derived-view refresh would then read those stale turns. Effect is transient inconsistency rather than permanent data corruption.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-4.1\\\", \\\"AC-4.4\\\", \\\"AC-5.5\\\", \\\"AC-7.1\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"archiveEntries and archiveTurns Convex functions lack the apiKey gate that derivedArchiveViews uses\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts:94, :150, :182 and convex/archiveTurns.ts:45, :96 expose appendArchiveEntry, listArchiveEntries, patchArchiveEntry, upsertArchiveTurns, and listArchiveTurns as raw mutation/query without the apiKey check that convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts:91-107 adds via replaceDerivedArchiveViewsForService. A grep of the client directory found no ConvexProvider, ConvexReactClient, or VITE_CONVEX URL exposure; Convex is accessed exclusively server-side by ConvexPlatformStore. The mutations validate processId/projectId ownership. The pattern is consistent with repo convention for other tables (processes.ts, processOutputs.ts, sourceProvenance.ts) but asymmetric with the Story 5 hardening pattern applied to derivedArchiveViews.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/archiveTurns.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-3.3\\\", \\\"A6\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Derived-view Convex pagination reads the prefix on every page (O(p²) over page index)\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts listDerivedArchiveViews decodes a numeric offset cursor and calls .take(args.limit + decodedCursor + 1), then slices. Page N loads N×limit rows server-side. Total derived views per process are structurally bounded (~1.5× turn count) and the route caps limit at 50, so behavior stays bounded for realistic process sizes but has a quadratic scaling shape if process sizes grow. apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts also accepts any digit-only cursor without range validation.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-7.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"appendFinalizedEntry enforces non-null sourceObjectId for all entry kinds; spec marks it optional\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/archive-finalization.service.ts:31-33 throws for any null sourceObjectId regardless of entry kind. The epic schema marks sourceObjectId optional (nullable when not applicable). If a runtime emits a reasoning or script_emission entry without a sourceObjectId, appendFinalizedEntry will throw rather than delegating to the Convex layer's nullable schema.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/archive-finalization.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-1.1\\\", \\\"AC-2.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N006\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"NullPlatformStore boots without error outside production, masking missing Convex configuration in local/staging environments\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/app.ts:190-227 defaults to NullPlatformStore when hasLiveConvexConfig returns false, only hard-failing when NODE_ENV=production or LIMINAL_VITE_RUNTIME_MODE=production. NullPlatformStore returns synthetic archive write acknowledgements and empty archive/turn/derived-view reads. This is a deliberate non-real path but can mask missing Convex configuration in non-production environments.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/app.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-3.2\\\", \\\"AC-7.1\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N007\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"listAllArchiveEntries pagination helper is duplicated across two service files\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"An identical pagination loop appears in both apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts (lines 159-182) and apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts (lines 273-296). Implementations are semantically identical. Not a functional problem but a cohesion gap relative to the module responsibility matrix.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N008\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"hasStoredTurns check in getTurns is redundant after storedPage.turns.length === 0 is confirmed\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts:103 calls hasStoredTurns (limit:1) after storedPage.turns.length === 0 has already been established. Both queries hit the same first page with cursor=null; the extra round-trip cannot return a different result.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N009\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"void archiveRoutePatterns statement in archive.ts is a code smell\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:59 contains `void archiveRoutePatterns;` which evaluates and discards an object. Appears to suppress a lint warning about an unused import rather than serving any runtime purpose.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N010\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Derived view page limit of 50 may truncate views for processes with 25+ turns\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts MAX_DERIVED_ARCHIVE_VIEW_LIMIT=50. buildDerivedArchiveViews emits one turn_range per turn plus one chunk_candidate per two turns, so a process with 34+ turns generates more than 50 views. Pagination is plumbed but client sections may not surface the next-page cursor to users yet.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/client/features/processes/derived-archive-views-section.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-5.1\\\", \\\"AC-7.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N011\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Default integrated execution payload is a static template rather than a model-driven runtime\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts:20-296 builds execution source using a runtimeProfileByProcessType lookup that hard-codes artifact title, source note title, history text, and commit message per process type. Real files are written and real checkpoint candidates produced, but synthesis content is not model-generated. Source comment documents this as intentional: 'The default payload may produce runtime artifacts/checkpoint candidates, but it must not synthesize canonical archive entries.'\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N012\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Archive e2e test validates routing and UI against InMemoryPlatformStore, not a real Convex durable-storage round-trip\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"tests/e2e/archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts:67-86 constructs InMemoryPlatformStore with pre-populated archive/turn/view fixtures and passes it via buildApp's platformStore option. Playwright asserts on rendered DOM against a live Fastify server. This validates routing, shell, and UI rendering but does not exercise ConvexPlatformStore. Acceptable for Epic 7 closeout; convex/archiveEntries.test.ts and service suites cover durable behavior.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"tests/e2e/archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"tests/utils/build-app.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"unresolvedItems\\\": [\\n    \\\"Whether the apiKey gate pattern from derivedArchiveViews (Story 5 hardening) should be propagated to archiveEntries and archiveTurns needs a human ruling. The current pattern is consistent with repo convention for other tables but asymmetric with the Story 5 fix. Resolution depends on whether the Convex deployment URL is treated as a private server-to-server boundary or as a potentially leakable surface.\\\",\\n    \\\"Whether NullPlatformStore availability outside production is acceptable for local/staging bootstrapping or should be restricted to avoid masking missing Convex config. No code change warranted without a human policy decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"If real model/tool producers are wired before a dedicated hardening story, CANON-N002 (archive signature gap) and CANON-N005 (appendFinalizedEntry sourceObjectId strictness) are the two items most likely to surface as observable runtime bugs at that time.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"gateResult\\\": \\\"pass\\\"\\n}\\n```\"}],\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6530,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":54441,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":6530},\"output_tokens\":8,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"not_available\"},\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"uuid\":\"0bf02574-5688-49e4-87a8-5f417b37070b\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/009-epic-review.stdout.log:5019:{\"type\":\"result\",\"subtype\":\"success\",\"is_error\":false,\"api_error_status\":null,\"duration_ms\":383887,\"duration_api_ms\":385523,\"num_turns\":12,\"result\":\"I now have all the evidence needed. Let me produce the canonical review.\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"outcome\\\": \\\"pass\\\",\\n  \\\"reviewerLabels\\\": [\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-1\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-2\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-3\\\",\\n    \\\"epic-reviewer-4\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"reconciliationSummary\\\": \\\"Two codex reviewers (epic-reviewer-1, epic-reviewer-2) blocked; two Claude reviewers (epic-reviewer-3, epic-reviewer-4) passed. Independent verification of the three contested findings resolves all downward. (1) EPIC7-B001 — no first-party producers for five of seven archive kinds: confirmed in code (script-execution.service.ts emits archiveEntries: []). However, Epic 7 scope covers the archive infrastructure, not a model-driven runtime. The team-impl-log records this as accepted platform-standup state and the source comment states the payload 'must not synthesize canonical archive entries'. Tests exercise all seven entry kinds via fixtures against the Convex primitive. This is a scope-conformant observation, not a blocking violation. Downgraded to CANON-N001. (2) EPIC7-B002 — stale cache during deferred reconciliation: confirmed that computeArchiveSignature fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt, omitting provenance and status fields. However, patchArchiveEntry already calls invalidateArchiveDerivedCaches atomically (convex/archiveEntries.ts:226), deleting all cached turns and views before returning. The residual risk is a narrow race where a concurrent rebuild reads pre-patch state and repopulates the cache after the invalidation; effect is transient inconsistency, not data corruption or data loss. Downgraded to CANON-N002. (3) epic7-public-convex-archive-bypass — public Convex archive functions lack apiKey gating: confirmed that archiveEntries and archiveTurns export raw mutation/query without the *ForService+apiKey pattern that derivedArchiveViews uses. However, a codebase grep found no ConvexProvider, ConvexReactClient, or VITE_CONVEX URL in the client directory; Convex is accessed exclusively server-side through ConvexPlatformStore. The public mutations validate processId/projectId ownership. The pattern is consistent with other repo tables (processes.ts, processOutputs.ts, sourceProvenance.ts). This is an asymmetric hardening gap from Story 5, not an end-user-accessible security bypass. Downgraded to CANON-N003. Both verification gates (green-verify and verify-all) passed in two independent codex runs, and all eight story gates passed in the team-impl-log. The epic infrastructure is correctly implemented with the platform-standup-state limitations explicitly documented and accepted.\\\",\\n  \\\"crossStoryFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"All eight stories compose correctly into the intended archive → turns → structural views flow: canonical entry persistence (Story 1) → finalization boundary (Story 2) → read/reopen surface (Story 3) → turn derivation (Story 4) → structural views (Story 5) → provenance enrichment (Story 6) → degraded-state hardening (Story 7). No cross-story schema or ID drift found.\\\",\\n    \\\"Cache invalidation is consistent end-to-end: appendArchiveEntry and patchArchiveEntry in convex/archiveEntries.ts delete all cached archiveTurns and derivedArchiveViews atomically; upsertArchiveTurns in convex/archiveTurns.ts deletes all cached derivedArchiveViews. This preserves the tech-design contract that turns and views are rebuildable projections, never canonical truth.\\\",\\n    \\\"Deferred archive patching (applyExecutionResultSideEffects writes a degraded row first, reconcileDeferredArchiveEntries patches later) correctly invalidates cached turns and views on patch. The computeArchiveSignature fingerprint in derived-archive-view.service.ts omits provenance and status fields, leaving a narrow race where a concurrent rebuild can repopulate the cache from pre-patch archive state after the patch invalidation. Effect is transient inconsistency, not permanent data corruption.\\\",\\n    \\\"Turn IDs are stable deterministic strings (processId:turn:turnIndex); derived view IDs are deterministic (processId:derived-view:viewKind:startIndex-endIndex). Provenance remains traceable across cache rebuilds as the tech design requires.\\\",\\n    \\\"The first-party runtime archives only user_message (via process-response.service.ts) and process_event (bridged via appendFromProcessHistoryItem). The remaining five PRD-mandated entry kinds (model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, tool_result) are accepted by the Convex primitive and exercised by tests, but the default integrated execution payload returns archiveEntries: []. This is accepted platform-standup state per the team-impl-log and does not violate Epic 7 scope, which covers archive infrastructure rather than model-driven runtime content.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"architectureFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"Module layout matches the tech design: convex/archiveEntries.ts, convex/archiveTurns.ts, convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts, convex/schema.ts, archive services, archive routes, contracts, and client sections are all present and wired as specified.\\\",\\n    \\\"Fastify owns finalization policy and access enforcement; Convex owns atomic sequence assignment, idempotency guard, and durable storage — consistent with the architecture split. Route handlers call processAccessService.assertProcessAccess before any archive read service runs.\\\",\\n    \\\"Asymmetric apiKey gating: convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts uses internalMutation/internalQuery plus *ForService apiKey-checked public wrappers (Story 5 hardening). convex/archiveEntries.ts and convex/archiveTurns.ts expose raw mutation/query. Browser clients have no direct Convex access (no ConvexProvider or VITE_CONVEX URL in client code), so the practical security risk is low. The pattern inconsistency relative to Story 5 is a cohesion gap worth resolving in a follow-up.\\\",\\n    \\\"computeArchiveSignature in derived-archive-view.service.ts fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt. Patches to relatedArtifactVersionId, relatedSourceProvenanceId, relatedToolCallId, entryStatus, or degradationReason via patchArchiveEntry are invisible to the conflict detector. Since patchArchiveEntry also invalidates the cache atomically, the residual risk is a narrow concurrent-rebuild race rather than a systematic data integrity gap.\\\",\\n    \\\"NullPlatformStore is correctly rejected in production (createApp enforces this at startup). It boots without error in non-production, returning synthetic archive responses — a deliberate fallback documented in source comments.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\": [\\n    \\\"AC-1.x (canonical entry persistence, taxonomy, ordering, idempotency, related-id links): Covered. convex/archiveEntries.ts appendArchiveEntry validates all seven entry kinds via a Set, assigns sequence atomically, and enforces finalizationKey idempotency per process.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-2.x (live/archive separation, finalization boundary): Covered. ArchiveFinalizationService is called only from completion points — accepted user responses (process-response.service.ts) and completed runtime objects (process-environment.service.ts). Raw deltas and interrupted partials never call append.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-3.x (archive read, reopen, access boundaries, degraded entry display): Covered. processAccessService.assertProcessAccess is called before every archive read. Empty and degraded states flow through the archivePageSchema parse contract.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-4.x (turn derivation, archive-entry immutability, degraded turn handling): Covered. deriveTurnsFromArchiveEntries is deterministic over sorted entries. Degradation propagates from entries to turns without mutating archive rows.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-5.x (structural derived views without model-generated summaries): Covered. buildDerivedArchiveViews generates turn_range and chunk_candidate views with deterministic structural labels only; no model-generated content path exists.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-6.x (artifact and source provenance enrichment, per-entry degradation): Covered. enrichArchiveEntries batches artifact version and source provenance lookups; each entry degrades independently when its related record is unavailable.\\\",\\n    \\\"AC-7.x (reload/environment-loss survival, bounded reads, derived-view failure isolation): Covered. All reads use durable Convex storage. Pagination limits are enforced. Archive reads succeed when derived-view refresh throws — routes handle them independently.\\\",\\n    \\\"Service tests for non-user archive kinds inject RuntimeArchiveEntry fixtures directly; no checked-in production execution path emits model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, or tool_result. Tests prove the infrastructure works; the production producer gap is accepted scope-defer per team-impl-log.\\\",\\n    \\\"Browser e2e test (archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts) uses InMemoryPlatformStore with pre-populated fixtures and validates routing, shell, and UI rendering against a live Fastify server. It does not exercise a ConvexPlatformStore durable-storage round-trip. Acceptable for Epic 7 closeout; convex/archiveEntries.test.ts and service suites cover durable behavior.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"productionPathFindings\\\": [\\n    \\\"ConvexPlatformStore is the production store and calls real Convex functions: appendArchiveEntry, upsertArchiveTurns, replaceDerivedArchiveViewsForService, and corresponding reads. No shims or fake adapters on the production path.\\\",\\n    \\\"DefaultArchiveReadService, DefaultTurnDerivationService, and DefaultDerivedArchiveViewService are instantiated unconditionally in app.ts when not overridden via test options.\\\",\\n    \\\"NullPlatformStore and InMemoryPlatformStore are test doubles only. NullPlatformStore is explicitly rejected in production (createApp throws when NODE_ENV=production or LIMINAL_VITE_RUNTIME_MODE=production). InMemoryPlatformStore is only reachable via test options.\\\",\\n    \\\"The default integrated execution payload (buildIntegratedWorkspaceExecutionSource) sets archiveEntries: [] and emits a single process_event history item per run. Canonical archive rows for model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, and tool_result are not produced by any checked-in production code path. Explicitly accepted as platform-standup state in the team-impl-log and in source-level comments.\\\",\\n    \\\"archiveFinalizationService is constructed with a default new ArchiveFinalizationService(platformStore) inside ProcessEnvironmentService and ProcessResponseService constructors. In production, platformStore resolves to ConvexPlatformStore, so the archive finalization path is real and durable.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"blockingFindings\\\": [],\\n  \\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N001\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Production runtime emits only two of the seven canonical archive entry kinds\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts buildIntegratedWorkspaceExecutionSource returns archiveEntries: [] in its result payload. process-environment.service.ts bridges process_event from history items; process-response.service.ts archives user_message on accepted responses. No checked-in production code path emits model_message, reasoning, script_emission, tool_call, or tool_result. The Convex primitive and service layer accept all seven kinds and tests exercise them via fixtures. The team-impl-log records this as accepted platform-standup state; the source comment explicitly notes the payload 'must not synthesize canonical archive entries'. Epic 7 scope covers archive infrastructure, not model-driven runtime content.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/process-environment.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/process-response.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-1.2\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N002\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"computeArchiveSignature omits provenance and status fields; concurrent rebuild can repopulate cache from pre-patch archive state\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts:262-270 fingerprints only archiveEntryId:sequence:recordedAt. Patches to relatedArtifactVersionId, relatedSourceProvenanceId, relatedToolCallId, entryStatus, or degradationReason via convex/archiveEntries.ts patchArchiveEntry are invisible to the drift detector. patchArchiveEntry calls invalidateArchiveDerivedCaches atomically (convex/archiveEntries.ts:226) deleting cached turns and views. The residual risk is a narrow race: a turn rebuild that started before the patch lands and completes after the cache was cleared will upsert turns derived from pre-patch archive state, repopulating the cache with stale projections. A subsequent derived-view refresh would then read those stale turns. Effect is transient inconsistency rather than permanent data corruption.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-4.1\\\", \\\"AC-4.4\\\", \\\"AC-5.5\\\", \\\"AC-7.1\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N003\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"archiveEntries and archiveTurns Convex functions lack the apiKey gate that derivedArchiveViews uses\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts:94, :150, :182 and convex/archiveTurns.ts:45, :96 expose appendArchiveEntry, listArchiveEntries, patchArchiveEntry, upsertArchiveTurns, and listArchiveTurns as raw mutation/query without the apiKey check that convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts:91-107 adds via replaceDerivedArchiveViewsForService. A grep of the client directory found no ConvexProvider, ConvexReactClient, or VITE_CONVEX URL exposure; Convex is accessed exclusively server-side by ConvexPlatformStore. The mutations validate processId/projectId ownership. The pattern is consistent with repo convention for other tables (processes.ts, processOutputs.ts, sourceProvenance.ts) but asymmetric with the Story 5 hardening pattern applied to derivedArchiveViews.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"convex/archiveEntries.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/archiveTurns.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-3.3\\\", \\\"A6\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N004\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Derived-view Convex pagination reads the prefix on every page (O(p²) over page index)\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts listDerivedArchiveViews decodes a numeric offset cursor and calls .take(args.limit + decodedCursor + 1), then slices. Page N loads N×limit rows server-side. Total derived views per process are structurally bounded (~1.5× turn count) and the route caps limit at 50, so behavior stays bounded for realistic process sizes but has a quadratic scaling shape if process sizes grow. apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts also accepts any digit-only cursor without range validation.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"convex/derivedArchiveViews.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-7.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N005\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"minor\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"appendFinalizedEntry enforces non-null sourceObjectId for all entry kinds; spec marks it optional\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/archive-finalization.service.ts:31-33 throws for any null sourceObjectId regardless of entry kind. The epic schema marks sourceObjectId optional (nullable when not applicable). If a runtime emits a reasoning or script_emission entry without a sourceObjectId, appendFinalizedEntry will throw rather than delegating to the Convex layer's nullable schema.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/archive-finalization.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-1.1\\\", \\\"AC-2.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N006\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"NullPlatformStore boots without error outside production, masking missing Convex configuration in local/staging environments\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/app.ts:190-227 defaults to NullPlatformStore when hasLiveConvexConfig returns false, only hard-failing when NODE_ENV=production or LIMINAL_VITE_RUNTIME_MODE=production. NullPlatformStore returns synthetic archive write acknowledgements and empty archive/turn/derived-view reads. This is a deliberate non-real path but can mask missing Convex configuration in non-production environments.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/app.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-3.2\\\", \\\"AC-7.1\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N007\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"listAllArchiveEntries pagination helper is duplicated across two service files\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"An identical pagination loop appears in both apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts (lines 159-182) and apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts (lines 273-296). Implementations are semantically identical. Not a functional problem but a cohesion gap relative to the module responsibility matrix.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N008\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"hasStoredTurns check in getTurns is redundant after storedPage.turns.length === 0 is confirmed\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts:103 calls hasStoredTurns (limit:1) after storedPage.turns.length === 0 has already been established. Both queries hit the same first page with cursor=null; the extra round-trip cannot return a different result.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/turn-derivation.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N009\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"void archiveRoutePatterns statement in archive.ts is a code smell\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:59 contains `void archiveRoutePatterns;` which evaluates and discards an object. Appears to suppress a lint warning about an unused import rather than serving any runtime purpose.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"quick-fix\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N010\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Derived view page limit of 50 may truncate views for processes with 25+ turns\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts MAX_DERIVED_ARCHIVE_VIEW_LIMIT=50. buildDerivedArchiveViews emits one turn_range per turn plus one chunk_candidate per two turns, so a process with 34+ turns generates more than 50 views. Pagination is plumbed but client sections may not surface the next-page cursor to users yet.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/archive/derived-archive-view.service.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"apps/platform/client/features/processes/derived-archive-views-section.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [\\\"AC-5.1\\\", \\\"AC-7.3\\\"],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N011\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Default integrated execution payload is a static template rather than a model-driven runtime\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts:20-296 builds execution source using a runtimeProfileByProcessType lookup that hard-codes artifact title, source note title, history text, and commit message per process type. Real files are written and real checkpoint candidates produced, but synthesis content is not model-generated. Source comment documents this as intentional: 'The default payload may produce runtime artifacts/checkpoint candidates, but it must not synthesize canonical archive entries.'\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/script-execution.service.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"id\\\": \\\"CANON-N012\\\",\\n      \\\"severity\\\": \\\"observation\\\",\\n      \\\"title\\\": \\\"Archive e2e test validates routing and UI against InMemoryPlatformStore, not a real Convex durable-storage round-trip\\\",\\n      \\\"evidence\\\": \\\"tests/e2e/archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts:67-86 constructs InMemoryPlatformStore with pre-populated archive/turn/view fixtures and passes it via buildApp's platformStore option. Playwright asserts on rendered DOM against a live Fastify server. This validates routing, shell, and UI rendering but does not exercise ConvexPlatformStore. Acceptable for Epic 7 closeout; convex/archiveEntries.test.ts and service suites cover durable behavior.\\\",\\n      \\\"affectedFiles\\\": [\\n        \\\"tests/e2e/archive-runtime-flow.spec.ts\\\",\\n        \\\"tests/utils/build-app.ts\\\"\\n      ],\\n      \\\"requirementIds\\\": [],\\n      \\\"recommendedFixScope\\\": \\\"human-ruling\\\",\\n      \\\"blocking\\\": false\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"unresolvedItems\\\": [\\n    \\\"Whether the apiKey gate pattern from derivedArchiveViews (Story 5 hardening) should be propagated to archiveEntries and archiveTurns needs a human ruling. The current pattern is consistent with repo convention for other tables but asymmetric with the Story 5 fix. Resolution depends on whether the Convex deployment URL is treated as a private server-to-server boundary or as a potentially leakable surface.\\\",\\n    \\\"Whether NullPlatformStore availability outside production is acceptable for local/staging bootstrapping or should be restricted to avoid masking missing Convex config. No code change warranted without a human policy decision.\\\",\\n    \\\"If real model/tool producers are wired before a dedicated hardening story, CANON-N002 (archive signature gap) and CANON-N005 (appendFinalizedEntry sourceObjectId strictness) are the two items most likely to surface as observable runtime bugs at that time.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"gateResult\\\": \\\"pass\\\"\\n}\\n```\",\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"2a93b29d-c885-4744-841e-e7896fb6b8e6\",\"total_cost_usd\":0.5702847,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":6,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":48936,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":191619,\"output_tokens\":21182,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":48936,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":1,\"output_tokens\":13581,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":54441,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":6530,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":6530},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001\":{\"inputTokens\":11461,\"outputTokens\":16,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":0,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.011541000000000001,\"contextWindow\":200000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000},\"claude-sonnet-4-6[1m]\":{\"inputTokens\":6,\"outputTokens\":21182,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":191619,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":48936,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":0.5587437,\"contextWindow\":1000000,\"maxOutputTokens\":32000}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"uuid\":\"d36c3231-5f04-48b3-b169-35c50ca43bfd\"}\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/epic/streams/006-epic-synthesis.stdout.log:83:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_40\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"import type { FastifyInstance } from 'fastify';\\nimport type { ZodTypeProvider } from 'fastify-type-provider-zod';\\nimport { buildShellBootstrapPayload } from '../config.js';\\nimport { AppError } from '../errors/app-error.js';\\nimport {\\n  getDerivedArchiveViewsRouteSchema,\\n  getProcessArchiveRouteSchema,\\n  getProcessArchiveTurnsRouteSchema,\\n  postRefreshDerivedArchiveViewsRouteSchema,\\n} from '../schemas/archive.js';\\nimport { sessionCookieName } from '../services/auth/auth-session.service.js';\\nimport {\\n  processArchiveApiPathnamePattern,\\n  processArchiveTurnsApiPathnamePattern,\\n  processDerivedArchiveViewsApiPathnamePattern,\\n  processDerivedArchiveViewsRefreshApiPathnamePattern,\\n  processArchiveRoutePathnamePattern,\\n  type RequestError,\\n} from '../../shared/contracts/index.js';\\n\\nfunction buildRequestError(error: AppError): RequestError {\\n  return {\\n    code: error.code as RequestError['code'],\\n    message: error.message,\\n    status: error.statusCode,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nexport const archiveRoutePatterns = {\\n  shell: processArchiveRoutePathnamePattern,\\n  bootstrap: processArchiveApiPathnamePattern,\\n  turns: processArchiveTurnsApiPathnamePattern,\\n  derivedViews: processDerivedArchiveViewsApiPathnamePattern,\\n  refreshDerivedViews: processDerivedArchiveViewsRefreshApiPathnamePattern,\\n} as const;\\n\\nfunction buildLoginRedirectPath(returnTo: string): string {\\n  return `/auth/login?returnTo=${encodeURIComponent(returnTo)}`;\\n}\\n\\nfunction renderUnavailableShell(title: string, message: string): string {\\n  return `<!doctype html>\\n<html lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n  <head>\\n    <meta charset=\\\"utf-8\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"viewport\\\" content=\\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\\\" />\\n    <title>${title}</title>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <main>\\n      <h1>${title}</h1>\\n      <p>${message}</p>\\n    </main>\\n  </body>\\n</html>`;\\n}\\n\\nexport async function registerArchiveRoutes(app: FastifyInstance): Promise<void> {\\n  void archiveRoutePatterns;\\n\\n  const typedApp = app.withTypeProvider<ZodTypeProvider>();\\n\\n  typedApp.get(\\n    '/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/archive',\\n    {\\n      schema: {\\n        params: getProcessArchiveRouteSchema.params,\\n      },\\n    },\\n    async (request, reply) => {\\n      if (request.actor === null) {\\n        if (request.authFailureReason === 'invalid_session') {\\n          reply.clearCookie(sessionCookieName, { path: '/' });\\n        }\\n\\n        return reply.redirect(buildLoginRedirectPath(request.url));\\n      }\\n\\n      const access = await app.processAccessService.getProcessAccess({\\n        actor: request.actor,\\n        projectId: request.params.projectId,\\n        processId: request.params.processId,\\n      });\\n\\n      if (access.kind === 'forbidden') {\\n        return reply\\n          .code(403)\\n          .type('text/html')\\n          .send(renderUnavailableShell('Access denied', 'You do not have access to this process.'));\\n      }\\n\\n      if (access.kind === 'project_not_found') {\\n        return reply\\n          .code(404)\\n          .type('text/html')\\n          .send(\\n            renderUnavailableShell(\\n              'Project not found',\\n              'The requested project could not be found.',\\n            ),\\n          );\\n      }\\n\\n      if (access.kind === 'process_not_found') {\\n        return reply\\n          .code(404)\\n          .type('text/html')\\n          .send(\\n            renderUnavailableShell(\\n              'Process unavailable',\\n              'The requested process archive could not be opened.',\\n            ),\\n          );\\n      }\\n\\n      const url = new URL(request.url, 'http://story3.local');\\n      const payload = buildShellBootstrapPayload({\\n        actor: {\\n          id: request.actor.userId,\\n          email: request.actor.email,\\n          displayName: request.actor.displayName,\\n        },\\n        pathname: `/projects/${request.params.projectId}/processes/${request.params.processId}/archive`,\\n        search: url.search,\\n        csrfToken: reply.generateCsrf(),\\n      });\\n\\n      return reply.type('text/html').send(await app.renderShellDocument(payload, request.url));\\n    },\\n  );\\n\\n  typedApp.get(\\n    '/api/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/archive/turns',\\n    {\\n      schema: getProcessArchiveTurnsRouteSchema,\\n    },\\n    async (request, reply) => {\\n      if (request.actor === null) {\\n        if (request.authFailureReason === 'invalid_session') {\\n          reply.clearCookie(sessionCookieName, { path: '/' });\\n        }\\n\\n        return reply.code(401).send({\\n          code: 'UNAUTHENTICATED',\\n          message: 'Authenticated access is required.',\\n          status: 401,\\n        });\\n      }\\n\\n      try {\\n        const turns = await app.turnDerivationService.getTurns({\\n          actor: request.actor,\\n          projectId: request.params.projectId,\\n          processId: request.params.processId,\\n          cursor: request.query.cursor,\\n          limit: request.query.limit,\\n        });\\n\\n        return reply.code(200).send(turns);\\n      } catch (error) {\\n        if (error instanceof AppError) {\\n          const statusCode = error.statusCode as 403 | 404 | 422;\\n\\n          return reply.code(statusCode).send(buildRequestError(error));\\n        }\\n\\n        throw error;\\n      }\\n    },\\n  );\\n\\n  typedApp.get(\\n    '/api/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/archive/derived-views',\\n    {\\n      schema: getDerivedArchiveViewsRouteSchema,\\n    },\\n    async (request, reply) => {\\n      if (request.actor === null) {\\n        if (request.authFailureReason === 'invalid_session') {\\n          reply.clearCookie(sessionCookieName, { path: '/' });\\n        }\\n\\n        return reply.code(401).send({\\n          code: 'UNAUTHENTICATED',\\n          message: 'Authenticated access is required.',\\n          status: 401,\\n        });\\n      }\\n\\n      try {\\n        const response = await app.derivedArchiveViewService.listViews({\\n          actor: request.actor,\\n          projectId: request.params.projectId,\\n          processId: request.params.processId,\\n          cursor: request.query.cursor,\\n          limit: request.query.limit,\\n        });\\n\\n        return reply.code(200).send(response);\\n      } catch (error) {\\n        if