docs/onboarding/current-state-index.md:78:| `FeatureImplementation` | `processFeatureImplementationStates` stores current artifact/source refs | Own dedicated process-type state row on creation | Draft creation, shell summary behavior, process-surface bootstrap shape, generic start/resume/respond lifecycle logic | No process-type-specific module, prompts, phase transitions, or request generation in repo code |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/onboarding/current-state-index.md:91:| Project and process shell | Authenticated project index, project shell bootstrap, process registration, shell summaries for processes/artifacts/source attachments, route-based selected-process handling, and durable reopen behavior | [current-state-project-and-process-shell.md](./current-state-project-and-process-shell.md) |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/onboarding/current-state-index.md:119:- Current process materials are a projection of current artifact/source refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:21:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_9\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/stories/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Story 1: Project Artifact Association Without Process Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Summary field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nMake project artifacts project-scoped durable assets, keep process-local visibility in current materials, and remove single-process ownership language from project-shell artifact state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Description\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Description field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**User Profile**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Primary User:** A technical founder, tech lead, staff engineer, or agentic operator who builds work across multiple related processes inside one project.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Context:** The user accumulates durable artifacts across planning, specification, implementation, and review work. They need those artifacts to remain stable project assets even when later processes revise, review, or package them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Mental Model:** \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Artifacts belong to the project. Processes work with them, create new versions of them, and package them together, but no single process owns the artifact itself.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Key Constraint:** This epic must realign artifact behavior inside the existing project shell, process work surface, and review/package surfaces without adding a separate standalone artifact-management product surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Objective**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRealign project-shell and process-surface artifact visibility so artifacts are durable project assets while current process visibility remains process-owned.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIn:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact summary no longer implies primary process ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing project artifacts can be added to a process's current refs without transferring ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- New process-created artifacts appear in the project artifact set immediately\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process materials continue to show only the artifacts currently referenced by that process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOut:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Versioned revision write-path changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Artifact review resolution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Package publication or package review behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Dependencies**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Story 0 foundation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing project shell and process work surface routes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [tech-design.md](../tech-design.md), [tech-design-server.md](../tech-design-server.md), [tech-design-client.md](../tech-design-client.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [test-plan.md](../test-plan.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.1:** A process can reference an existing artifact in the same project without becoming the artifact's sole owner.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.1a: Existing project artifact added to process**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A project already has a durable artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: A later process begins working with that artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The process can reference the artifact without replacing prior artifact provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.1b: Later process reference does not erase earlier process lineage**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: An artifact was first created or revised by an earlier process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: A later process references that artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: Earlier artifact provenance remains intact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.2:** A new artifact created during process work is immediately part of the project's artifact set.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.2a: New process artifact appears in project artifact state**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A process creates a new artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The creation settles durably\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact appears in the project's artifact state without a separate add-to-project step\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.2b: Project artifact state remains deduplicated**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: Several processes later reference the same artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The project artifact state is read\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: That artifact appears as one durable project artifact, not one row per process reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.3:** Process-local artifact visibility reflects current process reference, not artifact ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.3a: Current process materials show referenced artifact**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A process currently references an artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The process work surface is loaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact appears in that process's current materials\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.3b: Unrelated process does not gain automatic current-material visibility**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: One process currently references an artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: Another process in the same project loads its current materials\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact is not shown as current for that unrelated process unless that process also references it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Technical Design\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis story owns the first visible expression of the aligned model: project-scoped artifact identity and process-local current visibility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Endpoints\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Operation | Method | Path | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Get project shell | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}` | Returns project-level artifact state with project-scoped artifact identity and latest version summary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Get process work surface | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}` | Returns process-local current artifact materials and current outputs without implying artifact ownership |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Project Artifact Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Field | Type | Required | Validation | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifactId` | string | yes | non-empty | Stable artifact identifier |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `displayName` | string | yes | non-empty | Human-readable artifact name |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `currentVersionLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Latest current version label |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `updatedAt` | timestamp | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Latest durable update time for this artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Process Artifact Reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Field | Type | Required | Validation | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifactId` | string | yes | non-empty | Stable artifact identifier |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `displayName` | string | yes | non-empty | Artifact name in process context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `currentVersionLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Latest current version label for the artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `roleLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Process-local description such as current working artifact or referenced artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `updatedAt` | timestamp | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Latest durable artifact update time |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplementation note:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact rows no longer include `attachmentScope`, `processId`, or `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process-local visibility continues to come from bounded current refs in per-type process state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSee the tech design document for full architecture, implementation targets, and test mapping.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Definition of Done\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact summaries show project-scoped identity only and no longer imply artifact ownership by one process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing artifacts can be added to a process's current refs without changing artifact identity or provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process materials show only the current process's referenced artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project artifact state remains deduplicated when many processes reference one artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Story tests cover TC-1.1a through TC-1.1b, TC-1.2a through TC-1.2b, and TC-1.3a through TC-1.3b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:23:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_10\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Technical Design: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Purpose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis document translates Epic 5 into implementable architecture for the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact-model alignment step that sits between Epic 4's review/package surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand Epic 6's broader source-management work. It is the index document for a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfour-file tech design set:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Document | Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design.md` | Decision record, spec validation, inherited architecture, system view, module architecture overview, and work breakdown |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-server.md` | Fastify, review services, `PlatformStore`, Convex schema, migration plan, package-context model, and error taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-client.md` | Project-shell artifact summary changes, review workspace behavior, target-selection flows, bounded degraded states, and route/state updates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test-plan.md` | TC-to-test mapping, fixtures, mock strategy, file inventory, chunk counts, and verification gates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe downstream consumers are:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Audience | What they need from this design |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|---------------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Reviewers | A clear account of what is changing in the durable model, what is staying process-owned, and where the current code still encodes the old ownership assumption |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Story authors | Story-aligned chunk boundaries that match the epic's sequencing and leave no ambiguity about which contracts, migrations, and tests belong together |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Implementers | Exact file targets, durable-state decisions, review-context rules, package-context rules, migration sequencing, and verification commands |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Spec Validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is designable. The requirements are concrete, the ACs and TCs are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nusefully specific, and the epic asks the right questions for this phase. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmain work in validation was not repairing a broken epic; it was separating the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nepic's aligned target model from the current repo's partially pre-alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimplementation so the design could speak to both honestly.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe most important validation outcome is that the current repo is already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfarther along than the onboarding pack says. The onboarding docs in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`docs/onboarding/` were cut on `2026-04-15` at commit `d85d69b...`, but the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking tree for this design is at `f14c88e...` on `2026-04-25`. Review and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage surfaces now exist in the code and tests. What Epic 5 has to realign\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis not a hypothetical future review layer; it is a real, shipped-in-repo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview/package implementation whose core artifact assumptions are still too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclose to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"single primary process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat current-state gap changes the design emphasis. Epic 5 is not primarily a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnet-new UI epic. It is a data-model, read-model, and policy-alignment epic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat preserves the existing review/package surfaces while changing the rules\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose surfaces rely on. The design therefore stays tightly coupled to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexisting Fastify control plane, `PlatformStore` boundary, shared contracts, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview route family rather than inventing new top-level product surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Issues Found\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Issue | Spec Location | Resolution | Status |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|---------------|------------|--------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current onboarding pack says the markdown review workspace and package surface are not present, but the current repo now contains `routes/review.ts`, review services, review client pages, and full review/package test suites | `docs/onboarding/current-state-process-work-surface.md`, `docs/onboarding/current-state-tech-design.md` | Treat the onboarding pack as a stale baseline for this domain. Design against current code and tests, and record the drift here so later doc refresh work has a concrete starting point | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Epic 5's error-response table does not distinguish between review-workspace bootstrap reads, which benefit from bounded degraded target states, and target-specific follow-up reads, which can legitimately return request-level 404/409 responses | Data Contracts → Error Responses; AC-5.3, AC-5.4 | Design keeps the existing review-workspace bootstrap pattern: once project/process context resolves, `GET /review` returns `200` with bounded `target.status` / `target.error` degradation. Target-specific follow-up reads continue to use request-level `404` / `409` when the caller asks for an explicit missing version, missing member, or disallowed publication member | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current `PlatformStore` interface owns high-level review-target composition and package review assembly, which bakes the pre-alignment same-process assumptions into every store implementation | Current code: `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts`; Epic Q3, Q5 | Epic 5 narrows `PlatformStore` back toward durable-state primitives and moves review-context composition into server review services and pure helpers. This is an intentional design deviation because the old interface shape would force the wrong policy to keep leaking into every store implementation | Resolved — deviated |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package publication logic still requires every pinned member version to have `createdByProcessId === publishingProcessId`, which directly conflicts with AC-4.1 and AC-4.2 | Current code: package publication path in `platform-store.ts`; AC-4.1, AC-4.2 | Publish eligibility is rewritten around same-project membership plus current package-building context, not same-process production. Cross-process package members become valid when they are either current versions of currently referenced artifacts or explicitly pinned in the same process's current package context | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic asks how current package-building context should be represented durably, but the current repo only has immutable `packageSnapshots` and their members | Tech Design Questions Q4 | Design adds a dedicated mutable package-context model rather than overloading immutable package snapshots or broadening current-refs into a catch-all relation table | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic requires version provenance visibility via `producedByProcessId`, but raw ids alone are too thin for a user-facing provenance surface | Data Contracts → Artifact Version Summary; AC-2.2 | Design keeps `producedByProcessId` in browser-facing contracts and adds derived `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` so the UI can show readable provenance while still preserving the stable id | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 4 established the first review/package surface, but it did so on top of a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndurable artifact model that still leaked an older assumption: one artifact row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncould still look like it \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"belonged\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to one process. The repo has already moved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsome of the right semantics down to the version layer. `artifactVersions` has\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`createdByProcessId`, the process work surface already projects current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmaterials from per-process current refs, and package snapshots already pin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexplicit version ids. Epic 5 exists because those good local decisions are not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyet the platform's full governing model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe problem shows up anywhere a later process touches earlier work. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureSpecification` process should be able to reference a PRD created by a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProductDefinition` process without the artifact changing owners. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureImplementation` process should be able to revise a tech design artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfrom an earlier specification process without rewriting the artifact row to say\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"this belongs to implementation now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" A publishing process should be able to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbundle pinned versions from several processes into one reviewable package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwithout being told that only versions it personally produced are legitimate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is why Epic 5 belongs between Epic 4 and Epic 6 rather than inside either\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nof them. Epic 4 proved there is a usable review/package surface. Epic 6 will\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmake broader claims about canonical sources, provenance, freshness, and later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\narchive work. If Epic 6 inherited the current artifact assumptions, it would\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhave to untangle project-scoped artifact identity, version provenance, process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking context, and package membership at the same time it is trying to settle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsource-management behavior. Epic 5 narrows the job: fix the artifact world\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfirst so later source work inherits the right semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current repo architecture helps here. Fastify already owns the review route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfamily, the client already owns a dedicated review workspace state slice, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe durable model already has most of the necessary nouns: project artifacts,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact versions, per-process current refs, package snapshots, and package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers. The design therefore does not need a new product surface or a new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-runtime abstraction. It needs a cleaner separation of concerns and a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsmall amount of new durable state where the current implementation is missing a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshared, process-scoped package-context model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe alignment target is simple to say even if it touches a lot of code:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifacts are project-scoped durable identities, versions carry producing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess provenance, current process refs express what a process is working with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnow, package contexts express which earlier versions a process has explicitly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npinned into current publishing work, and package snapshots express the durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npublished result. Review and package behavior are allowed to look at all of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose layers, but none of those layers should collapse back into one overloaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifact owner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" field.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Inherited Decisions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 does not add new runtime dependencies and does not reopen the platform's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsettled architectural choices. It inherits the current stack and works within\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe same runtime surfaces already in use.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Area | Current Choice | Source | Epic 5 Posture |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|----------------|--------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Control plane | Fastify 5 monolith | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. All browser-facing behavior still routes through Fastify |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable state | Convex behind `PlatformStore` | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. Artifact and package alignment remain durable-store concerns, not browser concerns |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Client build and app shell | Vite-built client, Fastify-served shell | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. No separate review SPA or artifact admin app |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared contracts | Zod schemas in `apps/platform/shared/contracts` | Current repo | Keep. Epic 5 changes those schemas rather than bypassing them |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review surface shape | Existing `/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/review` route family | Current repo | Keep. Behavior realigns inside the existing route family |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process current refs | Per-type process state tables store bounded current artifact ids | Current repo + epic assumptions | Keep as the bounded working-set model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Epic-Scoped Stack Additions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNone. Epic 5 deliberately avoids new libraries. The work is in durable-state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshape, contract shape, service composition, and test coverage, not in new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage adoption.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Tech Design Question Answers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q1. Should durable process-to-artifact relationship beyond current working-set refs be modeled through a dedicated relation table or by extending the existing current-ref model?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDo not add a generic process-to-artifact relation table in Epic 5.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current-ref arrays in the per-type process state tables are still the right\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmodel for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what this process is working with now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" They are bounded, already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfit the process work-surface materials panel, and already line up with the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-first architecture. The problem is not that current refs are too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsimple; the problem is that the platform has been asking current refs to carry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconcerns they were never meant to carry.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 therefore keeps current refs for current working state and introduces a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nseparate durable package-context model for the one thing current refs cannot\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nencode cleanly: earlier pinned artifact versions that a process still needs to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncarry forward in package publication and reopen/edit flows. That new context is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot a generic relation table. It is a process-scoped package-building context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwith version-aware members, ordering, and reopen semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis split is intentional:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what artifacts are current for this process?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package-context members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"which artifact versions has this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  explicitly pinned into current package-building work?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- published package members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what exact versions did this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  publish as a durable package snapshot?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat gives the platform three precise layers instead of one mushy relation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlayer.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q2. Which existing artifact summary fields should be removed versus preserved as derived process-local display fields?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAt the project shell level, remove the artifact-level process fields entirely.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current `ArtifactSummary` contract still includes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `attachmentScope`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThose fields make sense only if a project artifact can still be described as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nattached to one primary process. Epic 5 removes them from the project-shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact summary contract and UI. The project shell keeps only project-scoped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity plus latest-version projection:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe process-local display fields stay where they belong: on\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProcessArtifactReference` in the process materials panel. That contract already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhas the right shape for this epic:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `roleLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is the cleanest separation between project-scoped durable summary and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-owned current meaning.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q3. How should current review-target eligibility be computed so it remains process-scoped without depending on single-process artifact ownership?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nReview eligibility becomes a process-context computation, not an artifact-row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncheck.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe aligned algorithm is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Start with the process's current artifact refs from its per-type state row.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add any artifact versions already pinned in that same process's current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package-building context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Add any artifact versions pinned inside package snapshots published from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   that same process, because reopening package review remains a valid process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Resolve those ids against the project's durable artifact identities and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   artifact versions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFrom that combined process review context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact targets in `availableTargets` come from artifact identities that are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  either currently referenced by the process or explicitly pinned in that same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process's current package-building context, provided the artifact has at least\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  one durable version and remains in this process review context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version current refs remain excluded from the default artifact target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  list\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package targets in `availableTargets` come from published package snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  for that process, including snapshots whose members may all be unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  later; the snapshot remains reviewable as a durable provenance object even\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  when its selected-member state is fully degraded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- direct artifact review succeeds when the artifact is reachable through\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current refs or pinned package context for that same process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unrelated project artifacts remain unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis keeps review process-scoped without relying on artifact-row ownership,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlatest-version producer, or any other single-field shortcut.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q4. How should the already-defined current package-building context be represented durably so package publish, review, and reopen flows share the same bounded member set?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAdd a dedicated mutable package-context model:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContexts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContextMembers`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContexts` is the header row for the current package-building\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontext owned by one process. It stores:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageType`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `basePackageSnapshotId` for reopen/edit-from-snapshot flows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContextMembers` stores the ordered, explicit version pins inside\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat current context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageContextId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `position`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactVersionId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `versionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `pinnedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis context is mutable and process-scoped. Published `packageSnapshots` remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimmutable and durable. A publishing process can request a snapshot from any\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmember set that is valid inside its current package-building context. Reopening\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nan existing package automatically seeds the current context from the published\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsnapshot's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers, after which the process may keep earlier pinned versions, replace some\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers with the current versions of currently referenced artifacts, or republish.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is cleaner than overloading immutable package snapshots into drafts and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncleaner than trying to make current refs carry version-specific package pins.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q5. What is the cleanest migration path for persisted artifact rows and review tests that currently assume a primary process on the artifact row?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nUse a staged code migration with a pragmatic data posture:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Change shared contracts and read models first so project-shell and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review/workspace code stop relying on artifact-row process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add the new package-context tables and store methods before removing the old\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package publication rule, so current and future publishers have a durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bounded context to validate against.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Rewrite artifact checkpoint, review eligibility, and package publication to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   stop reading `artifacts.processId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Remove `processId` from the `artifacts` table and delete the legacy summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   fields from `ArtifactSummary`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. Migrate or reset dev data as needed. Because this repo is still pre-customer,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   a dev deployment reset is acceptable if a local environment does not need\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   data preservation. The design still supports a one-off migration helper for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   teams that want to preserve local dev rows while iterating.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTest migration follows the same order: first update fixtures and contract tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nto the slimmer project artifact summary, then update review/package tests to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nassert process-context eligibility instead of same-process ownership, then add\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-process package and degraded-state coverage on top.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## System View\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 lives inside the same four runtime surfaces that already exist for this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npart of the product: Fastify routes and services, shared client/server\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontracts, the browser workspace/state layer, and Convex durable state. What\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchanges is the relationship between the concepts inside those surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Top-Tier Surfaces Touched\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Source | Epic 5 Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Projects | Inherited from platform tech arch + current project shell | Project-shell artifact summaries become truly project-scoped and lose artifact-level process fields |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Processes | Inherited from platform tech arch + current process work surface | Process current refs remain the bounded working-set model; review enablement is recomputed from aligned review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Artifacts | Inherited from platform tech arch + current artifact/version tables | Artifact identity becomes project-scoped only; version provenance remains version-scoped |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review Workspace | Existing Epic 4 surface | Review target resolution shifts from artifact ownership to process review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Packages | Existing Epic 4 surface, expanded in this epic | Published snapshots stay immutable; current package-building context becomes durable and mutable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared Contracts | Current repo | Project summaries, review contracts, error codes, and client state all realign around the new model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### System Context Diagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nflowchart LR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Client[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Browser Client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Shell[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Surface[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Review[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Fastify[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fastify Control Plane\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProjectSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ReviewSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Context + Review Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageCtx[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Package Context Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Convex[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Convex Durable State\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Artifacts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Versions[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifactVersions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessState[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"process*States current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageContexts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"processPackageContexts + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Snapshots[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packageSnapshots + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Shell --> ProjectSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Surface --> ProcessSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Review --> ReviewSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageCtx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Model Split\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe core architectural point of this epic is the model split below:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Concern | Durable Home | Meaning |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------------|---------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project artifact identity | `artifacts` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This artifact exists in this project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Revision provenance | `artifactVersions.createdByProcessId` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This version was produced by this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current process working set | per-type process state `currentArtifactIds` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process is currently working with these artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package-building pins | `processPackageContexts` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process has explicitly pinned these artifact versions into current publication work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable published package | `packageSnapshots` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This exact ordered set of pinned versions was published as one package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe rest of the design is a consequence of keeping those layers distinct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture Overview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is a four-surface change set: shared contracts, server services and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nroutes, browser read/render behavior, and Convex durable state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/shared/contracts/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas.ts                               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── review-workspace.ts                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── process-work-surface.ts                  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── state.ts                                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/server/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app.ts                                     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── routes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── services/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   ├── platform-store.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── readers/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │       └── artifact-section.reader.ts   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── process-work-surface.service.ts  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-workspace.service.ts      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── artifact-review.service.ts       # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── package-review.service.ts        # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-context.service.ts        # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       └── package-context.service.ts       # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/client/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── bootstrap.ts                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── store.ts                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── browser-api/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review-workspace-api.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── features/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── artifact-section.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── process-work-surface-page.ts     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── review-workspace-page.ts         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── artifact-review-panel.ts         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── package-review-panel.ts          # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── version-switcher.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        └── package-member-nav.ts            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconvex/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schema.ts                                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── artifacts.ts                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshots.ts                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshotMembers.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── processPackageContexts.ts                # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── processPackageContextMembers.ts          # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | ACs Covered |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `schemas.ts` | MODIFIED | Slim project artifact summary; add request-error codes for aligned review/package failures | AC-1, AC-2, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-workspace.ts` | MODIFIED | Expand review-target error taxonomy and selected-target semantics for zero-version, unavailable-version, and unavailable-member cases | AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `app.ts` + `server/schemas/review.ts` | MODIFIED | Wire new review/package-context services into the running app and tighten route-level request/response contracts for exact review/package error codes | AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `platform-store.ts` | MODIFIED | Expose aligned durable-state primitives; remove review composition from the store boundary; add package-context reads/writes | AC-1 through AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-context.service.ts` | NEW | Compute process-scoped review context from current refs, package context, and package snapshots | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-context.service.ts` | NEW | Maintain the current mutable package-building context and validate publish eligibility against it | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve artifact review through process context rather than artifact-row ownership | AC-2, AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve mixed-producer package members and degrade per member rather than per package | AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-section.ts` | MODIFIED | Render project artifact summaries without ownership language | AC-1, AC-2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `process-work-surface.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Gate review enablement on aligned review context instead of same-process production heuristics | AC-3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `bootstrap.ts` + `review-workspace-api.ts` | MODIFIED | Handle follow-up 404/409 responses by reloading bounded workspace state rather than collapsing the whole workspace | AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `processPackageContexts.ts` + members | NEW | Durable current package-building context for publish/reopen flows | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Work Breakdown\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 maps cleanly to the story breakdown already proposed in the epic. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwork should still be implemented vertically, but the design keeps one clear\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfoundation chunk up front so later chunks do not have to keep rediscovering the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsame contract and migration decisions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Planned Tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|-------|-----|---------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 0 | Foundations: contract changes, store narrowing, fixture refresh, migration helpers, package-context tables | — | 12 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 1 | Project artifact association without process ownership | AC-1.1 to AC-1.3 | 18 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 2 | Versioned checkpoint realignment and latest-version summary reads | AC-2.1 to AC-2.3 | 22 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 3 | Process-scoped artifact review realignment, including zero-version behavior | AC-3.1 to AC-3.4 | 27 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 4 | Cross-process package alignment and current package-building context | AC-4.1 to AC-4.4 | 23 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 5 | Reopen, unavailable, and degraded provenance states plus classification | AC-5.1 to AC-5.4 | 19 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Planned total:** 121 tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 0: Foundations\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk exists to make the rest of the epic legible. The wrong way to do\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 would be to mix schema removal, package-context introduction, review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npolicy changes, and UI copy adjustments into the first feature slice that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhappens to need them. Chunk 0 instead lands the shared contracts, the store\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboundary changes, the new package-context tables, and the migration scaffolding\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat later chunks rely on.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nKey deliverables:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- slimmer `ArtifactSummary` contract\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- aligned review error codes and request codes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- new package-context tables and store methods\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- review-context helper/service skeleton\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- fixture and contract-test refresh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 1: Project Artifact Association Without Process Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk removes ownership language from project-shell artifact state and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nensures a process can reference an existing project artifact without changing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity. The project shell and the process work surface both change\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhere, but only at the read-model level: the durable current-ref model remains\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbounded and process-owned, while project artifact identity becomes cleanly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nproject-owned.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 2: Versioned Checkpoint Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk rewrites the durable write path. Checkpointing a later revision of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nan existing project artifact must create a new version while leaving the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity untouched. The chunk also settles the project-shell and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-materials read-path rule for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"current version label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"updated at\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboth are derived from the latest artifact version, not from artifact-row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nownership metadata.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 3: Process-Scoped Artifact Review Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk is where the user first feels the alignment. A process can review an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:25:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_11\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '501,650p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifact it currently references even if another process created or last revised\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nit. Zero-version artifacts stay out of the default target list, but direct\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview paths become consistent and bounded. This chunk also removes the last\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact-row ownership check from artifact review resolution.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 4: Cross-Process Package Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk introduces the durable current package-building context and relaxes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage publish rules to the ones the epic actually wants: same project, valid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncurrent process context, and explicit version pinning. Cross-process members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbecome normal as long as they are in context. Package snapshots remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimmutable; the mutable context exists so the process can reopen, edit, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrepublish without losing bounded eligibility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk is mostly about resilience and classification. It ensures the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworkspace and package views degrade accurately when a reference disappears, a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nspecific version is missing, or one package member is unavailable later while\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nother members remain healthy. It also tightens observability so downstream\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndebugging can tell the difference between target lookup failures, version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfailures, and member failures.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Verification Scripts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 uses the repo's existing verification tiers without adding a new lane.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo new dependency package means no new package-build lane is required beyond\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwhat the repo already has.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Script | Command | Purpose |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|---------|---------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `red-verify` | `corepack pnpm run red-verify` | Format, lint, typecheck, and build without tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify` | `corepack pnpm run verify` | Standard dev gate |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `green-verify` | `corepack pnpm run green-verify` | Standard gate plus test-immutability guard |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify-all` | `corepack pnpm run verify-all` | Deep gate including integration and scaffolded e2e |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Open Questions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese do not block drafting, but they should remain visible while stories are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbeing prepared:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| # | Question | Owner | Blocks | Resolution |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|----------|-------|--------|------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Q1 | Future extension only: after Epic 5 ships with one current mutable package-building context per process, should a later epic support more than one draft context per process? | Platform TL | Later process-module enrichment only; does not block Epic 5 implementation | Pending |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Deferred Items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Item | Related AC | Reason Deferred | Future Work |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|-----------|-----------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Multi-draft package editing for one process | AC-4 | Epic 5 only needs one current package-building context per process to support bounded eligibility and reopen flows | Later process-module/package-authoring epic |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Cross-project artifact reuse | Out of scope | Epic 5 explicitly keeps artifacts scoped to one project | Later artifact-sharing epic, if ever needed |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Historical process-to-artifact relation browser | AC-1, AC-5 | Epic 5 keeps bounded current refs and package contexts, not a full historical relation product surface | Later archive/provenance UI work |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Full onboarding-pack refresh for review/package current state | — | This design records the drift but does not own the documentation refresh project | Docs refresh after Epic 5 lands |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Related Documentation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Epic: `epic.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Companion docs: `tech-design-server.md`, `tech-design-client.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Test plan: `test-plan.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Platform architecture: `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-arch.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Platform PRD: `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:27:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_12\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design-client.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Technical Design: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment — Client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis companion document covers the browser-facing changes for Epic 5: project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshell artifact summaries, process-surface review entry behavior, review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworkspace route/state handling, version/member selection, and bounded degraded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrendering. The server and durable model do most of the heavy lifting in this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nepic, but the client still has to present the aligned model clearly and avoid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncollapsing back into the old ownership language.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Client Posture\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 does not add a new browser surface. The user still encounters artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nin three places:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- the project shell's artifact summary section\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- the process work surface's current materials section\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- the dedicated review workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhat changes is the meaning those surfaces express.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe project shell stops talking like artifacts belong to one process. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess work surface keeps showing process-local current meaning because that is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstill process-owned. The review workspace becomes more careful about stale\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nselections: a missing explicit version or package member should not blow away\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe whole workspace state when the server can still explain the degradation in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbounded terms.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe client therefore has two jobs in Epic 5. First, it needs to present the new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmodel cleanly. Second, it needs to preserve orientation when the server says\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"this target still exists, but your selected version/member is stale\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" instead\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nof flattening that into one generic load failure.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Top-Tier Surface Nesting\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Epic 5 Client Nesting |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project shell | Slimmer artifact summary contract and copy; no artifact-level process ownership language |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process work surface | Review action visibility continues to live here, but the client trusts server-provided reviewability rather than inferring from artifact provenance |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review workspace | Existing route/state/panel surface; selection and degraded-state handling become more precise |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared client state | `reviewWorkspace` remains a dedicated state slice; target-level degradation stays inside `target`, while fatal load failures remain in `error` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/client/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── bootstrap.ts                              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── router.ts                                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── store.ts                                  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── browser-api/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review-workspace-api.ts                   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── features/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── artifact-section.ts                   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── process-work-surface-page.ts          # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── review-workspace-page.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── artifact-review-panel.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── package-review-panel.ts               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── version-switcher.ts                   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        └── package-member-nav.ts                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/shared/contracts/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas.ts                                    # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── review-workspace.ts                           # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── state.ts                                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | ACs Covered |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-section.ts` | MODIFIED | Render project artifact summaries as project-scoped durable assets only | AC-1, AC-2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `process-work-surface-page.ts` | MODIFIED | Present review entry only when server says review is meaningful for the current process context | AC-3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `bootstrap.ts` | MODIFIED | Keep review workspace state coherent across bootstrap, version/member selection, and fallback reloads | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-workspace-api.ts` | MODIFIED | Map new request-error codes and support non-destructive fallback when explicit selection reads fail | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-workspace-page.ts` | MODIFIED | Distinguish workspace load failures from bounded target degradation and preserve target selection affordances | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-review-panel.ts` | MODIFIED | Render zero-version empty state, unavailable-version messaging, and earlier-version selection with clearer provenance language | AC-2, AC-3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-review-panel.ts` | MODIFIED | Render mixed-producer package members without ownership assumptions and keep unrelated members visible when one degrades | AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `version-switcher.ts` | MODIFIED | Keep explicit version selection process-aware and recover gracefully when the selected version disappears later | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-member-nav.ts` | MODIFIED | Preserve selection and accessibility semantics while surfacing unavailable members accurately | AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Contract and State Changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 changes the browser model in two places: the project-shell artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsummary contract and the review workspace's distinction between fatal load\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfailure and bounded target degradation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 1. Project Artifact Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`artifactSummarySchema` becomes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport const artifactSummarySchema = z.object({\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactId: z.string().min(1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: z.string().min(1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  currentVersionLabel: z.string().min(1).nullable(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  updatedAt: z.string().min(1),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n});\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe client removes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `attachmentScope`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe project-shell artifact section therefore stops rendering text like\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Attached to Feature Specification #2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and instead renders only artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nidentity plus current-version projection.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 2. Review Workspace State\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe state split remains:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `reviewWorkspace.error` for fatal request-level failure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `reviewWorkspace.target` for bounded target-level state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhat changes is how the client gets there.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`reviewWorkspace.error` is reserved for:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unauthenticated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- forbidden\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project not found\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- process not found\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- server unavailable / generic request failure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`reviewWorkspace.target` now carries the nuanced states for:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- valid zero-version artifact (`status: empty`)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unavailable target in current workspace context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unsupported or unavailable selected package member inside a valid package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- stale explicit version/member selection after a fallback workspace reload\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis keeps the review workspace page from short-circuiting into a generic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"failed to load\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" section when the server can still tell the user what happened.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 1: Project Shell Artifact Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5's first visible client change is small but important. The project shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmust stop implying that one process owns one artifact.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Shell as Project Shell Page\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant API as GET /api/projects/:projectId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Shell,API: AC-1.2 / AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Shell->>API: load project shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    API-->>Shell: artifact summaries { artifactId, displayName, currentVersionLabel, updatedAt }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Shell->>Shell: render project-scoped summary rows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe old artifact section rendered a scope paragraph:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `Project-scoped artifact.`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `Attached to {processDisplayLabel}.`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 removes that paragraph entirely. The updated row has:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current version label or \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"No current version available.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- updated timestamp\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat copy change matters because the project shell is where users form their\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmental model of what artifacts are.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 2: Process Work Surface Review Entry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe process work surface already has a review affordance. Epic 5 changes what\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat affordance means.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Surface as Process Work Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant API as GET /api/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Review as Review Workspace Route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Surface,Review: AC-3.1 / AC-3.2 / AC-3.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Surface->>API: load process work surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    API-->>Surface: process summary + controls.review.enabled\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    alt review enabled\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Surface->>Review: navigate to /review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    else review disabled\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Surface-->>Surface: render disabled control / omit action\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe client no longer needs to infer reviewability from artifact provenance or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage composition. It trusts the server's aligned review-context decision.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat is healthier for this epic because the rules now depend on several layers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe client should not try to recompute:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current package-building context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- published package snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version exclusion\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- degraded snapshot member states\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 3: Review Workspace Bootstrap\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nBootstrap remains the authoritative read for the review workspace. Epic 5\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nleans into that instead of trying to make every panel-level action own all\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndegradation semantics itself.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Router as bootstrap.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant API as review-workspace-api.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Page as review-workspace-page.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Router,Page: AC-3.1 / AC-3.4 / AC-5.1 / AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Router->>API: getReviewWorkspace(projectId, processId, selection)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    API-->>Router: workspace envelope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Router->>Router: patch reviewWorkspace state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Router->>Page: render workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe bootstrap outcomes are now intentionally distinct:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Outcome | Client State |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| process context missing or forbidden | `reviewWorkspace.error` populated; `project` / `process` absent or null |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| no targets for this process | `availableTargets: []`, `target: null`, `error: null` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| multi-target workspace awaiting selection | `availableTargets.length > 1`, `target: null`, `error: null` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| zero-version direct artifact path | `target.status === 'empty'` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| fully degraded published package target | `target.targetKind === 'package'`, package visible, selected member may be unavailable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| stale explicit selection after reopen | `target.status === 'unavailable'` with exact error code |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis lets the page render targeted copy rather than one generic failure block.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 4: Explicit Version Selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nVersion selection is where stale-state handling becomes visible.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Switcher as version-switcher.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Router as bootstrap.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant API as review-workspace-api.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Switcher,API: AC-3.3 / AC-3.4 / AC-5.3 / AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Switcher->>Router: onSelectVersion(versionId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Router->>API: GET /review/artifacts/:artifactId?versionId=...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    alt 200\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: artifact target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>Router: patch target + selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    else 404 ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: request error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>API: reload workspace bootstrap with same selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: 200 workspace with bounded unavailable target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>Router: patch workspace state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe fallback reload is the key client decision in this epic. It preserves the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworkspace shell, the target list, and the process context even when a selected\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexplicit version disappears later. Without this step, the client would have to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nflatten a nuanced stale-selection case into a generic request failure.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Version Switcher Behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`version-switcher.ts` keeps the same interaction model:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- click selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- keyboard listbox navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- explicit selected/current labels\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhat changes is the result handling:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- successful explicit selection patches the artifact target directly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- failed explicit selection triggers bootstrap reload\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version artifacts still render without a version switcher because\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `versions.length === 0`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 5: Package Member Selection and Degraded Members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nPackage review is where Epic 5 most clearly benefits from the aligned model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Nav as package-member-nav.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Router as bootstrap.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant API as review-workspace-api.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Panel as package-review-panel.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Nav,Panel: AC-4.3 / AC-4.4 / AC-5.2 / AC-5.3 / AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Nav->>Router: onSelectMember(memberId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Router->>API: GET /review/packages/:packageId?memberId=...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    alt 200\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: package target with selected member\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>Panel: render member state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    else 404 PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: request error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>API: reload workspace bootstrap with same selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        API-->>Router: workspace with bounded degraded selected member\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Router->>Panel: render unavailable selected member and remaining members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe important client rule is that one unavailable member should not collapse\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe package panel. The navigation list remains visible, other members remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nvisible, and the selected-member subpanel carries the unavailable state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Package Member Navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`package-member-nav.ts` already supports:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- listbox keyboard navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ready` / `unsupported` / `unavailable` labels\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- disabled selection for non-ready members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 keeps that structure but updates the copy and expectations:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- a member may be unavailable even though the package itself is still valid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- an unavailable member is a provenance/degradation state, not evidence the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  package is out of process scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- mixed-producer packages are normal, not exceptional\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Panel Rendering Changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `artifact-review-panel.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis panel already renders:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact identity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current version label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selected version label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- version switcher\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unsupported fallback\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- markdown body\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 changes the messaging more than the structure:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version empty state becomes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This artifact exists in the current process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  context but has no durable version yet.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unavailable-version messaging should mention version availability, not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process mismatch\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current version label remains a projection of the latest durable version, not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  an ownership marker\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selected-version metadata and version-switcher rows expose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `producedByProcessId` and `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` so the producing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process remains visible in the review experience\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `package-review-panel.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis panel already renders:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package identity and type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- member nav\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selected-member subpanel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- export trigger\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 keeps that structure and changes the interpretation:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selected member failure messages refer to unavailable pinned versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- mixed-producer members are normal\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- fully degraded published packages still remain targetable so reopen and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  provenance inspection remain possible\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- exportability is still server-driven, but the client copy should explain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"One or more pinned members are unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" rather than \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This package is not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  owned by the current process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Interface Definitions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Project Artifact Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ArtifactSummary {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  currentVersionLabel: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  updatedAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Review Version Contracts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ArtifactVersionSummary {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  isCurrent: boolean;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessDisplayLabel: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ArtifactVersionDetail extends ArtifactVersionSummary {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  contentKind: 'markdown' | 'unsupported';\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bodyStatus?: 'ready' | 'error';\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  body?: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bodyError?: ReviewTargetError;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  mermaidBlocks?: MermaidBlock[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nClient consequences:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `version-switcher.ts` can keep its current listbox structure, but its row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  metadata should include the producing process display label, falling back to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  the id only when no display label can be resolved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifact-review-panel.ts` should render the selected version's producing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process display label beside the created timestamp, again falling back to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  id only when needed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package-member artifact panels inherit that same provenance field through the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  pinned artifact review payload\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Review Workspace Fallback Handling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntype ReviewSelectionFailureCode =\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | 'ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | 'PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | 'REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND';\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntype ReviewSelectionRecovery = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  shouldReloadWorkspace: boolean;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  code: ReviewSelectionFailureCode;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nClient rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- if a target-specific read fails with one of the codes above, reload the full\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  workspace with the same route selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- if bootstrap fails with auth/access/process errors, set `reviewWorkspace.error`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  and do not attempt bounded target rendering\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Review Workspace State Contract\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo new top-level state slice is required. The important contract invariant is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreviewWorkspace.error !== null\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmeans \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the workspace failed as a request,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" while:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreviewWorkspace.error === null && reviewWorkspace.target?.status === 'unavailable'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmeans \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"the workspace is readable, but the selected target degraded.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat distinction is the client-side backbone of AC-5.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Accessibility and UX Notes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is not a styling epic, but it does tighten the semantics of existing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ninteractive controls:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- target selector remains a listbox and should keep keyboard navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- version switcher remains a listbox and should keep current/selected labels\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package member nav remains a listbox with `aria-disabled` on non-ready members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- stale explicit selections should preserve focus and heading context after the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bootstrap fallback, rather than dumping the user onto a generic error screen\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe product goal is orientation. Users should be able to tell whether they are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlooking at:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- a valid empty artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- a missing explicit version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- a degraded package member\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- a process/workspace access failure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwithout reconstructing the difference from a generic error banner.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:29:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_13\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design-server.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Technical Design: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment — Server\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis companion document covers the Fastify control plane, review/package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nservices, `PlatformStore` boundary, Convex durable-state changes, and the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmigration path for Epic 5. It expands the index into exact server-side module\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboundaries, table decisions, flow design, and interface targets.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Server Posture\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 does not create a second backend or a new top-level service. The server\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstill looks like one Fastify-owned control plane sitting over Convex durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstate and a browser client that talks only to Fastify. What changes is where\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe alignment logic lives.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nToday, some of that logic is trapped in the wrong layer. `PlatformStore`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimplements high-level review target listing and package review assembly, which\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmeans the pre-alignment same-process assumptions are duplicated in the Convex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstore implementation and the in-memory test store. Epic 5 fixes that by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npulling review-context composition back up into review-owned services and pure\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhelpers while keeping `PlatformStore` focused on durable reads and writes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat server posture is important because Epic 5 is fundamentally a policy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchange. The durable store should answer factual questions:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- what artifact identities exist in this project?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- what versions exist for this artifact?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- what artifacts does this process currently reference?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- what package context does this process currently hold?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- what package snapshots and members exist?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe review/package services should answer semantic questions:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- which artifacts are reviewable from this process context?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- which versions are allowed for publication from this process context?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- which failures are missing-target failures versus missing-version failures?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat separation keeps the durable boundary reusable and keeps the semantic rules\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclose to the server surface that exposes them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Top-Tier Surface Nesting\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Epic 5 Server Nesting |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Projects | `artifact-section.reader.ts` and project-shell summary builders drop artifact-level process ownership language |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Processes | `process-work-surface.service.ts` keeps process current refs as the bounded working-set model and asks review services whether review is currently meaningful |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Artifacts | `convex/artifacts.ts` trims the artifact row to project-scoped identity only; version provenance stays in `artifactVersions` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review Workspace | `routes/review.ts` plus review services remain the owner of review/bootstrap/target semantics |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Packages | Immutable `packageSnapshots` stay durable publication records; new mutable package-context tables support aligned publication eligibility and reopen flows |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/server/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app.ts                                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── routes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                                     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                                     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── services/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   ├── platform-store.ts                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── readers/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │       └── artifact-section.reader.ts            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── process-work-surface.service.ts           # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-workspace.service.ts               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── artifact-review.service.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── package-review.service.ts                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-context.service.ts                 # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-context.ts                         # NEW pure helpers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── package-context.service.ts                # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       └── package-context.ts                        # NEW pure helpers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/shared/contracts/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas.ts                                        # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── review-workspace.ts                               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── process-work-surface.ts                           # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconvex/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schema.ts                                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── artifacts.ts                                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshots.ts                               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshotMembers.ts                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── processPackageContexts.ts                         # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── processPackageContextMembers.ts                   # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | Dependencies | ACs Covered |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|--------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `app.ts` | MODIFIED | Wire `ReviewContextService` and `PackageContextService` into `DefaultArtifactReviewService`, `DefaultPackageReviewService`, `DefaultReviewWorkspaceService`, and process-surface review enablement; register any new review/package context dependencies at app composition time | Fastify service composition | AC-3 through AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `schemas/review.ts` | MODIFIED | Expand route schemas and response-code expectations so target-specific review endpoints can return exact 404 codes for missing explicit versions and unavailable package members | shared contracts | AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `routes/review.ts` | MODIFIED | Map review lookup result kinds and `AppError` subclasses to exact request codes instead of collapsing all null reads into `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` | review services, route schemas | AC-3 through AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `platform-store.ts` | MODIFIED | Provide low-level durable reads and writes for artifacts, versions, current refs, package contexts, and package snapshots; stop owning review-context composition | Convex queries/mutations, shared contracts | AC-1 through AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-context.service.ts` | NEW | Resolve aligned process review context from current refs, package context, and published package snapshots; answer `availableTargets`, direct-access eligibility, and review enablement | `PlatformStore`, pure helpers | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-context.service.ts` | NEW | Upsert current package-building context, seed from published snapshot on reopen, and validate publish eligibility against same-project + in-context rules | `PlatformStore`, pure helpers | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve artifact review through process review context, not artifact ownership; return zero-version empty state and exact missing-version failures | `PlatformStore`, `review-context.service.ts`, renderer | AC-2, AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve mixed-producer package members, keep unrelated members readable when one degrades, and classify selected-member failures accurately | `PlatformStore`, `review-context.service.ts`, `artifact-review.service.ts` | AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-workspace.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Bootstrap the review workspace from aligned review context and keep bounded degraded target states inside the workspace envelope | process access, review services | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-section.reader.ts` | MODIFIED | Build project artifact summaries from project identity + latest version projection only | `PlatformStore` | AC-1, AC-2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `process-work-surface.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Compute review control enablement from aligned review context instead of same-process production shortcuts | `review-context.service.ts` | AC-3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/artifacts.ts` | MODIFIED | Remove artifact-row process ownership and keep checkpoint writes version-oriented | `artifactVersions`, process refs | AC-1, AC-2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/processPackageContexts.ts` + members | NEW | Durable current package-building context with ordered pinned version members | Convex schema | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/packageSnapshots.ts` | MODIFIED | Publish immutable snapshots from aligned same-project/current-context rules instead of same-process-producer rules | Convex schema, package context validation | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Durable State Model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe durable model after Epic 5 has five artifact/package layers:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. project artifact identity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. artifact versions with provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. per-process current artifact refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. per-process current package-building context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. immutable published package snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 1. `artifacts`: Project Identity Only\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCurrent state still stores `processId` on the artifact row. Epic 5 removes it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Table Shape\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport const artifactsTableFields = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  projectId: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdAt: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Why\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe artifact row should answer only one question: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what durable artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nidentity exists in this project?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" If the row also says \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"this belongs to process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nX,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" the rest of the system will keep trying to use that shortcut for review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\neligibility, package publication, and process lineage. Epic 5 removes the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshortcut rather than trying to teach every caller not to use it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 2. `artifactVersions`: Keep Provenance Here\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`artifactVersions` remains the version-provenance table. Epic 5 does not change\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nits core purpose; it leans harder on it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Table Shape\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport const artifactVersionsTableFields = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactId: v.id('artifacts'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  contentStorageId: v.id('_storage'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  contentKind: v.union(v.literal('markdown'), v.literal('unsupported')),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bytes: v.number(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdAt: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdByProcessId: v.id('processes'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Required Reads\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese reads become first-class rather than incidental:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- latest version by artifact id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- all versions by artifact id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- version by version id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- all artifacts ever produced by process id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 keeps the existing indexes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `by_artifactId_createdAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `by_createdByProcessId_createdAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 3. Per-Type Current Refs: Keep the Bounded Working Set\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 keeps the existing per-type state rows:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processProductDefinitionStates.currentArtifactIds`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processFeatureSpecificationStates.currentArtifactIds`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processFeatureImplementationStates.currentArtifactIds`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese remain the canonical answer to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what artifacts are current for this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" They do not become historical membership and they do not become the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage pinning mechanism.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 4. New Mutable Package Context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 adds two new tables.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### `processPackageContexts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport const processPackageContextsTableFields = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  processId: v.id('processes'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageType: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  basePackageSnapshotId: v.union(v.id('packageSnapshots'), v.null()),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  updatedAt: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIndexes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `by_processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis table stores the header row for one process's current package-building\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontext. The design assumes one current mutable context per process for this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nepic. That is enough to support draft/edit/reopen behavior without committing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe platform to multi-draft package management yet.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Uniqueness, Cleanup, and Idempotency\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConvex indexes do not enforce uniqueness, so Epic 5 defines the enforcement\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrule in the write path, not in the index declaration.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`upsertCurrentProcessPackageContext` runs as one mutation and must:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. query all `processPackageContexts` rows for `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. choose one canonical row:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - latest `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - tie-breaker: lowest `_id`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. delete any duplicate header rows and their member rows in the same mutation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. replace the canonical row's header fields with the requested payload\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. replace that row's members wholesale with the normalized requested member set\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIdempotency rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- if the normalized requested payload matches the current canonical row and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  member set exactly, the mutation returns the existing row without creating a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  new one\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- otherwise it performs a last-write-wins replacement of the canonical row and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  member set\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConcurrency rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- because the entire operation runs in one Convex mutation, optimistic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  concurrency retries converge on one surviving canonical row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- duplicate cleanup is therefore part of every upsert, not a one-time repair job\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### `processPackageContextMembers`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport const processPackageContextMembersTableFields = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageContextId: v.id('processPackageContexts'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  position: v.number(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactId: v.id('artifacts'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactVersionId: v.id('artifactVersions'),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  pinnedAt: v.string(),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIndexes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `by_packageContextId_position`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis table stores the explicit pinned version members for the current context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe denormalized `displayName` and `versionLabel` fields are intentional; they\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlet reopened or degraded contexts remain legible even if one of the downstream\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrows later becomes unavailable.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 5. Immutable Published Package Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`packageSnapshots` and `packageSnapshotMembers` remain immutable after write.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 changes the publication rule, not the snapshot concept.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Current Rule to Remove\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current implementation rejects any member whose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`artifactVersion.createdByProcessId !== publishingProcessId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat rule is deleted.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### New Publication Rule\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nA requested package member is allowed only when:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. the artifact belongs to the same project as the publishing process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. the version belongs to that artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. the version is either:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - the current version of a currently referenced artifact for that process, or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   - an explicit version already pinned in that same process's current package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n     context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis rule is validated in the server package-context/publish path and mirrored\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nin the in-memory store used by tests.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Store Boundary Changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 narrows `PlatformStore`. The store boundary should return durable facts,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot precomposed review semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Remove or Retire High-Level Review Composition\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe following methods are retired from the long-term interface:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProcessReviewTargets`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getProcessReviewPackage`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThey currently embed the wrong policy. Epic 5 moves that logic into\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`review-context.service.ts` and `package-review.service.ts`, which can use\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstore primitives and keep the policy local to the review/package surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDuring Chunk 0 and early Chunk 1 work, those methods may remain as deprecated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncompile-bridge adapters while callers are being moved. They are not part of the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nend-state interface for this epic and should be removed before Epic 5 closes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Add Low-Level Package-Context Primitives\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport type ProcessPackageContextRecord = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageContextId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageType: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  basePackageSnapshotId: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  updatedAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport type ProcessPackageContextMemberRecord = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  memberId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageContextId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  position: number;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactVersionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  pinnedAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ninterface PlatformStore {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  listProjectArtifactsByIds(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    projectId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    artifactIds: string[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<ArtifactSummary[]>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  listProcessesByIds(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processIds: string[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<Array<ProcessSummary & { projectId: string }>>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  getCurrentProcessPackageContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<ProcessPackageContextRecord | null>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  listProcessPackageContextMembers(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    packageContextId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<ProcessPackageContextMemberRecord[]>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  upsertCurrentProcessPackageContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    packageType: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    basePackageSnapshotId: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: Array<{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      position: number;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      artifactId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      artifactVersionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      versionLabel: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    }>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    context: ProcessPackageContextRecord;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: ProcessPackageContextMemberRecord[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  clearCurrentProcessPackageContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<void>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Keep Existing Durable Facts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese existing methods remain central:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProjectArtifacts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProjectArtifactsByIds`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listArtifactVersions`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getArtifactVersion`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getLatestArtifactVersion`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getProcessRecord`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProcessesByIds`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getCurrentProcessMaterialRefs`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listPackageSnapshotsForProcess`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `getPackageSnapshot`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listPackageSnapshotMembers`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `publishPackageSnapshot`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`publishPackageSnapshot` stays, but its validation rule changes and it becomes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontext-aware rather than same-process-producer aware.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nWhy these additions matter:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProjectArtifactsByIds` keeps review-context and package-context services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  from reimplementing ad hoc artifact/project joins\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `listProcessesByIds` is the primitive that lets review services derive\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` without turning `PlatformStore` back into a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  high-level review assembler\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Migration Plan\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 uses a direct pre-customer breaking rollout, not a widen-migrate-narrow\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nproduction migration. That choice is intentional. The repo is still pre-customer,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe current artifact-row ownership field is architecturally wrong, and trying\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nto preserve half-aligned intermediate schemas would add more implementation risk\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthan it removes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNormative rollout rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- ship the new code and schema together\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- reset any dev/local deployment whose legacy rows fail schema validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- treat any one-off helper that preserves local dev data as optional developer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  convenience, not as Epic 5 scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is therefore best delivered in three server-side phases.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Phase A: Additive Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nLand the new package-context tables, store methods, review-context service, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshared helper functions while the old artifact-row `processId` still exists.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis keeps the server buildable and lets tests move one seam at a time.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Phase B: Policy Flip\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nChange review eligibility, package publication validation, and process-surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview enablement to use current refs + package context + package snapshots.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOnce these reads no longer depend on `artifacts.processId`, the old field is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nonly dead weight.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Phase C: Artifact Row Cleanup\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRemove `processId` from the `artifacts` table and from all summary builders,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshared contracts, fixtures, and tests. The expected path is a direct breaking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndeploy with a dev deployment reset where legacy rows block schema validation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAn optional local helper may exist for developer convenience, but it is not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrequired for Epic 5 completion and is not part of the core story scope.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 1: Referencing Project Artifacts From Process Work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis flow is the durable-state baseline for AC-1. A later process should be\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nable to bring an existing project artifact into its current materials without\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchanging the artifact identity or claiming sole ownership of it.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant ProcessSvc as Process Work Service\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Store as PlatformStore\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Convex as Convex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over ProcessSvc,Convex: AC-1.1 / AC-1.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc->>Store: setCurrentProcessMaterialRefs(processId, artifactIds)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: validate artifacts belong to same project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: write currentArtifactIds on per-type process state row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store-->>ProcessSvc: current refs updated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc-->>ProcessSvc: materials panel projects artifacts by current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nServer consequences:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- no artifact row update when a process merely references an existing artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project-shell artifact listing stays deduplicated because artifact identity is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  still one project-level row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- process materials panel is the place where per-process visibility appears\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 2: Creating a New Version Without Reassigning the Artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis flow is the write-path center of AC-2.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant EnvSvc as Process Environment Service\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Store as PlatformStore\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Convex as Convex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over EnvSvc,Convex: AC-2.1 / AC-2.2 / AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    EnvSvc->>Store: persistCheckpointArtifacts(processId, artifacts[])\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: upsert artifact identity by project + artifactId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: insert new artifactVersions row with createdByProcessId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: update currentArtifactIds if artifact enters current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store-->>EnvSvc: latest output refs + linked artifact ids\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe crucial server change is in `convex/artifacts.ts`:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- if `artifactId` is supplied and belongs to the same project, keep the same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifact row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- update `displayName` only if the checkpoint target label changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- never patch a process ownership field because none exists anymore\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- always insert a new `artifactVersions` row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nProject and process summary reads then derive `currentVersionLabel` and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`updatedAt` from `getLatestArtifactVersion(artifactId)`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 3: Review Context Resolution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis flow is the semantic heart of AC-3 and part of AC-5.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Route as review.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:31:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_14\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '501,900p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design-server.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"    participant Workspace as ReviewWorkspaceService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Context as ReviewContextService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Store as PlatformStore\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Route,Store: AC-3.1 / AC-3.2 / AC-3.4 / AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Route->>Workspace: getWorkspace(projectId, processId, selection)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Workspace->>Context: resolveProcessReviewContext(projectId, processId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Context->>Store: getCurrentProcessMaterialRefs(processId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Context->>Store: getCurrentProcessPackageContext(processId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Context->>Store: listPackageSnapshotsForProcess(processId)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Context->>Store: listPackageSnapshotMembers(snapshotIds)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Context-->>Workspace: available targets + direct access sets\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Workspace->>Workspace: select target or bounded unavailable state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Workspace-->>Route: review workspace envelope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe alignment rule is implemented here, not in the store:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs create artifact targets\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- explicitly pinned artifact versions in the current package context also create\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifact targets for their artifact identities, even if that artifact is no\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  longer in today's current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version refs do not create default artifact targets\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package snapshots create package targets, including fully degraded snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  whose members may all be unavailable later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- direct artifact access is allowed through current refs or pinned package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unrelated project artifacts stay unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 4: Current Package Context and Publication\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis flow settles AC-4.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant ProcessModule as Downstream Process Module\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant PkgCtx as PackageContextService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Store as PlatformStore\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Convex as Convex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over ProcessModule,Convex: AC-4.1 / AC-4.2 / AC-4.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessModule->>PkgCtx: upsertCurrentContext(processId, members)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PkgCtx->>Store: validate same project + version ownership-to-artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PkgCtx->>Store: write processPackageContext + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessModule->>PkgCtx: publishSnapshot(processId, requestedMembers)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PkgCtx->>Store: load current refs + current package context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PkgCtx->>PkgCtx: validate package-building context rule\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PkgCtx->>Store: publishPackageSnapshot(processId, requestedMembers)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Store->>Convex: insert packageSnapshots + packageSnapshotMembers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTwo details matter here:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. A requested upstream version is allowed even when another process produced\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   it, as long as it is the current version of a currently referenced artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   or already pinned in the same process's current package context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. An earlier pinned version stays eligible across reopen/edit flows because it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   lives in `processPackageContextMembers`, not only in ephemeral client state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Flow 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis flow settles AC-5 and the boundary between bounded target degradation and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrequest-level failure.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsequenceDiagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Client as Client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Route as review.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Workspace as ReviewWorkspaceService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Artifact as ArtifactReviewService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    participant Package as PackageReviewService\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Note over Client,Package: AC-5.1 / AC-5.2 / AC-5.3 / AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Client->>Route: GET /review?targetKind=...&targetId=...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Route->>Workspace: bootstrap workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Workspace-->>Client: 200 workspace with ready/empty/unavailable target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Client->>Route: GET /review/artifacts/:id?versionId=...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    alt explicit version missing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Route-->>Client: 404 ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    else valid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Route-->>Client: 200 artifact target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Client->>Route: GET /review/packages/:id?memberId=...\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    alt explicit selected member unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Route-->>Client: 404 PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    else valid package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Route-->>Client: 200 package target with degraded member states as needed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe core server rule is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- bootstrap favors bounded degraded states\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- explicit follow-up reads favor exact request-level error codes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat gives the UI a durable workspace shell while still keeping machine-readable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfailure classification precise.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Interface Definitions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Shared Contract Shapes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 needs more exact shared-contract guidance than the first draft provided.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe provenance rules are only implementable if they land in the actual shared\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nschemas, not just in prose.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ArtifactVersionSummary {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  isCurrent: boolean;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessDisplayLabel: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ArtifactVersionDetail {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  versionLabel: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  contentKind: 'markdown' | 'unsupported';\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bodyStatus?: 'ready' | 'error';\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  body?: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  bodyError?: ReviewTargetError;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  mermaidBlocks?: MermaidBlock[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  createdAt: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  producedByProcessDisplayLabel: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nContract consequences:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `apps/platform/shared/contracts/review-workspace.ts` adds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `producedByProcessId` to both `artifactVersionSummarySchema` and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `artifactVersionDetailSchema`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- the shared contracts also add `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` as an optional\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  derived provenance field for user-facing review surfaces\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact review responses and package-member artifact projections must carry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  that field through without dropping it during pinned-version adaptation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- the client panels are expected to render or expose that provenance clearly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  enough that AC-2.2 remains visible in the product rather than only in logs or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  raw payloads\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- naming rule: durable rows keep `createdByProcessId`; browser-facing contracts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  expose the same fact as `producedByProcessId` plus\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `producedByProcessDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Review Context Shapes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport type ProcessReviewContext = {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  currentArtifactIds: string[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactIdsReachableForDirectReview: Set<string>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  artifactIdsVisibleInTargetList: Set<string>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  packageSnapshotIdsVisibleInTargetList: Set<string>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  zeroVersionArtifactIds: Set<string>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n};\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface ReviewContextService {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  resolveProcessReviewContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    projectId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<ProcessReviewContext>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  listAvailableTargets(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    projectId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<ReviewTargetSummary[]>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  canReviewArtifact(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    projectId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    artifactId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<boolean>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  hasAnyVisibleTargets(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    projectId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<boolean>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Package Context Shapes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport interface PackageContextService {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  getCurrentContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    context: ProcessPackageContextRecord | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: ProcessPackageContextMemberRecord[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  upsertCurrentContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    packageType: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    basePackageSnapshotId: string | null;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: Array<{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      position: number;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      artifactId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n      artifactVersionId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    }>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    context: ProcessPackageContextRecord;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: ProcessPackageContextMemberRecord[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  publishFromCurrentContext(args: {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    processId: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    displayName: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    packageType: string;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    members: PackageSnapshotMemberWriteInput[];\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  }): Promise<string>;\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Review Lookup Result Types\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe first draft named the right error codes but did not make the mechanics\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimplementable enough. Epic 5 now standardizes the service result layer before\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nroute mapping.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport type ArtifactReviewLookupResult =\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'artifact_ready'; artifact: ArtifactReviewTarget }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'artifact_empty'; artifact: ArtifactReviewTarget }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'artifact_unsupported'; target: ReviewTarget }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'artifact_target_not_found' }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'artifact_version_not_found'; artifactId: string; versionId: string };\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexport type PackageReviewLookupResult =\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'package_ready'; package: PackageReviewTarget }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'package_target_not_found' }\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  | { kind: 'package_member_unavailable'; packageId: string; memberId: string };\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRoute mapping rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `routes/review.ts` consumes these result kinds directly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- bootstrap routes (`GET /review`) convert the not-found/version/member cases\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  into bounded `target.status` / `target.error` states once project/process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  context has resolved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- target-specific routes keep exact request-level responses:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `artifact_target_not_found` -> `404 REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `artifact_version_not_found` -> `404 ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `package_target_not_found` -> `404 REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `package_member_unavailable` -> `404 PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplementation rule:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- where the review services already throw `AppError` subclasses, Epic 5 adds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  exact classes such as `ArtifactVersionNotFoundAppError`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `PackageMemberUnavailableAppError`, and `PackageMemberNotAllowedAppError`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `routes/review.ts` may either map discriminated result kinds directly or map\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  these exact `AppError` subclasses through `buildRequestError`, but it must no\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  longer collapse them all into `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`server/schemas/review.ts` must therefore document those exact codes on the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact and package route schemas, even though the HTTP status remains `404`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Error Taxonomy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nShared request-level errors gain:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `PACKAGE_MEMBER_NOT_ALLOWED`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nReview-target envelope errors gain the same codes where bootstrap needs bounded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndegradation instead of a request-level failure.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis means the server now distinguishes four materially different cases:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Condition | Bootstrap `/review` | Direct endpoint |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-----------|---------------------|-----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Target not in current process context | bounded unavailable target with `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` | `404 REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Explicit version missing | bounded unavailable artifact target with `ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND` | `404 ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Explicit package member unavailable | bounded unavailable selected-member state with `PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE` | `404 PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Publish request includes member outside allowed context | n/a | `409 PACKAGE_MEMBER_NOT_ALLOWED` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Observability\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 adds or sharpens the following log points:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifact.alignment.checkpoint.persisted`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `versionId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `createdByProcessId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `projectId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `review.context.resolved`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `projectId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `currentArtifactCount`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `directReviewArtifactCount`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `packageTargetCount`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `zeroVersionArtifactCount`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `package.context.updated`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `packageContextId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `memberCount`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `basePackageSnapshotId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `package.publish.rejected`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `reason`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `artifactVersionId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `review.target.degraded`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `projectId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `targetKind`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `targetId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `reason`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe observability goal is not more logs for their own sake. It is to make\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlater provenance debugging tractable when the failure is \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"missing version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"member outside allowed context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" rather than a generic review failure.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:33:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_15\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/test-plan.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Test Plan: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Purpose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis document maps every Epic 5 test condition to a planned test file and test\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napproach. Epic 5 is not a surface-first feature, so the plan leans heavily on\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nservice-mock tests at the durable-state and server-boundary layers. The client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstill matters, but the highest-value confidence comes from proving that the new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact/package semantics are correct before the browser renders them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe confidence chain for this epic is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAC → TC → Test File / Test Name → Implementation Module\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 adds one testing emphasis beyond earlier epics: the difference between\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity, version provenance, current refs, package context, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npublished package membership must be tested directly. If those layers collapse\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nin fixtures or tests, the implementation can accidentally regress to the old\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"primary process owns artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" model without anyone noticing.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Verification Tiers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Script | Command | Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|---------|-------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `red-verify` | `corepack pnpm run red-verify` | Format, lint, typecheck, and build. No tests. |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test:convex` | `corepack pnpm exec vitest run convex --environment node` | Durable-state service-mock tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test:service` | `corepack pnpm exec vitest run tests/service/server --environment node` | Fastify route and service-mock tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test:client` | `corepack pnpm exec vitest run tests/service/client --environment jsdom` | Client route/page/panel tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test:packages` | `corepack pnpm -r --filter \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"./packages/*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" test` | Runs because the repo-level `verify` lane includes it, even though Epic 5 is not expected to add package-workspace tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test:integration` | `corepack pnpm exec vitest run tests/integration --environment node` | Assembled app integration tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify` | `corepack pnpm run verify` | Standard development gate |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `green-verify` | `corepack pnpm run green-verify` | Standard gate plus test-immutability guard |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify-all` | `corepack pnpm run verify-all` | Deep gate including integration and scaffolded e2e |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Test Layers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Convex and Durable-State Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese are the primary confidence layer for the model realignment.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/artifacts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/packageSnapshots.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/processPackageContexts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Server Service-Mock Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese verify the Fastify and service composition over real internal code and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmocked external boundaries.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-foundation-contracts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/process-foundation-contracts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/project-shell-bootstrap-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/observability.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Client Service-Mock Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese verify the browser's public entry points and bounded degraded rendering.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/project-shell-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/process-work-surface-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/version-switcher.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/package-review-panel.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/package-member-nav.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Integration Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese verify durable reopen, cross-surface coherence, and end-to-end policy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nalignment through the assembled Fastify app and in-memory store.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/integration/review-workspace.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Mock Boundaries\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Layer | Mock? | Why |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|-------|-----|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| WorkOS/session validation | Yes | External auth boundary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Convex HTTP client in server tests | Yes | External durable-store boundary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Browser `fetch` in client tests | Yes | HTTP boundary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Signed content URL fetches | Yes | External content boundary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Fastify routes and review services | No | They are the behavior under test |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `PlatformStore` in in-memory/integration tests | No | It is part of the system we are validating |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Client router, store, pages, panels | No | They are the public client entry points |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Pure review/package helper functions | No | They are our own logic |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Fixture Strategy\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 fixtures must carry the aligned model explicitly. Every fixture group\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshould make it obvious which layer is being exercised.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Artifact Fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`tests/fixtures/artifacts.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project-scoped artifact with no process-owner fields\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact with versions from multiple processes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version artifact still reachable through current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact revised by a later process while retaining one identity\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Process-Ref Fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`tests/fixtures/process-material-refs.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs for one process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unrelated process with no current ref to the artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- process with zero-version current ref\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Package Context Fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`tests/fixtures/package-contexts.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current package context seeded from current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current package context with earlier pinned version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- reopened package context seeded from published snapshot\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- context containing one disallowed unrelated version candidate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Package Snapshot Fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`tests/fixtures/package-snapshots.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- mixed-producer package snapshot\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- snapshot with later newer version outside the pinned member set\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- snapshot with one unavailable member\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- snapshot with all members readable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Error Fixtures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`tests/fixtures/review-errors.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `PACKAGE_MEMBER_NOT_ALLOWED`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## File Inventory and Count Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe table below is the authoritative count surface for the planned `121` tests.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEach file has one primary ownership slot for count reconciliation even if later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstories reference that file across more than one chunk.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Test File | Primary Chunk | Planned Tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-----------|---------------|---------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/review-foundation-contracts.test.ts` | Chunk 0 | 6 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/process-foundation-contracts.test.ts` | Chunk 0 | 6 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/project-shell-bootstrap-api.test.ts` | Chunk 1 | 8 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts` | Chunk 1 | 5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/project-shell-page.test.ts` | Chunk 1 | 5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/artifacts.test.ts` | Chunk 2 | 10 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts` | Chunk 2 | 12 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts` | Chunk 3 | 10 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts` | Chunk 3 | 9 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/version-switcher.test.ts` | Chunk 3 | 8 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/packageSnapshots.test.ts` | Chunk 4 | 6 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/processPackageContexts.test.ts` | Chunk 4 | 5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts` | Chunk 4 | 7 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | Chunk 4 | 2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/package-review-panel.test.ts` | Chunk 4 | 4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/integration/review-workspace.test.ts` | Chunk 5 | 7 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/server/observability.test.ts` | Chunk 5 | 4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/process-work-surface-page.test.ts` | Chunk 5 | 4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/service/client/package-member-nav.test.ts` | Chunk 5 | 3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| **Total** | — | **121** |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## TC → Test Mapping\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEvery TC from the epic appears below. Each row names the primary test file that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nowns that condition. Additional client or integration coverage may exist, but\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe table below is the authoritative coverage map.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `convex/artifacts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.1a | later process can add an existing project artifact to current refs without changing artifact identity | Proves current refs are process-local, not artifact ownership |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.1b | later process reference preserves earlier lineage | Validates version provenance remains untouched |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.1a | revising an existing artifact creates a new version row | Direct write-path assertion |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.1b | revising process does not reassign the artifact to itself | Guards removal of artifact-row ownership |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.2a | producing process is recorded on the new version | Direct version-provenance assertion |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/project-shell-bootstrap-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.2a | new process-created artifact appears in project artifact state immediately | Confirms no separate add-to-project step |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.2b | project artifact state remains deduplicated when several processes reference one artifact | Guards against per-process artifact rows |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.3a | project artifact summaries show latest version label and update time after later-process revision | Confirms latest-version projection rule |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.3a | current process materials show a referenced artifact | Validates process-local visibility |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-1.3b | unrelated process does not gain automatic current-material visibility | Guards process-locality |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.2a | one unavailable referenced artifact does not fail unrelated current materials | Confirms section-level degradation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.1a | process can review referenced artifact created by earlier process | Reviewability from current refs |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.1b | process can still review referenced artifact after later revision by another process | Guards against latest-producer mismatch |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.4a | zero-version artifact is omitted from default review target list | Target-list rule |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.4a | missing review target is classified as review-target failure, not export failure | Bootstrap classification |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.4b | missing artifact target, missing version, and unavailable package member remain distinct | Workspace-level classification |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.2b | earlier version provenance remains readable | Uses cross-process version history |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-2.3b | earlier versions remain reviewable | Explicit earlier-version selection |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.2a | review succeeds despite primary-process mismatch because reviewability is process-context based | Direct regression guard |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.2b | unrelated artifact remains unavailable when not in current refs or pinned context | Negative reviewability case |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.3a | selected earlier version is displayed | Explicit version path |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.3b | current-version review remains stable after newer versions exist | Guards drift to wrong current/pinned interpretation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.4b | direct zero-version artifact review returns bounded empty state | Empty, not unavailable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.4c | explicit version request against zero-version artifact returns `ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND` | Exact request-level classification |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.3a | artifact review unavailable reason reflects missing reference vs missing version accurately | Error taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `convex/packageSnapshots.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.1a | mixed-producer package publication is allowed inside one project | Removes same-process-producer restriction |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.1b | cross-project package member is rejected | Same-project boundary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.2a | publishing process can package referenced upstream versions | Current-ref current-version eligibility |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.2b | unrelated project artifact version outside current package-building context is rejected | `PACKAGE_MEMBER_NOT_ALLOWED` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `convex/processPackageContexts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.2c | earlier pinned version remains eligible when reopening or editing package context | Core reason for durable package context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.3a | package member opens the pinned version | Snapshot pin integrity |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.3b | newer later artifact version does not replace pinned package member | Guards floating membership |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.4a | mixed-producer package review succeeds | Cross-process package review |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.2b | one unavailable package member does not remove unrelated members | Per-member degradation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.3b | unavailable package member reason reflects pinned-version failure | Error taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.4b | mixed-producer package export succeeds with pinned contents | Export behavior on aligned snapshot |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/integration/review-workspace.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.1a | reopening process artifact materials restores latest durable state after later cross-process update | Durable reopen path |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.1b | reopening package review after later artifact activity still resolves pinned members | Durable package reopen path |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Secondary Client Coverage\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese rows are intentionally secondary rather than primary ownership rows. They\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmake the user-visible obligations explicit so story authors and implementers do\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot accidentally treat stale-selection fallback or provenance display as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nserver-only concerns.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.4b | zero-version direct artifact review renders bounded empty state | User-visible empty-state obligation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.4a | stale target bootstrap renders review-target failure copy, not export-failure copy | UI classification obligation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/client/version-switcher.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.3a | selecting an earlier version updates the rendered artifact review state | Client selection path |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-3.4c | stale explicit version selection falls back to workspace reload and bounded unavailable state | Explicit client recovery obligation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### `tests/service/client/package-review-panel.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC | Test Description | Coverage Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----|------------------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-4.4a | mixed-producer package renders as normal package review state | User-visible package alignment |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.2b | one unavailable package member leaves unrelated members visible | Client-side partial degradation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| TC-5.3b | selected member failure copy refers to unavailable pinned version rather than ownership mismatch | User-visible taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Non-TC Decided Tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 also needs defensive tests that are not one-to-one with a TC but protect\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe alignment itself:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project-shell artifact rows no longer render ownership language\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- review enablement on the process work surface is false when the process has\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  only zero-version refs and no package targets\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selecting a stale explicit version triggers workspace reload fallback instead\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  of collapsing the whole workspace into a fatal error\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- selecting a stale explicit package member triggers workspace reload fallback\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package snapshot publication from current context preserves member order\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- observability events emit exact error reasons for version and member failures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Work Breakdown by Chunk\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 0: Foundations\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Contract changes, error-code additions, store-boundary narrowing,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage-context fixtures, and migration scaffolding.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q2`, `Q3`, `Q4`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Durable State Model`, `Store Boundary Changes`, `Shared Contract Shapes`, `Error Taxonomy`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `Contract and State Changes`, `Review Version Contracts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-foundation-contracts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/process-foundation-contracts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact summary schema no longer accepts process-owner fields\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- review-target error schema accepts `ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE`, and `PACKAGE_MEMBER_NOT_ALLOWED`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ReviewWorkspaceState` continues distinguishing request failure from bounded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  target degradation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 12\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 1: Project Artifact Association Without Process Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Project-shell artifact summary, current refs, and process-local\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmaterials visibility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**ACs:** AC-1.1 through AC-1.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q2`, `Work Breakdown`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Flow 1: Referencing Project Artifacts From Process Work`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `Flow 1: Project Shell Artifact Summary`, `Flow 2: Process Work Surface Review Entry`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/project-shell-bootstrap-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/project-shell-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project shell removes \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Attached to X process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" copy entirely\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- process-surface review control does not infer eligibility from artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 18\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 2: Versioned Checkpoint Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Artifact revision write path, latest-version projection, and earlier\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nversion readability.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**ACs:** AC-2.1 through AC-2.3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q5`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Durable State Model`, `Flow 2: Creating a New Version Without Reassigning the Artifact`, `Shared Contract Shapes`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `artifact-review-panel.ts`, `Review Version Contracts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/artifacts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/integration/review-workspace.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- checkpoint write preserves artifact identity when artifact id is supplied\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- project and process reads both derive `updatedAt` from latest version rather\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  than from artifact-row ownership state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact review payload includes `producedByProcessId` on version summaries and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  selected version detail\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 22\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 3: Process-Scoped Artifact Review Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Review target eligibility, explicit version selection, zero-version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbehavior, and bootstrap versus follow-up read handling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**ACs:** AC-3.1 through AC-3.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q3`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Flow 3: Review Context Resolution`, `Flow 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States`, `Error Taxonomy`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `Flow 3: Review Workspace Bootstrap`, `Flow 4: Explicit Version Selection`, `Review Workspace Fallback Handling`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- bootstrap uses bounded unavailable target state for stale selection\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- client version-selection fallback preserves workspace orientation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version artifact copy distinguishes empty from unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- version switcher and selected-version panel render `producedByProcessId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  metadata clearly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 27\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 4: Cross-Process Package Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Current package-building context, cross-process publish eligibility,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage review, and package export.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**ACs:** AC-4.1 through AC-4.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q4`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Durable State Model`, `Flow 4: Current Package Context and Publication`, `Package Context Shapes`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `Flow 5: Package Member Selection and Degraded Members`, `package-review-panel.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/packageSnapshots.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `convex/processPackageContexts.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/package-member-nav.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- reopened package context seeds from published snapshot members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- export trigger remains visible for aligned mixed-producer package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unavailable member stays disabled but visible in member navigation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 23\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope:** Durable reopen, partial degradation, exact classification, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nobservability.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**ACs:** AC-5.1 through AC-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant Tech Design Sections:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design.md` → `Q3`, `Q5`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-server.md` → `Flow 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States`, `Error Taxonomy`, `Observability`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tech-design-client.md` → `Flow 3: Review Workspace Bootstrap`, `Flow 4: Explicit Version Selection`, `Flow 5: Package Member Selection and Degraded Members`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Relevant files:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/server/observability.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/integration/review-workspace.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `tests/service/client/process-work-surface-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Non-TC decided tests:**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- stale explicit selection does not lose target list or process context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unavailable package member preserves exportability reason messaging\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- observability emits distinct reason codes for target, version, and member failures\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Test Count:** 19\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Count Reconciliation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk | Tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|-------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 0 | 12 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 1 | 18 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 2 | 22 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 3 | 27 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 4 | 23 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 5 | 19 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| **Total** | **121** |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Manual Verification Checklist\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Open a project shell with several artifacts and confirm artifact rows no\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   longer mention attachment to one process.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Open a process that references an artifact first created elsewhere and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   confirm review is still available.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Open artifact review on an earlier version, then create a later version and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   confirm the earlier version remains selectable.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Open a zero-version artifact directly and confirm the workspace shows an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/00-foundation/009-self-review-pass-1.json:1:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"resultId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"64406627-3c14-4769-9e6e-1d2a3e2b78c2\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"codex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"model\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gpt-5.4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"story_self_review_pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sessionId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"019dcb8d-54d4-7882-8bd6-b820edf96e06\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"continuation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"provider\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"codex\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sessionId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"019dcb8d-54d4-7882-8bd6-b820edf96e06\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"storyId\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"00-foundation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"outcome\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ready-for-verification\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"story\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"00-foundation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"title\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story 0: Foundation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"passNumber\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"planSummary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Completed the Story 0 follow-up by renaming the shared package-member browser vocabulary to `artifactVersionId`, wiring exact route-level review error taxonomy for explicit missing artifact versions and package members, and refreshing fixtures/test