\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Populated review version provenance ids and display labels from store data.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added package-context record types/methods and made package review scaffolding provenance-aware.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/processPackageContexts.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added durable current package-context query and mutation support with canonical upsert and duplicate cleanup semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/processPackageContextMembers.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added durable ordered package-context member query support.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/schema.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Registered the new process package-context tables and indexes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/artifacts.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Slimmed project artifact summary reads to the Story 0 project-scoped vocabulary.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/_generated/api.d.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Regenerated Convex bindings after adding package-context functions and tables.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"convex/_generated/ai/ai-files.state.json\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Refreshed generated Convex AI metadata during codegen/dev.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"apps/platform/client/features/projects/artifact-section.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Removed project-shell ownership copy so artifact rows render as project assets only.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/fixtures/artifact-versions.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added provenance metadata to shared artifact-version fixtures.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/fixtures/package-snapshots.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated package-member degradation fixtures to the new error vocabulary.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/fixtures/review-workspace.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated bounded review-workspace fixtures for the new package-member error code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/fixtures/review-errors.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added shared request and target error fixtures for Story 0 review/package classifications.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/fixtures/package-contexts.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added shared current and reopened package-context fixtures for later Epic 5 stories.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/review-foundation-contracts.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Expanded Story 0 contract coverage for slim artifact summaries, provenance, and new error codes.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated package-review fixture expectations for required provenance fields.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated server package-review fixtures to include provenance-bearing version summaries.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/nfr.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated package-review NFR fixtures to satisfy provenance-bearing artifact review contracts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/observability.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated observed package-review fixtures to satisfy provenance-bearing artifact review contracts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/client/artifact-review-panel.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated unsupported-version fixture data for required provenance fields.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/client/artifact-section.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Updated UI assertions for ownership-free project artifact rendering.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/integration/platform-shell.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Made the production-shell asset assertion use the active worktree build output path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"added\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"modified\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/review-foundation-contracts.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/nfr.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/server/observability.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/client/artifact-review-panel.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/service/client/artifact-section.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"tests/integration/platform-shell.test.ts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"removed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"totalAfterStory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":552,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"deltaFromPriorBaseline\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"gatesRun\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"corepack pnpm run green-verify\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"corepack pnpm run verify-all\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"result\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"pass\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"selfReview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"passesRun\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":1,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"findingsFixed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Removed artifact ownership fields from project-shell artifact summaries and rendering while preserving process-local artifact references.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added provenance ids and display labels to review version contracts and patched store/service/test scaffolding to emit them consistently.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Added durable process package-context tables plus store methods with canonical upsert and idempotent semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fixed the production-shell integration assertion to resolve the built asset from the active worktree instead of a hard-coded sibling checkout path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"findingsSurfaced\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[]},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"openQuestions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"specDeviations\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Browser-facing package member contracts still expose legacy `versionId`; Story 0 added the durable/package-context `artifactVersionId` vocabulary and deferred the surface rename to avoid wider Story 3/4 regressions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review target schemas still accept legacy `REVIEW_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE` alongside `PACKAGE_MEMBER_UNAVAILABLE` as a compatibility bridge for existing package review flows.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedNextStep\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Implement Story 1 against the new project-scoped artifact summary and package-context foundation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/001-implementor.stdout.log:10:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/stories/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Story 1: Project Artifact Association Without Process Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Summary field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nMake project artifacts project-scoped durable assets, keep process-local visibility in current materials, and remove single-process ownership language from project-shell artifact state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Description\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Description field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**User Profile**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Primary User:** A technical founder, tech lead, staff engineer, or agentic operator who builds work across multiple related processes inside one project.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Context:** The user accumulates durable artifacts across planning, specification, implementation, and review work. They need those artifacts to remain stable project assets even when later processes revise, review, or package them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Mental Model:** \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Artifacts belong to the project. Processes work with them, create new versions of them, and package them together, but no single process owns the artifact itself.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **Key Constraint:** This epic must realign artifact behavior inside the existing project shell, process work surface, and review/package surfaces without adding a separate standalone artifact-management product surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Objective**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nRealign project-shell and process-surface artifact visibility so artifacts are durable project assets while current process visibility remains process-owned.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Scope**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nIn:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact summary no longer implies primary process ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing project artifacts can be added to a process's current refs without transferring ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- New process-created artifacts appear in the project artifact set immediately\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process materials continue to show only the artifacts currently referenced by that process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nOut:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Versioned revision write-path changes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Artifact review resolution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Package publication or package review behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Dependencies**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Story 0 foundation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing project shell and process work surface routes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [tech-design.md](../tech-design.md), [tech-design-server.md](../tech-design-server.md), [tech-design-client.md](../tech-design-client.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [test-plan.md](../test-plan.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Acceptance Criteria field -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.1:** A process can reference an existing artifact in the same project without becoming the artifact's sole owner.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.1a: Existing project artifact added to process**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A project already has a durable artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: A later process begins working with that artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The process can reference the artifact without replacing prior artifact provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.1b: Later process reference does not erase earlier process lineage**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: An artifact was first created or revised by an earlier process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: A later process references that artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: Earlier artifact provenance remains intact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.2:** A new artifact created during process work is immediately part of the project's artifact set.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.2a: New process artifact appears in project artifact state**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A process creates a new artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The creation settles durably\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact appears in the project's artifact state without a separate add-to-project step\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.2b: Project artifact state remains deduplicated**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: Several processes later reference the same artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The project artifact state is read\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: That artifact appears as one durable project artifact, not one row per process reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**AC-1.3:** Process-local artifact visibility reflects current process reference, not artifact ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.3a: Current process materials show referenced artifact**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: A process currently references an artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: The process work surface is loaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact appears in that process's current materials\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- **TC-1.3b: Unrelated process does not gain automatic current-material visibility**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Given: One process currently references an artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - When: Another process in the same project loads its current materials\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Then: The artifact is not shown as current for that unrelated process unless that process also references it\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Technical Design\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Technical Notes or sub-section of Description -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis story owns the first visible expression of the aligned model: project-scoped artifact identity and process-local current visibility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Endpoints\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Operation | Method | Path | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Get project shell | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}` | Returns project-level artifact state with project-scoped artifact identity and latest version summary |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Get process work surface | `GET` | `/api/projects/{projectId}/processes/{processId}` | Returns process-local current artifact materials and current outputs without implying artifact ownership |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Project Artifact Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Field | Type | Required | Validation | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifactId` | string | yes | non-empty | Stable artifact identifier |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `displayName` | string | yes | non-empty | Human-readable artifact name |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `currentVersionLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Latest current version label |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `updatedAt` | timestamp | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Latest durable update time for this artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n#### Process Artifact Reference\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Field | Type | Required | Validation | Description |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifactId` | string | yes | non-empty | Stable artifact identifier |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `displayName` | string | yes | non-empty | Artifact name in process context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `currentVersionLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Latest current version label for the artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `roleLabel` | string | no | non-empty when present | Process-local description such as current working artifact or referenced artifact |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `updatedAt` | timestamp | yes | ISO 8601 UTC | Latest durable artifact update time |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplementation note:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact rows no longer include `attachmentScope`, `processId`, or `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process-local visibility continues to come from bounded current refs in per-type process state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nSee the tech design document for full architecture, implementation targets, and test mapping.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Definition of Done\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n<!-- Jira: Definition of Done or Acceptance Criteria footer -->\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project-shell artifact summaries show project-scoped identity only and no longer imply artifact ownership by one process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Existing artifacts can be added to a process's current refs without changing artifact identity or provenance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process materials show only the current process's referenced artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project artifact state remains deduplicated when many processes reference one artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Story tests cover TC-1.1a through TC-1.1b, TC-1.2a through TC-1.2b, and TC-1.3a through TC-1.3b\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/001-implementor.stdout.log:23:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_10\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Technical Design: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Purpose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis document translates Epic 5 into implementable architecture for the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact-model alignment step that sits between Epic 4's review/package surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand Epic 6's broader source-management work. It is the index document for a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfour-file tech design set:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Document | Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design.md` | Decision record, spec validation, inherited architecture, system view, module architecture overview, and work breakdown |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-server.md` | Fastify, review services, `PlatformStore`, Convex schema, migration plan, package-context model, and error taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-client.md` | Project-shell artifact summary changes, review workspace behavior, target-selection flows, bounded degraded states, and route/state updates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test-plan.md` | TC-to-test mapping, fixtures, mock strategy, file inventory, chunk counts, and verification gates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe downstream consumers are:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Audience | What they need from this design |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|---------------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Reviewers | A clear account of what is changing in the durable model, what is staying process-owned, and where the current code still encodes the old ownership assumption |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Story authors | Story-aligned chunk boundaries that match the epic's sequencing and leave no ambiguity about which contracts, migrations, and tests belong together |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Implementers | Exact file targets, durable-state decisions, review-context rules, package-context rules, migration sequencing, and verification commands |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Spec Validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is designable. The requirements are concrete, the ACs and TCs are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nusefully specific, and the epic asks the right questions for this phase. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmain work in validation was not repairing a broken epic; it was separating the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nepic's aligned target model from the current repo's partially pre-alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimplementation so the design could speak to both honestly.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe most important validation outcome is that the current repo is already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfarther along than the onboarding pack says. The onboarding docs in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`docs/onboarding/` were cut on `2026-04-15` at commit `d85d69b...`, but the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking tree for this design is at `f14c88e...` on `2026-04-25`. Review and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage surfaces now exist in the code and tests. What Epic 5 has to realign\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis not a hypothetical future review layer; it is a real, shipped-in-repo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview/package implementation whose core artifact assumptions are still too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclose to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"single primary process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat current-state gap changes the design emphasis. Epic 5 is not primarily a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnet-new UI epic. It is a data-model, read-model, and policy-alignment epic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat preserves the existing review/package surfaces while changing the rules\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose surfaces rely on. The design therefore stays tightly coupled to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexisting Fastify control plane, `PlatformStore` boundary, shared contracts, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview route family rather than inventing new top-level product surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Issues Found\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Issue | Spec Location | Resolution | Status |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|---------------|------------|--------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current onboarding pack says the markdown review workspace and package surface are not present, but the current repo now contains `routes/review.ts`, review services, review client pages, and full review/package test suites | `docs/onboarding/current-state-process-work-surface.md`, `docs/onboarding/current-state-tech-design.md` | Treat the onboarding pack as a stale baseline for this domain. Design against current code and tests, and record the drift here so later doc refresh work has a concrete starting point | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Epic 5's error-response table does not distinguish between review-workspace bootstrap reads, which benefit from bounded degraded target states, and target-specific follow-up reads, which can legitimately return request-level 404/409 responses | Data Contracts → Error Responses; AC-5.3, AC-5.4 | Design keeps the existing review-workspace bootstrap pattern: once project/process context resolves, `GET /review` returns `200` with bounded `target.status` / `target.error` degradation. Target-specific follow-up reads continue to use request-level `404` / `409` when the caller asks for an explicit missing version, missing member, or disallowed publication member | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current `PlatformStore` interface owns high-level review-target composition and package review assembly, which bakes the pre-alignment same-process assumptions into every store implementation | Current code: `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts`; Epic Q3, Q5 | Epic 5 narrows `PlatformStore` back toward durable-state primitives and moves review-context composition into server review services and pure helpers. This is an intentional design deviation because the old interface shape would force the wrong policy to keep leaking into every store implementation | Resolved — deviated |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package publication logic still requires every pinned member version to have `createdByProcessId === publishingProcessId`, which directly conflicts with AC-4.1 and AC-4.2 | Current code: package publication path in `platform-store.ts`; AC-4.1, AC-4.2 | Publish eligibility is rewritten around same-project membership plus current package-building context, not same-process production. Cross-process package members become valid when they are either current versions of currently referenced artifacts or explicitly pinned in the same process's current package context | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic asks how current package-building context should be represented durably, but the current repo only has immutable `packageSnapshots` and their members | Tech Design Questions Q4 | Design adds a dedicated mutable package-context model rather than overloading immutable package snapshots or broadening current-refs into a catch-all relation table | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic requires version provenance visibility via `producedByProcessId`, but raw ids alone are too thin for a user-facing provenance surface | Data Contracts → Artifact Version Summary; AC-2.2 | Design keeps `producedByProcessId` in browser-facing contracts and adds derived `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` so the UI can show readable provenance while still preserving the stable id | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 4 established the first review/package surface, but it did so on top of a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndurable artifact model that still leaked an older assumption: one artifact row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncould still look like it \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"belonged\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to one process. The repo has already moved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsome of the right semantics down to the version layer. `artifactVersions` has\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`createdByProcessId`, the process work surface already projects current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmaterials from per-process current refs, and package snapshots already pin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexplicit version ids. Epic 5 exists because those good local decisions are not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyet the platform's full governing model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe problem shows up anywhere a later process touches earlier work. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureSpecification` process should be able to reference a PRD created by a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProductDefinition` process without the artifact changing owners. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureImplementation` process should be able to revise a tech design artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfrom an earlier specification process without rewriting the artifact row to say\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"this belongs to implementation now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" A publishing process should be able to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbundle pinned versions from several processes into one reviewable package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwithout being told that only versions it personally produced are legitimate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is why Epic 5 belongs between Epic 4 and Epic 6 rather than inside either\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nof them. Epic 4 proved there is a usable review/package surface. Epic 6 will\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmake broader claims about canonical sources, provenance, freshness, and later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\narchive work. If Epic 6 inherited the current artifact assumptions, it would\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhave to untangle project-scoped artifact identity, version provenance, process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking context, and package membership at the same time it is trying to settle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsource-management behavior. Epic 5 narrows the job: fix the artifact world\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfirst so later source work inherits the right semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current repo architecture helps here. Fastify already owns the review route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfamily, the client already owns a dedicated review workspace state slice, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe durable model already has most of the necessary nouns: project artifacts,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact versions, per-process current refs, package snapshots, and package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers. The design therefore does not need a new product surface or a new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-runtime abstraction. It needs a cleaner separation of concerns and a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsmall amount of new durable state where the current implementation is missing a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshared, process-scoped package-context model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe alignment target is simple to say even if it touches a lot of code:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifacts are project-scoped durable identities, versions carry producing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess provenance, current process refs express what a process is working with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnow, package contexts express which earlier versions a process has explicitly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npinned into current publishing work, and package snapshots express the durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npublished result. Review and package behavior are allowed to look at all of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose layers, but none of those layers should collapse back into one overloaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifact owner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" field.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Inherited Decisions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 does not add new runtime dependencies and does not reopen the platform's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsettled architectural choices. It inherits the current stack and works within\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe same runtime surfaces already in use.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Area | Current Choice | Source | Epic 5 Posture |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|----------------|--------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Control plane | Fastify 5 monolith | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. All browser-facing behavior still routes through Fastify |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable state | Convex behind `PlatformStore` | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. Artifact and package alignment remain durable-store concerns, not browser concerns |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Client build and app shell | Vite-built client, Fastify-served shell | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. No separate review SPA or artifact admin app |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared contracts | Zod schemas in `apps/platform/shared/contracts` | Current repo | Keep. Epic 5 changes those schemas rather than bypassing them |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review surface shape | Existing `/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/review` route family | Current repo | Keep. Behavior realigns inside the existing route family |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process current refs | Per-type process state tables store bounded current artifact ids | Current repo + epic assumptions | Keep as the bounded working-set model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Epic-Scoped Stack Additions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNone. Epic 5 deliberately avoids new libraries. The work is in durable-state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshape, contract shape, service composition, and test coverage, not in new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage adoption.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Tech Design Question Answers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q1. Should durable process-to-artifact relationship beyond current working-set refs be modeled through a dedicated relation table or by extending the existing current-ref model?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDo not add a generic process-to-artifact relation table in Epic 5.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current-ref arrays in the per-type process state tables are still the right\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmodel for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what this process is working with now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" They are bounded, already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfit the process work-surface materials panel, and already line up with the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-first architecture. The problem is not that current refs are too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsimple; the problem is that the platform has been asking current refs to carry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconcerns they were never meant to carry.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 therefore keeps current refs for current working state and introduces a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nseparate durable package-context model for the one thing current refs cannot\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nencode cleanly: earlier pinned artifact versions that a process still needs to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncarry forward in package publication and reopen/edit flows. That new context is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot a generic relation table. It is a process-scoped package-building context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwith version-aware members, ordering, and reopen semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis split is intentional:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what artifacts are current for this process?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package-context members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"which artifact versions has this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  explicitly pinned into current package-building work?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- published package members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what exact versions did this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  publish as a durable package snapshot?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat gives the platform three precise layers instead of one mushy relation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlayer.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q2. Which existing artifact summary fields should be removed versus preserved as derived process-local display fields?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAt the project shell level, remove the artifact-level process fields entirely.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current `ArtifactSummary` contract still includes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `attachmentScope`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThose fields make sense only if a project artifact can still be described as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nattached to one primary process. Epic 5 removes them from the project-shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact summary contract and UI. The project shell keeps only project-scoped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity plus latest-version projection:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe process-local display fields stay where they belong: on\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProcessArtifactReference` in the process materials panel. That contract already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhas the right shape for this epic:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `roleLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is the cleanest separation between project-scoped durable summary and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-owned current meaning.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q3. How should current review-target eligibility be computed so it remains process-scoped without depending on single-process artifact ownership?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nReview eligibility becomes a process-context computation, not an artifact-row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncheck.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe aligned algorithm is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Start with the process's current artifact refs from its per-type state row.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add any artifact versions already pinned in that same process's current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package-building context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Add any artifact versions pinned inside package snapshots published from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   that same process, because reopening package review remains a valid process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Resolve those ids against the project's durable artifact identities and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   artifact versions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFrom that combined process review context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact targets in `availableTargets` come from artifact identities that are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  either currently referenced by the process or explicitly pinned in that same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process's current package-building context, provided the artifact has at least\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  one durable version and remains in this process review context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version current refs remain excluded from the default artifact target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  list\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package targets in `availableTargets` come from published package snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  for that process, including snapshots whose members may all be unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  later; the snapshot remains reviewable as a durable provenance object even\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  when its selected-member state is fully degraded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- direct artifact review succeeds when the artifact is reachable through\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current refs or pinned package context for that same process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unrelated project artifacts remain unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis keeps review process-scoped without relying on artifact-row ownership,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlatest-version producer, or any other single-field shortcut.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q4. How should the already-defined current package-building context be represented durably so package publish, review, and reopen flows share the same bounded member set?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAdd a dedicated mutable package-context model:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContexts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContextMembers`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContexts` is the header row for the current package-building\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontext owned by one process. It stores:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageType`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `basePackageSnapshotId` for reopen/edit-from-snapshot flows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContextMembers` stores the ordered, explicit version pins inside\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat current context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageContextId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `position`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactVersionId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `versionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `pinnedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis context is mutable and process-scoped. Published `packageSnapshots` remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimmutable and durable. A publishing process can request a snapshot from any\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmember set that is valid inside its current package-building context. Reopening\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nan existing package automatically seeds the current context from the published\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsnapshot's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers, after which the process may keep earlier pinned versions, replace some\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers with the current versions of currently referenced artifacts, or republish.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is cleaner than overloading immutable package snapshots into drafts and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncleaner than trying to make current refs carry version-specific package pins.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q5. What is the cleanest migration path for persisted artifact rows and review tests that currently assume a primary process on the artifact row?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nUse a staged code migration with a pragmatic data posture:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Change shared contracts and read models first so project-shell and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review/workspace code stop relying on artifact-row process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add the new package-context tables and store methods before removing the old\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package publication rule, so current and future publishers have a durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bounded context to validate against.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Rewrite artifact checkpoint, review eligibility, and package publication to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   stop reading `artifacts.processId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Remove `processId` from the `artifacts` table and delete the legacy summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   fields from `ArtifactSummary`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. Migrate or reset dev data as needed. Because this repo is still pre-customer,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   a dev deployment reset is acceptable if a local environment does not need\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   data preservation. The design still supports a one-off migration helper for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   teams that want to preserve local dev rows while iterating.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTest migration follows the same order: first update fixtures and contract tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nto the slimmer project artifact summary, then update review/package tests to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nassert process-context eligibility instead of same-process ownership, then add\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-process package and degraded-state coverage on top.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## System View\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 lives inside the same four runtime surfaces that already exist for this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npart of the product: Fastify routes and services, shared client/server\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontracts, the browser workspace/state layer, and Convex durable state. What\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchanges is the relationship between the concepts inside those surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Top-Tier Surfaces Touched\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Source | Epic 5 Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Projects | Inherited from platform tech arch + current project shell | Project-shell artifact summaries become truly project-scoped and lose artifact-level process fields |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Processes | Inherited from platform tech arch + current process work surface | Process current refs remain the bounded working-set model; review enablement is recomputed from aligned review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Artifacts | Inherited from platform tech arch + current artifact/version tables | Artifact identity becomes project-scoped only; version provenance remains version-scoped |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review Workspace | Existing Epic 4 surface | Review target resolution shifts from artifact ownership to process review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Packages | Existing Epic 4 surface, expanded in this epic | Published snapshots stay immutable; current package-building context becomes durable and mutable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared Contracts | Current repo | Project summaries, review contracts, error codes, and client state all realign around the new model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### System Context Diagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nflowchart LR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Client[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Browser Client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Shell[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Surface[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Review[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Fastify[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fastify Control Plane\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProjectSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ReviewSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Context + Review Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageCtx[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Package Context Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Convex[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Convex Durable State\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Artifacts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Versions[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifactVersions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessState[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"process*States current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageContexts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"processPackageContexts + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Snapshots[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packageSnapshots + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Shell --> ProjectSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Surface --> ProcessSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Review --> ReviewSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageCtx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Model Split\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe core architectural point of this epic is the model split below:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Concern | Durable Home | Meaning |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------------|---------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project artifact identity | `artifacts` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This artifact exists in this project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Revision provenance | `artifactVersions.createdByProcessId` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This version was produced by this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current process working set | per-type process state `currentArtifactIds` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process is currently working with these artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package-building pins | `processPackageContexts` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process has explicitly pinned these artifact versions into current publication work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable published package | `packageSnapshots` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This exact ordered set of pinned versions was published as one package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe rest of the design is a consequence of keeping those layers distinct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture Overview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is a four-surface change set: shared contracts, server services and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nroutes, browser read/render behavior, and Convex durable state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/shared/contracts/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas.ts                               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── review-workspace.ts                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── process-work-surface.ts                  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── state.ts                                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/server/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app.ts                                     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── routes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── services/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   ├── platform-store.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── readers/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │       └── artifact-section.reader.ts   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── process-work-surface.service.ts  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-workspace.service.ts      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── artifact-review.service.ts       # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── package-review.service.ts        # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-context.service.ts        # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       └── package-context.service.ts       # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/client/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── bootstrap.ts                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── store.ts                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── browser-api/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review-workspace-api.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── features/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── artifact-section.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── process-work-surface-page.ts     # MODIFIED\\\\o production path findings.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]},{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"S1-F3\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fixed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"018-verify.json reports S1-F3 resolved because appendArchiveEntry and listArchiveEntries exports were restored in convex/archiveEntries.ts.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"018-verify.json reports both the focused archiveEntries test and green-verify passed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"baselineBeforeStory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":634,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"baselineAfterStory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":641,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"openRisks\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[]},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cumulativeBaseline\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"baselineBeforeCurrentStory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":634,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"expectedAfterCurrentStory\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":641,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"latestActualTotal\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":641},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"commitReadiness\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"not-ready\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reason\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Story gate has not passed in the current evidence set.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"openRisks\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[]},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cleanupHandoff\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"acceptedRiskItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"deferredItems\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"cleanupRequired\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":false},\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rulingRequest\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":null,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"recommendedImplLeadAction\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"reopen\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence/story-lead/streams/001-story-lead.stdout.log:11:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_0\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"action\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"request-ruling\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rationale\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"The latest verifier result has a blocking human-ruling finding: Story 1's scope says finalization hooks, archive API/UI, and provenance enrichment are deferred, while its AC/test mappings still name TC-1.1a/b/c and TC-1.4a/b service/API evidence that is absent. Acceptance is disallowed because the verifier outcome is not pass and open finding S1-F1 remains.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"inputs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"decisionType\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"scope-vs-evidence-conflict\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"question\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"For Story 1, should story-local acceptance be based on the implemented primitive-only Convex append/read evidence, with TC-1.1 finalization-hook and TC-1.4 read/enrichment service/API tests deferred to later stories, or must those mapped tests and services be implemented before this story can proceed?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"defaultRecommendation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Rule Story 1 as primitive-only: accept Convex append/read, taxonomy validation, sequence/idempotency, related-id storage, and bounded page shape as the required evidence for this story; defer finalization hook/API enrichment tests to the later stories already named as owning that behavior. After that ruling, run a quick fix for the non-blocking pagination materialization finding if desired, then re-verify.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"evidence\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Verifier artifact /Users/leemoore/code/liminal-build/docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence/008-verify.json reports status needs-user-decision and outcome needs-human-ruling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Open blocking finding S1-F1 says Story 1 defers live finalization hooks, archive read routes/UI, and read-time provenance enrichment while still requiring TC-1.1a/b/c and TC-1.4a/b evidence.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Requirement coverage in the verifier marks AC-1.2 and AC-1.3 verified, but AC-1.1 and AC-1.4 unverified.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"All gates reported by the verifier passed: targeted Convex test, green-verify, and verify-all.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"],\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"allowedResponses\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"primitive-only evidence is sufficient; defer TC-1.1/TC-1.4 service/API proof\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-1.1 and TC-1.4 mapped service/API tests remain in scope; implement before acceptance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"block story until story/test-plan wording is corrected\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]}}\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/artifacts/01-canonical-archive-entry-persistence/story-lead/streams/001-story-lead.stdout.log:15:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\