:only, leaves archive and derived views to Epic 7, and defers MCP/external-source\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/tech-design.md:85:those provenance records without asking source attachments to become archive\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/stories/04-package-review-workspace.md:14:- **Key Constraint:** The review surface must stay process-aware and version-aware without turning into a generic document-management product, archive browser, or broader publication workflow in the same epic.\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/stories/04-package-review-workspace.md:40:- Story 3 markdown and Mermaid review\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/06--source-attachments-and-canonical-source-management/artifacts/04-provenance-and-canonical-source-visibility/story-lead/prompts/001-planner-turn-004.md:62:- Story 3 current source/freshness surface\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:227:function readTarEntry(archive: Buffer, entryPath: string): string | null {\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:230:  while (offset + 512 <= archive.byteLength) {\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:231:    const header = archive.subarray(offset, offset + 512);\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:243:    const content = archive.subarray(offset, offset + size);\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:257:  it('TC-5.1a and TC-5.2 export the current package as a pinned .mpkz archive with manifest metadata', async () => {\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:313:    const archiveBytes =\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:317:    const archive = gunzipSync(archiveBytes);\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:318:    const manifest = readTarEntry(archive, '_nav.md');\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:319:    const specification = readTarEntry(archive, '1-feature-specification.md');\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:347:  it('preserves package members with colliding display names as distinct archive paths', async () => {\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:383:    const archiveBytes =\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:387:    const archive = gunzipSync(archiveBytes);\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:388:    const manifest = readTarEntry(archive, '_nav.md');\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:389:    const firstSpec = readTarEntry(archive, '1-spec.md');\\\\\\\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:390:    const secondSpec = readTarEntry(archive, '2-spec.md');\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/stories/03-markdown-and-mermaid-review.md:1:# Story 3: Markdown and Mermaid Review\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/stories/03-markdown-and-mermaid-review.md:14:- **Key Constraint:** The review surface must stay process-aware and version-aware without turning into a generic document-management product, archive browser, or broader publication workflow in the same epic.\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/stories/03-markdown-and-mermaid-review.md:34:- Global archive browsing or arbitrary rich-media review support\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:11:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_4\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/onboarding/current-state-index.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Liminal Build — Current State Index\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis onboarding pack reflects repository state on `main` at commit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`d85d69b5478a1435db49495a541b6ac2c1523d07`, inspected on `2026-04-15`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is the first current-state baseline pack for the repo. It is a catch-up\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbaseline, not a refresh of earlier current-state docs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Evidence Scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Historical artifacts reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-arch.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/02--process-work-surface/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v1/prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/epic-01-handoff.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Code and runtime surfaces reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/server`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/client`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `package.json`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/package.json`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Tests and repo-visible validation surfaces reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/integration/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/client/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - root `pnpm` scripts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Evidence limitations:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - No prior `current-state*.md` docs existed.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - No `.github` workflow configuration is present in the repo.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `test:e2e` is scaffolded but intentionally non-executable today.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - No live runtime probe was executed; this pack is repository-evidence-bound.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Read This First\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis index is the entry point for the pack.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. [current-state-project-and-process-shell.md](./current-state-project-and-process-shell.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. [current-state-process-work-surface.md](./current-state-process-work-surface.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. [current-state-tech-design.md](./current-state-tech-design.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. [current-state-code-map.md](./current-state-code-map.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. [current-state-drift-ledger.md](./current-state-drift-ledger.md) when historical lineage matters\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n6. [current-state-doc-meta.json](./current-state-doc-meta.json) and [current-state-module-tree.json](./current-state-module-tree.json) only when automation or machine-readable clustering matters\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## System Overview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nLiminal Build currently ships a Fastify-served, auth-gated web workspace for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrunning crafted processes inside projects. The implemented user-facing product\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis centered on two surfaces:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. a project and process shell at `/projects` and `/projects/:projectId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. a dedicated process work surface at\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDurable project, process, history, output, side-work, artifact, and source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nstate lives in Convex behind a Fastify-owned `PlatformStore` boundary. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbrowser does not talk to Convex directly. WorkOS handles hosted\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nauthentication, while Fastify owns session resolution, shell delivery, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbrowser-facing APIs.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThree process types can be created now:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ProductDefinition`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `FeatureSpecification`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `FeatureImplementation`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state: those three types are durable first-class process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntypes in contracts, routes, and Convex tables. Implemented, weakly evidenced:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntheir current user-visible behavior is still mostly shared platform behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrather than distinct process-specific orchestration.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Current Process Types\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process Type | Durable State Owned Today | Distinct Current Behavior | Shared Current Behavior | Not Yet Differentiated |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------------|---------------------------|---------------------------|-------------------------|------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `ProductDefinition` | `processProductDefinitionStates` stores current artifact/source refs | Own dedicated process-type state row on creation | Draft creation, shell summary behavior, process-surface bootstrap shape, generic start/resume/respond lifecycle logic | No process-type-specific module, prompts, phase transitions, or request generation in repo code |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `FeatureSpecification` | `processFeatureSpecificationStates` stores current artifact/source refs | Own dedicated process-type state row on creation | Draft creation, shell summary behavior, process-surface bootstrap shape, generic start/resume/respond lifecycle logic | No process-type-specific module, prompts, phase transitions, or request generation in repo code |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `FeatureImplementation` | `processFeatureImplementationStates` stores current artifact/source refs | Own dedicated process-type state row on creation | Draft creation, shell summary behavior, process-surface bootstrap shape, generic start/resume/respond lifecycle logic | No process-type-specific module, prompts, phase transitions, or request generation in repo code |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplemented, weakly evidenced:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The per-type state tables are real current state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The meaningful behavioral difference between the three types is still minimal\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  in current repo code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Future process-specific differentiation is implied by structure, not yet\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  delivered by concrete registered modules.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Top-Tier Capability Domains\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Domain | What Exists Now | Read Next |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|------------------|-----------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project and process shell | Authenticated project index, project shell bootstrap, process registration, shell summaries for processes/artifacts/source attachments, route-based selected-process handling, and durable reopen behavior | [current-state-project-and-process-shell.md](./current-state-project-and-process-shell.md) |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process work surface | Dedicated process route, bootstrap payload for process state, history/current request/materials/side-work sections, start/resume/respond actions, live websocket updates, and durable reload/return behavior | [current-state-process-work-surface.md](./current-state-process-work-surface.md) |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Cross-Domain Flows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Authenticated entry and project selection.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   The browser loads a Fastify-rendered shell, resolves the current actor, then\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   fetches either the project index or one project shell.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Project creation and process registration.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Creating a project returns an empty shell immediately. Creating a process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   from that shell adds a typed draft process, updates project counts, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   focuses the shell on the new process through `?processId=...`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Process opening and active work.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Opening a process moves the user from project-level summaries to a dedicated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   process surface with current phase, status, history, materials, current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   request, and side work.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Active-work reconciliation and return.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   Start, resume, and respond actions update durable state first, then publish\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   live updates. Reloading or returning later rehydrates from the durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bootstrap APIs rather than from transient websocket state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Current Constraints and Invariants\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Unauthenticated HTML requests redirect to `/auth/login`; unauthenticated API\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  requests return `401 UNAUTHENTICATED`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project shell and process work-surface APIs return stable section envelopes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  and degrade section-by-section instead of failing the whole surface when one\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  section reader fails.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project shell process selection is route-derived query state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  (`?processId=...`). The dedicated process work surface uses a separate path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current process materials are a projection of current artifact/source refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  plus current outputs, not a full historical listing of everything ever\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  attached to the process.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Live websocket updates supplement the durable bootstrap. They are not the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  only readable source of truth, and retry falls back to a fresh HTTP read.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Fastify remains the browser-facing control plane. Convex remains a durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  store behind that boundary.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## What Is Not Current State Yet\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Planned, not current: provider-backed environments, sandbox filesystem\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  lifecycle, and in-sandbox tool runtime execution from the platform PRD and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  architecture.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Planned, not current: GitHub-backed repo hydration flows beyond summary-level\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  source attachment visibility and hydration-state labels.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Planned, not current: full markdown review workspace, Mermaid/package export\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  surfaces, and archive/turn/chunk browsing.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Implemented, weakly evidenced: process-type-specific durable state tables\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  exist, but current start/resume/respond behavior is still generic lifecycle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  transition logic rather than distinct registered process modules.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Suggested Read Path By Task\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Extending project entry, project shell summaries, or process registration:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  read [current-state-project-and-process-shell.md](./current-state-project-and-process-shell.md),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  then [current-state-tech-design.md](./current-state-tech-design.md), then the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  shell sections in [current-state-code-map.md](./current-state-code-map.md).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Extending active process behavior, materials, side work, or live updates:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  read [current-state-process-work-surface.md](./current-state-process-work-surface.md),\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  then [current-state-tech-design.md](./current-state-tech-design.md), then the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process-surface sections in [current-state-code-map.md](./current-state-code-map.md).\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Reconciling old specs against implemented reality:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  start here, then use [current-state-drift-ledger.md](./current-state-drift-ledger.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  before returning to the historical epic or tech design.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:15:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_6\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/onboarding/current-state-process-work-surface.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Liminal Build — Current State: Process Work Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDomain baseline reflects repository state on `main` at commit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`d85d69b5478a1435db49495a541b6ac2c1523d07`, inspected on `2026-04-15`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Evidence Scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Historical artifacts reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/02--process-work-surface/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Code areas reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/server/routes/processes.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/server/services/processes/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/client/app/bootstrap.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/client/app/process-live.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/client/features/processes/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts/process-work-surface.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts/live-process-updates.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/processes.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/processHistoryItems.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/processOutputs.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/processSideWorkItems.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Tests reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/integration/process-work-surface.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/process-work-surface-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/process-actions-api.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/process-live-updates.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/client/process-work-surface-page.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/client/process-live.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Overview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis domain covers the dedicated per-process route, its bootstrap payload, the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncurrent request/history/materials/side-work surface, and the shared process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nactions that operate inside that surface. It is the current place where the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nuser follows one active process rather than a project’s summary list.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplemented, weakly evidenced:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface and contracts support richer lifecycle states such as `waiting`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `paused`, `completed`, `failed`, and `interrupted`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current in-repo orchestration does not yet produce those states. The shipped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  mutations currently create draft processes, move them to `running`, and clear\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current requests on response handling.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Current User Outcomes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The user can open a process directly by URL or from the project shell.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface shows project identity, process display label, process type,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  status, phase label, and next-action label.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface shows current request state separately from the visible history.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The user can start a draft process and move it into active running state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Implemented, weakly evidenced: the surface supports resuming paused or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  interrupted processes when those states exist in durable data.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Implemented, weakly evidenced: the surface supports responding to a waiting\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process when a current request is present, but current in-repo orchestration\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  does not generate unresolved current requests.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface shows current materials beside the history rather than forcing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  the user to infer them from prior messages.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface shows side work as summary/result items, not as a second\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  inspectable conversation surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The surface receives typed live updates over websocket and can retry the live\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  subscription after disconnect.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Submitted user responses remain visible after reload or return.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## In Scope Now\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Dedicated process route and bootstrap API\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process HTML unavailable states for project-forbidden, project-not-found, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process-not-found outcomes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Start, resume, and respond actions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current request panel\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process-facing history feed with normalized item kinds\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current materials envelope for artifacts, outputs, and source attachments\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Side-work summary section\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Live process snapshot and upsert events over websocket\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Section-level degradation for history, materials, and side work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Durable reload and return behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Not Present / Deferred / Deprecated\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No distinct process-type module behavior is registered in the app today\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No inspectable subordinate thread or delegated-work surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No environment control, filesystem view, or tool-runtime controls in the UI\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No markdown review workspace, package export surface, or archive/chunk viewer\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No automatic reconnect loop after websocket loss; the current UX exposes a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  retry path instead\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Major Current Flows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 1. Opening And Bootstrapping A Process Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The process route is `/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The bootstrap API returns one payload with `project`, `process`, `history`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `materials`, `currentRequest`, and `sideWork`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Early processes return stable `empty` envelopes rather than null sections.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Access failures become stable unavailable states instead of partially loaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 2. Starting And Resuming Work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `start` is available only from `draft`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Starting a draft process is a current produced path in repo code.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplemented, weakly evidenced:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `resume` is available only from `paused` or `interrupted`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current start and resume behavior is generic lifecycle transition logic:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  both actions move the process to `running`, clear any current request, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  set the next-action label to `Monitor progress in the work surface`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- When a process leaves `Draft` through start, its phase label becomes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `Working`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The current repo supports resume behavior if paused/interrupted records exist,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  but no in-repo process module currently produces those states.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 3. Responding To A Waiting Process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nImplemented, weakly evidenced:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `respond` is available only when the process surface exposes that action.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Submitted responses require non-empty `clientRequestId` and `message`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Response handling is idempotent by `clientRequestId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Accepting a response finalizes the previously unresolved attention request,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  appends a finalized `user_message` history item, clears `currentRequest`, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  moves the process back to `running`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current repo code supports this flow when waiting state and unresolved current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  request data already exist, but no in-repo producer currently creates that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  data.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 4. History, Materials, And Side Work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- History items are returned in chronological order by `createdAt`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current request is projected separately from history, so unresolved user\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  action remains pinned without forcing the user to infer it from the timeline.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Materials are returned as a current-state envelope:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `currentArtifacts`, `currentOutputs`, `currentSources`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current artifacts and current sources are filtered from project-level durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  rows by process-owned current refs, not by “everything attached to this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  project.”\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Published outputs that are already represented by a visible current artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  are omitted from `currentOutputs` to avoid duplicate current-state rows.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Side work is sorted with running items first, then settled items by newest\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  update.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 5. Live Updates And Degradation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The websocket route is `/ws/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Subscribing yields an immediate `snapshot` publication split into typed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  entity messages for process, history, current request, materials, and side\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  work.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Later updates arrive as `upsert`, `complete`, or `error` messages with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  subscription id and sequence number.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Client-side reconciliation ignores stale sequence numbers and cross-process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  messages.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- If history, materials, or side work fails independently, the corresponding\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  section can become an error envelope while the rest of the process surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  stays open.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### 6. Reload, Return, And Recovery\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nConfirmed current state:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Reloading the process route re-fetches the durable bootstrap payload.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Returning later shows the latest durable history and current request state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Submitted responses remain visible after reload and return.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Manual retry for live updates performs a fresh HTTP bootstrap read before\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  attempting to re-open the websocket.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Important Current Contracts And Invariants\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Surface-supported action model on the dedicated process surface is narrower\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  than the shell summary action vocabulary:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `draft` -> `start`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `running` -> `review`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `waiting` -> `respond`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `paused` -> `resume`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `completed` -> `review`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `failed` -> `review`, `restart`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `interrupted` -> `resume`, `review`, `restart`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Implemented, weakly evidenced: not every status in that contract vocabulary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  is currently produced by in-repo orchestration, even though the surface and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  tests support them.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Current request is `null` whenever the process is not waiting on an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  unresolved attention request.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process-facing history kinds are normalized domain events:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `user_message`, `process_message`, `progress_update`, `attention_request`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `side_work_update`, `process_event`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Materials are replace-style current state. The current implementation treats\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  the materials envelope as the whole current panel state rather than patching\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  individual rows in place.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Live updates are assistive current-state transport, not the only source of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  truth. Durable bootstrap reads remain the fallback and recovery path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Extension Guidance\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- New work that changes action availability should update both the durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process summary and the process-surface action model. The shell summary and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process surface do not share exactly the same vocabulary.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- New work that changes materials should preserve the “current-state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  projection” model unless the product intentionally introduces a historical\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  materials browser.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- New work that deepens process-specific orchestration should account for the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current gap between durable per-type state tables and the still-generic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  lifecycle logic in `convex/processes.ts`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Read Next\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Read [current-state-tech-design.md](./current-state-tech-design.md) for the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current HTTP/websocket/store seams behind the process surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Read the process-surface and durable-state sections in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  [current-state-code-map.md](./current-state-code-map.md) before changing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  actions, live updates, history, materials, or side work.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Read [current-state-drift-ledger.md](./current-state-drift-ledger.md) if you\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  need to reconcile Epic 2 intent with the current generic lifecycle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  implementation.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Adjacent Domains\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [current-state-project-and-process-shell.md](./current-state-project-and-process-shell.md)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  for how the user gets into this surface and how process registration happens.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- [current-state-tech-design.md](./current-state-tech-design.md) for the shared\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  contract, `PlatformStore`, and live-update transport surfaces this domain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  depends on.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:17:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_7\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,220p' docs/onboarding/current-state-tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Liminal Build — Current Technical Baseline\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis technical baseline reflects repository state on `main` at commit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`d85d69b5478a1435db49495a541b6ac2c1523d07`, inspected on `2026-04-15`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Evidence Scope\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Historical inputs:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-arch.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `docs/spec-build/v2/epics/02--process-work-surface/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Code and config reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `package.json`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/package.json`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/server/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/client/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Validation surfaces reviewed:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - root `pnpm` scripts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/integration/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/client/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/*.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Current System Context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current runtime is a TypeScript monorepo with one real app package at\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`apps/platform`, root-level Convex functions, and Vitest-based test coverage.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCurrent runtime surfaces:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Browser client:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Vite-built TypeScript app in `apps/platform/client`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Fastify control plane:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - route, auth, shell delivery, and websocket handling in `apps/platform/server`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Shared contracts and client/server state shapes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Durable store:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Convex queries and mutations in `convex`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nCurrent external systems:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- WorkOS for hosted auth and session-cookie validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Convex for durable project/process/artifact/source/history/output/side-work state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Browser WebSocket for live process updates\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNot current state yet:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No GitHub integration code in the implemented app\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No provider-backed environment or sandbox runtime\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- No archive/chunk layer beyond visible process history rows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Top-Tier Technical Surfaces\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Owns | Depends On | Future Work Should Respect |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|------|------------|-----------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shell delivery and auth gating | HTML routes, session resolution, login/logout flow, bootstrap payload injection | WorkOS, Fastify plugins, shared bootstrap contract | Browser entry stays Fastify-owned; do not bypass shell bootstrap with direct client-side auth assumptions |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Browser route and state orchestration | Route parsing, fetch sequencing, store updates, live subscription lifecycle | Shared contracts, browser-api modules, page renderers | Route-derived state and durable refetch semantics are part of current behavior |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project shell read path | Project shell API, section readers, process registration, summary ordering and degradation | PlatformStore, shared contracts | Section envelopes and partial degradation are stable seams |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process work-surface read/action/live path | Process bootstrap API, start/resume/respond actions, live normalization and websocket hub | PlatformStore, shared contracts, in-memory live hub | Bootstrap + live reconciliation is the current pattern for active process state |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Platform store and durable state boundary | Fastify-to-Convex boundary, store abstractions, null/in-memory test doubles | ConvexHttpClient, Convex functions | Browser-facing code should continue talking to Fastify rather than directly to Convex |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared contract and state root | Zod schemas for requests, responses, routes, app state, and live messages | Client and server modules | Contract changes fan out widely; update both sides and tests together |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Current Stack And Version-Sensitive Decisions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Area | Current Choice | Evidence | Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|----------------|----------|-------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Workspace runtime | Node `>=24.14.0 <25` | `package.json` | Root repo expects current Node 24 line |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Package manager | `pnpm@10.33.0` | `package.json` | Workspace-root scripts assume pnpm |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Server framework | Fastify `5.8.4` | `apps/platform/package.json` | Fastify owns browser-facing routes, plugins, and websocket server |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Auth SDK | `@workos-inc/node` `8.12.1` | `apps/platform/package.json` | Hosted login/logout with app-owned session cookie |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Client build | Vite `8.0.8` | `apps/platform/package.json` | Fastify serves built assets in production |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Validation and typing | Zod `4.3.6`, TypeScript `6.0.2` | `apps/platform/package.json`, root `package.json` | Shared contracts and app state are schema-validated |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable state | Convex `1.35.1` | root `package.json`, `convex/schema.ts` | Used behind Fastify via `PlatformStore`, not as the public control plane |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Test runner | Vitest `4.1.4` | root `package.json` | Covers client, server, integration, and Convex slices |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| E2E harness | Playwright `1.59.1` | root `package.json`, `playwright.config.ts` | Current repo keeps the lane scaffolded but non-executable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Module / Area | Responsibility | Domain(s) Served | Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------------|----------------|------------------|------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/index.ts` | Runtime entry, env loading, Fastify listen | Whole app | Loads `.env` and `.env.local` from workspace root |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/app.ts` | Server composition root | Whole app | Wires plugins, services, routes, and store implementation |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/plugins/workos-auth.plugin.ts` | Per-request session resolution and actor attachment | Auth, all server routes | Every request gets `actor` and `authFailureReason` attached before handlers |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/routes/auth.ts` | Login, callback, me, logout routes | Auth and shell entry | HTML redirect flow plus `/auth/me` JSON lookup |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/routes/projects.ts` | Project HTML routes and project/process registration APIs | Project and process shell | Owns `/projects`, `/api/projects`, and `/api/projects/:projectId/processes` |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/routes/processes.ts` | Process HTML route, process bootstrap API, process actions, websocket route | Process work surface | Owns HTTP bootstrap plus websocket subscribe path |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/services/projects/*` | Project access, creation, shell assembly, readers, summary shaping | Project and process shell | `ProjectShellService` parallelizes section reads and degrades them independently |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/services/processes/*` | Process access, bootstrap assembly, actions, live transport | Process work surface | `DefaultProcessWorkSurfaceService` parallelizes history/materials/current request/side work |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts` | Store abstraction and Convex boundary | Both domains | Also provides `NullPlatformStore` and `InMemoryPlatformStore` for placeholder/test use |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/shared/contracts/*` | Shared route, payload, live-message, and app-state schemas | Client, server, tests | This is the highest-leverage contract root in the repo |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/client/app/*` | Browser bootstrap, routing, store, live reconciliation | Both domains | `bootstrap.ts` is the main client coordinator |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/client/browser-api/*` | Fetch wrappers and request-error parsing | Both domains | Thin boundary between UI orchestration and HTTP APIs |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/client/features/projects/*` | Project index/shell rendering and modals | Project and process shell | Mostly DOM-first render functions |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `apps/platform/client/features/processes/*` | Process work-surface rendering and controls | Process work surface | History/materials/current request/live status all render here |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `convex/*` | Durable tables and query/mutation behavior | Both domains | Process lifecycle, current refs, outputs, side work, access summaries |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tests/*` | Repo-visible behavior truth and mock boundaries | Whole app | Client JSDOM tests, server app-inject tests, integration tests, Convex tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Major Runtime / Request / Interaction Sequences\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. HTML shell bootstrap.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `apps/platform/server/routes/projects.ts` or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `apps/platform/server/routes/processes.ts` resolves access, builds a shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bootstrap payload with actor and CSRF token, and renders the Vite-backed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   document. `apps/platform/client/main.ts` then calls `bootstrapApp()`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Browser route load.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `apps/platform/client/app/bootstrap.ts` resolves `/auth/me`, parses the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   current URL into `project-index`, `project-shell`, or `process-work-surface`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   state, then loads the matching API surface.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Project shell read path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `GET /api/projects/:projectId` checks access, then `ProjectShellService`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   reads processes, artifacts, and source attachments in parallel. Each reader\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   can independently become an `error` envelope.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Process work-surface bootstrap.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `GET /api/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId` checks access, then\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `DefaultProcessWorkSurfaceService` reads history, materials, current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   request, and side work in parallel and returns one assembled payload.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. Process action path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `start`, `resume`, and `respond` APIs validate action availability, mutate\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   durable Convex state through `PlatformStore`, and publish live updates\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   through `ProcessLiveHub`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n6. Live subscription path.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   The websocket route subscribes to an in-memory stream keyed by\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   `projectId:processId`, sends an initial snapshot derived from the current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bootstrap surface, and later normalizes publications into typed messages\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   with sequence numbers.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Boundary Contracts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- HTML bootstrap payload:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `apps/platform/shared/contracts/schemas.ts` via `shellBootstrapPayloadSchema`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project shell API:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `ProjectShellResponse` with `project`, `processes`, `artifacts`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `sourceAttachments`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Process bootstrap API:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `ProcessWorkSurfaceResponse` with `project`, `process`, `history`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `materials`, `currentRequest`, `sideWork`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Request error contract:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `RequestError` and stable error codes such as `PROJECT_FORBIDDEN`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `PROCESS_NOT_FOUND`, `PROCESS_ACTION_NOT_AVAILABLE`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Live update contract:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `LiveProcessUpdateMessage` for `process`, `history`, `current_request`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `materials`, and `side_work` entity updates\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Client state root:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `AppState` and `ProcessSurfaceState` in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `apps/platform/shared/contracts/state.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Test Seams And Mock Boundaries\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Server route tests:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/server/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Use `buildApp()` with test auth services and in-memory stores, then hit\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    routes with Fastify `inject()`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Client rendering and orchestration tests:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/service/client/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Use JSDOM, mocked `fetch`, and fake websocket objects\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Integration tests:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `tests/integration/*`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Start a real Fastify server on an ephemeral port and use\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    `InMemoryPlatformStore`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Convex tests:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - `convex/*.test.ts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  - Cover durable state helpers and table-level behavior\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Repo Validation Surfaces\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Evidence | Notes |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|----------|-------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Fast lane | root `pnpm verify` | Runs format check, lint, typecheck, build, Convex tests, server tests, and client tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Deeper lane | root `pnpm verify-all` | Adds integration tests and the scaffolded e2e lane |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Build-only lane | root `pnpm red-verify` | Format, lint, typecheck, and build without tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Integration lane | root `pnpm test:integration` | Fastify app integration coverage |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| E2E lane | root `pnpm test:e2e` | Currently prints a scaffold message rather than running tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| CI config | no `.github` workflow files present | Repo-visible validation is script-defined today |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Known Technical Drift / Legacy Exceptions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The v2 platform PRD and architecture describe a broader platform than the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current codebase implements. Fastify, Convex, WorkOS, and Vite are current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  reality; provider-backed environments, GitHub hydration, archive layers, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  review/export surfaces are still planned, not current.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `ProcessModuleRegistry` exists as an intended per-process-module seam, but\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `createApp()` currently instantiates an empty registry without registering\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  concrete per-type modules. Current process lifecycle behavior is still\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  generic logic in `convex/processes.ts`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Durable per-type process state tables already exist\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  (`processProductDefinitionStates`, `processFeatureSpecificationStates`,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `processFeatureImplementationStates`), but they are used mainly for current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  material refs rather than differentiated orchestration.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Project summary counts in `convex/projects.ts` are currently derived from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  full per-project reads rather than maintained counters. That matches an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  early-stage repo shape, not the eventual scale assumptions in the platform\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  architecture.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- The shell summary action vocabulary is broader than implemented interactive\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  controls. For example, shell summaries can expose `review`, `rehydrate`, or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  `restart` labels, while current browser actions are implemented on the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  dedicated process surface for `start`, `resume`, and `respond`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Several file names, error labels, and README text still mention Story 0 or\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  Epic 1 even though the repo now includes later shell and process-surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  behavior. Treat those names as historical residue, not as the real current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  scope boundary.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:23:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_10\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '1,500p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"# Technical Design: Artifact Model and Review Provenance Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Purpose\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis document translates Epic 5 into implementable architecture for the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact-model alignment step that sits between Epic 4's review/package surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nand Epic 6's broader source-management work. It is the index document for a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfour-file tech design set:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Document | Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design.md` | Decision record, spec validation, inherited architecture, system view, module architecture overview, and work breakdown |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-server.md` | Fastify, review services, `PlatformStore`, Convex schema, migration plan, package-context model, and error taxonomy |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `tech-design-client.md` | Project-shell artifact summary changes, review workspace behavior, target-selection flows, bounded degraded states, and route/state updates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `test-plan.md` | TC-to-test mapping, fixtures, mock strategy, file inventory, chunk counts, and verification gates |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe downstream consumers are:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Audience | What they need from this design |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|----------|---------------------------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Reviewers | A clear account of what is changing in the durable model, what is staying process-owned, and where the current code still encodes the old ownership assumption |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Story authors | Story-aligned chunk boundaries that match the epic's sequencing and leave no ambiguity about which contracts, migrations, and tests belong together |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Implementers | Exact file targets, durable-state decisions, review-context rules, package-context rules, migration sequencing, and verification commands |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Spec Validation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is designable. The requirements are concrete, the ACs and TCs are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nusefully specific, and the epic asks the right questions for this phase. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmain work in validation was not repairing a broken epic; it was separating the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nepic's aligned target model from the current repo's partially pre-alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimplementation so the design could speak to both honestly.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe most important validation outcome is that the current repo is already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfarther along than the onboarding pack says. The onboarding docs in\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`docs/onboarding/` were cut on `2026-04-15` at commit `d85d69b...`, but the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking tree for this design is at `f14c88e...` on `2026-04-25`. Review and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage surfaces now exist in the code and tests. What Epic 5 has to realign\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nis not a hypothetical future review layer; it is a real, shipped-in-repo\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview/package implementation whose core artifact assumptions are still too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nclose to \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"single primary process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat current-state gap changes the design emphasis. Epic 5 is not primarily a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnet-new UI epic. It is a data-model, read-model, and policy-alignment epic\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat preserves the existing review/package surfaces while changing the rules\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose surfaces rely on. The design therefore stays tightly coupled to the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexisting Fastify control plane, `PlatformStore` boundary, shared contracts, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview route family rather than inventing new top-level product surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Issues Found\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Issue | Spec Location | Resolution | Status |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|---------------|------------|--------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current onboarding pack says the markdown review workspace and package surface are not present, but the current repo now contains `routes/review.ts`, review services, review client pages, and full review/package test suites | `docs/onboarding/current-state-process-work-surface.md`, `docs/onboarding/current-state-tech-design.md` | Treat the onboarding pack as a stale baseline for this domain. Design against current code and tests, and record the drift here so later doc refresh work has a concrete starting point | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Epic 5's error-response table does not distinguish between review-workspace bootstrap reads, which benefit from bounded degraded target states, and target-specific follow-up reads, which can legitimately return request-level 404/409 responses | Data Contracts → Error Responses; AC-5.3, AC-5.4 | Design keeps the existing review-workspace bootstrap pattern: once project/process context resolves, `GET /review` returns `200` with bounded `target.status` / `target.error` degradation. Target-specific follow-up reads continue to use request-level `404` / `409` when the caller asks for an explicit missing version, missing member, or disallowed publication member | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current `PlatformStore` interface owns high-level review-target composition and package review assembly, which bakes the pre-alignment same-process assumptions into every store implementation | Current code: `apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts`; Epic Q3, Q5 | Epic 5 narrows `PlatformStore` back toward durable-state primitives and moves review-context composition into server review services and pure helpers. This is an intentional design deviation because the old interface shape would force the wrong policy to keep leaking into every store implementation | Resolved — deviated |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package publication logic still requires every pinned member version to have `createdByProcessId === publishingProcessId`, which directly conflicts with AC-4.1 and AC-4.2 | Current code: package publication path in `platform-store.ts`; AC-4.1, AC-4.2 | Publish eligibility is rewritten around same-project membership plus current package-building context, not same-process production. Cross-process package members become valid when they are either current versions of currently referenced artifacts or explicitly pinned in the same process's current package context | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic asks how current package-building context should be represented durably, but the current repo only has immutable `packageSnapshots` and their members | Tech Design Questions Q4 | Design adds a dedicated mutable package-context model rather than overloading immutable package snapshots or broadening current-refs into a catch-all relation table | Resolved |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| The epic requires version provenance visibility via `producedByProcessId`, but raw ids alone are too thin for a user-facing provenance surface | Data Contracts → Artifact Version Summary; AC-2.2 | Design keeps `producedByProcessId` in browser-facing contracts and adds derived `producedByProcessDisplayLabel` so the UI can show readable provenance while still preserving the stable id | Resolved — clarified |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 4 established the first review/package surface, but it did so on top of a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndurable artifact model that still leaked an older assumption: one artifact row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncould still look like it \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"belonged\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" to one process. The repo has already moved\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsome of the right semantics down to the version layer. `artifactVersions` has\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`createdByProcessId`, the process work surface already projects current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmaterials from per-process current refs, and package snapshots already pin\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nexplicit version ids. Epic 5 exists because those good local decisions are not\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nyet the platform's full governing model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe problem shows up anywhere a later process touches earlier work. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureSpecification` process should be able to reference a PRD created by a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProductDefinition` process without the artifact changing owners. A\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`FeatureImplementation` process should be able to revise a tech design artifact\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfrom an earlier specification process without rewriting the artifact row to say\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"this belongs to implementation now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" A publishing process should be able to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbundle pinned versions from several processes into one reviewable package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwithout being told that only versions it personally produced are legitimate.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is why Epic 5 belongs between Epic 4 and Epic 6 rather than inside either\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nof them. Epic 4 proved there is a usable review/package surface. Epic 6 will\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmake broader claims about canonical sources, provenance, freshness, and later\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\narchive work. If Epic 6 inherited the current artifact assumptions, it would\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhave to untangle project-scoped artifact identity, version provenance, process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworking context, and package membership at the same time it is trying to settle\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsource-management behavior. Epic 5 narrows the job: fix the artifact world\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfirst so later source work inherits the right semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current repo architecture helps here. Fastify already owns the review route\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfamily, the client already owns a dedicated review workspace state slice, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe durable model already has most of the necessary nouns: project artifacts,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact versions, per-process current refs, package snapshots, and package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers. The design therefore does not need a new product surface or a new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-runtime abstraction. It needs a cleaner separation of concerns and a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsmall amount of new durable state where the current implementation is missing a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshared, process-scoped package-context model.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe alignment target is simple to say even if it touches a lot of code:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifacts are project-scoped durable identities, versions carry producing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess provenance, current process refs express what a process is working with\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnow, package contexts express which earlier versions a process has explicitly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npinned into current publishing work, and package snapshots express the durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npublished result. Review and package behavior are allowed to look at all of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthose layers, but none of those layers should collapse back into one overloaded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifact owner\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" field.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Inherited Decisions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 does not add new runtime dependencies and does not reopen the platform's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsettled architectural choices. It inherits the current stack and works within\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthe same runtime surfaces already in use.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Area | Current Choice | Source | Epic 5 Posture |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|----------------|--------|----------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Control plane | Fastify 5 monolith | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. All browser-facing behavior still routes through Fastify |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable state | Convex behind `PlatformStore` | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. Artifact and package alignment remain durable-store concerns, not browser concerns |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Client build and app shell | Vite-built client, Fastify-served shell | Platform tech arch + current repo | Keep. No separate review SPA or artifact admin app |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared contracts | Zod schemas in `apps/platform/shared/contracts` | Current repo | Keep. Epic 5 changes those schemas rather than bypassing them |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review surface shape | Existing `/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/review` route family | Current repo | Keep. Behavior realigns inside the existing route family |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Process current refs | Per-type process state tables store bounded current artifact ids | Current repo + epic assumptions | Keep as the bounded working-set model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Epic-Scoped Stack Additions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNone. Epic 5 deliberately avoids new libraries. The work is in durable-state\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nshape, contract shape, service composition, and test coverage, not in new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage adoption.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Tech Design Question Answers\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q1. Should durable process-to-artifact relationship beyond current working-set refs be modeled through a dedicated relation table or by extending the existing current-ref model?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nDo not add a generic process-to-artifact relation table in Epic 5.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current-ref arrays in the per-type process state tables are still the right\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmodel for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what this process is working with now.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" They are bounded, already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfit the process work-surface materials panel, and already line up with the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-first architecture. The problem is not that current refs are too\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsimple; the problem is that the platform has been asking current refs to carry\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconcerns they were never meant to carry.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 therefore keeps current refs for current working state and introduces a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nseparate durable package-context model for the one thing current refs cannot\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nencode cleanly: earlier pinned artifact versions that a process still needs to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncarry forward in package publication and reopen/edit flows. That new context is\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nnot a generic relation table. It is a process-scoped package-building context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwith version-aware members, ordering, and reopen semantics.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis split is intentional:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- current refs answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what artifacts are current for this process?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package-context members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"which artifact versions has this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  explicitly pinned into current package-building work?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- published package members answer \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"what exact versions did this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  publish as a durable package snapshot?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThat gives the platform three precise layers instead of one mushy relation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlayer.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q2. Which existing artifact summary fields should be removed versus preserved as derived process-local display fields?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAt the project shell level, remove the artifact-level process fields entirely.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe current `ArtifactSummary` contract still includes:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `attachmentScope`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processDisplayLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThose fields make sense only if a project artifact can still be described as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nattached to one primary process. Epic 5 removes them from the project-shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact summary contract and UI. The project shell keeps only project-scoped\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity plus latest-version projection:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe process-local display fields stay where they belong: on\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`ProcessArtifactReference` in the process materials panel. That contract already\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhas the right shape for this epic:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `currentVersionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `roleLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is the cleanest separation between project-scoped durable summary and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-owned current meaning.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q3. How should current review-target eligibility be computed so it remains process-scoped without depending on single-process artifact ownership?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nReview eligibility becomes a process-context computation, not an artifact-row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncheck.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe aligned algorithm is:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Start with the process's current artifact refs from its per-type state row.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add any artifact versions already pinned in that same process's current\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package-building context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Add any artifact versions pinned inside package snapshots published from\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   that same process, because reopening package review remains a valid process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review context.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Resolve those ids against the project's durable artifact identities and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   artifact versions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nFrom that combined process review context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- artifact targets in `availableTargets` come from artifact identities that are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  either currently referenced by the process or explicitly pinned in that same\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  process's current package-building context, provided the artifact has at least\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  one durable version and remains in this process review context\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- zero-version current refs remain excluded from the default artifact target\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  list\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- package targets in `availableTargets` come from published package snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  for that process, including snapshots whose members may all be unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  later; the snapshot remains reviewable as a durable provenance object even\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  when its selected-member state is fully degraded\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- direct artifact review succeeds when the artifact is reachable through\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  current refs or pinned package context for that same process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- unrelated project artifacts remain unavailable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis keeps review process-scoped without relying on artifact-row ownership,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nlatest-version producer, or any other single-field shortcut.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q4. How should the already-defined current package-building context be represented durably so package publish, review, and reopen flows share the same bounded member set?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nAdd a dedicated mutable package-context model:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContexts`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processPackageContextMembers`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContexts` is the header row for the current package-building\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontext owned by one process. It stores:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `processId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageType`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `basePackageSnapshotId` for reopen/edit-from-snapshot flows\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `updatedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n`processPackageContextMembers` stores the ordered, explicit version pins inside\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat current context:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `packageContextId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `position`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `artifactVersionId`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `displayName`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `versionLabel`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- `pinnedAt`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis context is mutable and process-scoped. Published `packageSnapshots` remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimmutable and durable. A publishing process can request a snapshot from any\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmember set that is valid inside its current package-building context. Reopening\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nan existing package automatically seeds the current context from the published\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsnapshot's\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers, after which the process may keep earlier pinned versions, replace some\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nmembers with the current versions of currently referenced artifacts, or republish.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis is cleaner than overloading immutable package snapshots into drafts and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncleaner than trying to make current refs carry version-specific package pins.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Q5. What is the cleanest migration path for persisted artifact rows and review tests that currently assume a primary process on the artifact row?\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nUse a staged code migration with a pragmatic data posture:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n1. Change shared contracts and read models first so project-shell and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   review/workspace code stop relying on artifact-row process ownership.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n2. Add the new package-context tables and store methods before removing the old\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   package publication rule, so current and future publishers have a durable\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   bounded context to validate against.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n3. Rewrite artifact checkpoint, review eligibility, and package publication to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   stop reading `artifacts.processId`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n4. Remove `processId` from the `artifacts` table and delete the legacy summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   fields from `ArtifactSummary`.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n5. Migrate or reset dev data as needed. Because this repo is still pre-customer,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   a dev deployment reset is acceptable if a local environment does not need\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   data preservation. The design still supports a one-off migration helper for\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   teams that want to preserve local dev rows while iterating.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nTest migration follows the same order: first update fixtures and contract tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nto the slimmer project artifact summary, then update review/package tests to\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nassert process-context eligibility instead of same-process ownership, then add\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncross-process package and degraded-state coverage on top.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## System View\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 lives inside the same four runtime surfaces that already exist for this\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npart of the product: Fastify routes and services, shared client/server\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncontracts, the browser workspace/state layer, and Convex durable state. What\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nchanges is the relationship between the concepts inside those surfaces.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Top-Tier Surfaces Touched\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Surface | Source | Epic 5 Role |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Projects | Inherited from platform tech arch + current project shell | Project-shell artifact summaries become truly project-scoped and lose artifact-level process fields |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Processes | Inherited from platform tech arch + current process work surface | Process current refs remain the bounded working-set model; review enablement is recomputed from aligned review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Artifacts | Inherited from platform tech arch + current artifact/version tables | Artifact identity becomes project-scoped only; version provenance remains version-scoped |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Review Workspace | Existing Epic 4 surface | Review target resolution shifts from artifact ownership to process review context |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Packages | Existing Epic 4 surface, expanded in this epic | Published snapshots stay immutable; current package-building context becomes durable and mutable |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Shared Contracts | Current repo | Project summaries, review contracts, error codes, and client state all realign around the new model |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### System Context Diagram\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```mermaid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nflowchart LR\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Client[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Browser Client\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Shell[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Surface[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Review[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Workspace\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Fastify[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Fastify Control Plane\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProjectSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Project Shell Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Process Work Surface Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ReviewSvc[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Review Context + Review Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageCtx[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Package Context Services\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    subgraph Convex[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Convex Durable State\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Artifacts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Versions[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifactVersions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ProcessState[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"process*States current refs\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        PackageContexts[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"processPackageContexts + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        Snapshots[\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"packageSnapshots + members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"]\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    end\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Shell --> ProjectSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Surface --> ProcessSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    Review --> ReviewSvc\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageCtx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProjectSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ProcessSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Versions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> ProcessState\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ReviewSvc --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> PackageContexts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    PackageCtx --> Snapshots\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Model Split\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe core architectural point of this epic is the model split below:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Concern | Durable Home | Meaning |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---------|--------------|---------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Project artifact identity | `artifacts` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This artifact exists in this project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Revision provenance | `artifactVersions.createdByProcessId` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This version was produced by this process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current process working set | per-type process state `currentArtifactIds` | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process is currently working with these artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Current package-building pins | `processPackageContexts` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This process has explicitly pinned these artifact versions into current publication work\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Durable published package | `packageSnapshots` + members | \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"This exact ordered set of pinned versions was published as one package\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThe rest of the design is a consequence of keeping those layers distinct.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Module Architecture Overview\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 is a four-surface change set: shared contracts, server services and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nroutes, browser read/render behavior, and Convex durable state.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```text\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/shared/contracts/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas.ts                               # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── review-workspace.ts                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── process-work-surface.ts                  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── state.ts                                 # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/server/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app.ts                                     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── routes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schemas/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review.ts                            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── services/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   ├── platform-store.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── readers/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │       └── artifact-section.reader.ts   # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   │   └── process-work-surface.service.ts  # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-workspace.service.ts      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── artifact-review.service.ts       # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── package-review.service.ts        # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       ├── review-context.service.ts        # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│       └── package-context.service.ts       # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\napps/platform/client/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── app/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   ├── bootstrap.ts                         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── store.ts                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── browser-api/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n│   └── review-workspace-api.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── features/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── projects/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── artifact-section.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    ├── processes/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    │   └── process-work-surface-page.ts     # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n    └── review/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── review-workspace-page.ts         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── artifact-review-panel.ts         # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── package-review-panel.ts          # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        ├── version-switcher.ts              # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n        └── package-member-nav.ts            # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nconvex/\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── schema.ts                                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── artifacts.ts                             # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshots.ts                      # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── packageSnapshotMembers.ts                # MODIFIED\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n├── processPackageContexts.ts                # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n└── processPackageContextMembers.ts          # NEW\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Responsibility Matrix\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Module | Status | Responsibility | ACs Covered |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|--------|----------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `schemas.ts` | MODIFIED | Slim project artifact summary; add request-error codes for aligned review/package failures | AC-1, AC-2, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-workspace.ts` | MODIFIED | Expand review-target error taxonomy and selected-target semantics for zero-version, unavailable-version, and unavailable-member cases | AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `app.ts` + `server/schemas/review.ts` | MODIFIED | Wire new review/package-context services into the running app and tighten route-level request/response contracts for exact review/package error codes | AC-3, AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `platform-store.ts` | MODIFIED | Expose aligned durable-state primitives; remove review composition from the store boundary; add package-context reads/writes | AC-1 through AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `review-context.service.ts` | NEW | Compute process-scoped review context from current refs, package context, and package snapshots | AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-context.service.ts` | NEW | Maintain the current mutable package-building context and validate publish eligibility against it | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve artifact review through process context rather than artifact-row ownership | AC-2, AC-3, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `package-review.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Resolve mixed-producer package members and degrade per member rather than per package | AC-4, AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `artifact-section.ts` | MODIFIED | Render project artifact summaries without ownership language | AC-1, AC-2 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `process-work-surface.service.ts` | MODIFIED | Gate review enablement on aligned review context instead of same-process production heuristics | AC-3 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `bootstrap.ts` + `review-workspace-api.ts` | MODIFIED | Handle follow-up 404/409 responses by reloading bounded workspace state rather than collapsing the whole workspace | AC-5 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `processPackageContexts.ts` + members | NEW | Durable current package-building context for publish/reopen flows | AC-4 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Work Breakdown\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 maps cleanly to the story breakdown already proposed in the epic. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwork should still be implemented vertically, but the design keeps one clear\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfoundation chunk up front so later chunks do not have to keep rediscovering the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nsame contract and migration decisions.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk Summary\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk | Scope | ACs | Planned Tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|-------|-------|-----|---------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 0 | Foundations: contract changes, store narrowing, fixture refresh, migration helpers, package-context tables | — | 12 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 1 | Project artifact association without process ownership | AC-1.1 to AC-1.3 | 18 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 2 | Versioned checkpoint realignment and latest-version summary reads | AC-2.1 to AC-2.3 | 22 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 3 | Process-scoped artifact review realignment, including zero-version behavior | AC-3.1 to AC-3.4 | 27 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 4 | Cross-process package alignment and current package-building context | AC-4.1 to AC-4.4 | 23 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Chunk 5 | Reopen, unavailable, and degraded provenance states plus classification | AC-5.1 to AC-5.4 | 19 |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n**Planned total:** 121 tests\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 0: Foundations\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk exists to make the rest of the epic legible. The wrong way to do\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 would be to mix schema removal, package-context introduction, review\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npolicy changes, and UI copy adjustments into the first feature slice that\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhappens to need them. Chunk 0 instead lands the shared contracts, the store\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboundary changes, the new package-context tables, and the migration scaffolding\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nthat later chunks rely on.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nKey deliverables:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- slimmer `ArtifactSummary` contract\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- aligned review error codes and request codes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- new package-context tables and store methods\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- review-context helper/service skeleton\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- fixture and contract-test refresh\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 1: Project Artifact Association Without Process Ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk removes ownership language from project-shell artifact state and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nensures a process can reference an existing project artifact without changing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity. The project shell and the process work surface both change\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nhere, but only at the read-model level: the durable current-ref model remains\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbounded and process-owned, while project artifact identity becomes cleanly\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nproject-owned.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 2: Versioned Checkpoint Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk rewrites the durable write path. Checkpointing a later revision of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nan existing project artifact must create a new version while leaving the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact identity untouched. The chunk also settles the project-shell and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nprocess-materials read-path rule for \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"current version label\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"updated at\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nboth are derived from the latest artifact version, not from artifact-row\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nownership metadata.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 3: Process-Scoped Artifact Review Realignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk is where the user first feels the alignment. A process can review an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:25:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_11\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"sed -n '501,650p' docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design.md\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"artifact it currently references even if another process created or last revised\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nit. Zero-version artifacts stay out of the default target list, but direct\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nreview paths become consistent and bounded. This chunk also removes the last\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nartifact-row ownership check from artifact review resolution.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 4: Cross-Process Package Alignment\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk introduces the durable current package-building context and relaxes\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\npackage publish rules to the ones the epic actually wants: same project, valid\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ncurrent process context, and explicit version pinning. Cross-process members\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbecome normal as long as they are in context. Package snapshots remain\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nimmutable; the mutable context exists so the process can reopen, edit, and\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nrepublish without losing bounded eligibility.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n### Chunk 5: Reopen and Degraded Provenance States\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThis chunk is mostly about resilience and classification. It ensures the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nworkspace and package views degrade accurately when a reference disappears, a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nspecific version is missing, or one package member is unavailable later while\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nother members remain healthy. It also tightens observability so downstream\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndebugging can tell the difference between target lookup failures, version\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nfailures, and member failures.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Verification Scripts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nEpic 5 uses the repo's existing verification tiers without adding a new lane.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nNo new dependency package means no new package-build lane is required beyond\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nwhat the repo already has.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Script | Command | Purpose |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|--------|---------|---------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `red-verify` | `corepack pnpm run red-verify` | Format, lint, typecheck, and build without tests |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify` | `corepack pnpm run verify` | Standard dev gate |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `green-verify` | `corepack pnpm run green-verify` | Standard gate plus test-immutability guard |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| `verify-all` | `corepack pnpm run verify-all` | Deep gate including integration and scaffolded e2e |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Open Questions\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nThese do not block drafting, but they should remain visible while stories are\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\nbeing prepared:\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| # | Question | Owner | Blocks | Resolution |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|---|----------|-------|--------|------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Q1 | Future extension only: after Epic 5 ships with one current mutable package-building context per process, should a later epic support more than one draft context per process? | Platform TL | Later process-module enrichment only; does not block Epic 5 implementation | Pending |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Deferred Items\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Item | Related AC | Reason Deferred | Future Work |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n|------|-----------|-----------------|-------------|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Multi-draft package editing for one process | AC-4 | Epic 5 only needs one current package-building context per process to support bounded eligibility and reopen flows | Later process-module/package-authoring epic |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Cross-project artifact reuse | Out of scope | Epic 5 explicitly keeps artifacts scoped to one project | Later artifact-sharing epic, if ever needed |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Historical process-to-artifact relation browser | AC-1, AC-5 | Epic 5 keeps bounded current refs and package contexts, not a full historical relation product surface | Later archive/provenance UI work |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n| Full onboarding-pack refresh for review/package current state | — | This design records the drift but does not own the documentation refresh project | Docs refresh after Epic 5 lands |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n## Related Documentation\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Epic: `epic.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Companion docs: `tech-design-server.md`, `tech-design-client.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Test plan: `test-plan.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Platform architecture: `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-arch.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n- Platform PRD: `docs/spec-build/v2/core-platform-prd.md`\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"exit_code\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":0,\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}}\\\\\\\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/artifacts/01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership/streams/006-verify.stdout.log:37:{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item.completed\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"item_17\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command_execution\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"command\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"TC-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.|AC-1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.|project artifact|process ownership|currentArtifactIds|attachmentScope|processDisplayLabel|ArtifactSummary|ProcessArtifactReference|artifact summary|process-local visibility|ownership language\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" apps/platform server convex tests docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment -g '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'!docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/tech-design*'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"' -g '\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'!docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/test-plan.md'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"aggregated_output\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"rg: server: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/team-impl-log.md:40:- 01-project-artifact-association-without-process-ownership (Story 1: AC-1.1..1.3, TC-1.1a..1.3b)\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/team-impl-log.md:107:- Artifacts become project-scoped durable assets; no single-process ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-router.test.ts:119:  it('TC-1.1a direct review URL mounts the review workspace and fetches the bootstrap API', async () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-router.test.ts:137:  it('TC-1.1e zero-target review URL renders the empty review state', async () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts:86:  it('TC-1.2a keeps project, process, and artifact target context visible together', () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts:102:  it('TC-1.2b renders target selection state without stale target content', () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts:148:  it('TC-1.3b identifies package review targets on first render', () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts:178:  it('TC-1.4a back-to-process returns to the same process context', () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ntests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts:196:  it('TC-1.1e renders the empty target state when no reviewable targets exist', () => {\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:31:process can reference an existing project artifact, create a new version of an\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:49:- Allow processes to reference existing project artifacts without taking\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:51:- Allow later processes to create new versions of existing project artifacts\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:58:- Keep project artifact state deduplicated at the project level even when\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:74:- Cross-project artifact sharing\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:101:| Review/package eligibility model | Package publication eligibility is bounded by current process reference and pinned package context rather than by the full project artifact set | Keeps Epic 5's package rules aligned with the architecture and downstream source-management assumptions |\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:109:The user needs a process to work with project artifacts that may already have\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:116:2. User creates a new artifact or brings an existing project artifact into the\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:125:**AC-1.1:** A process can reference an existing artifact in the same project\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:128:- **TC-1.1a: Existing project artifact added to process**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:133:- **TC-1.1b: Later process reference does not erase earlier process lineage**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:138:**AC-1.2:** A new artifact created during process work is immediately part of\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:141:- **TC-1.2a: New process artifact appears in project artifact state**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:146:- **TC-1.2b: Project artifact state remains deduplicated**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:148:  - When: The project artifact state is read\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:149:  - Then: That artifact appears as one durable project artifact, not one row per\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:152:**AC-1.3:** Process-local artifact visibility reflects current process\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:155:- **TC-1.3a: Current process materials show referenced artifact**\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:159:- **TC-1.3b: Unrelated process does not gain automatic current-material\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:177:5. System updates project artifact summaries and process current-material reads\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:182:**AC-2.1:** When a process revises an existing project artifact, the system\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:356:- **TC-4.2b: Unrelated project artifact version not in current package-building\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:398:version may become unavailable while other project artifacts remain healthy. The\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:443:   availability accurately rather than implying a primary-process ownership\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:604:2. Which existing artifact summary fields should be removed versus preserved as\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:627:**Delivers:** Processes can reference existing project artifacts and new\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/epic.md:628:process-created artifacts automatically join the project artifact set without\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ndocs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--ar